How travel prices have evolved since 1989
Until 1989 there were only five authorized state travel agencies in Czechoslovakia: Čedok, Sporturist, CKM, Rekrea and Autoturist.
Travel agencies at the time in most cases sold trips only to the socialist bloc, as travel to the West was very restricted.
Czech tourists went, therefore, on holiday to the beaches of the GDR (Democratic Republic of Germany), to the Soviet Union, (Sochi in Crimea in the Black Sea) to Bulgaria and Romania (both in the Black Sea) and to Hungary (Balaton).
A positive exception was the period between 1962 and 1972, when you could travel to Yugoslavia, however, then the Yugoslavian regime leaned west and the travel of Czech tourists were then quite restricted.
A great paradox of socialism was the fact that the citizens of the socialist bloc (i.e. the Czechoslovaks) were not allowed to travel freely and individually even through the socialist countries.
If a citizen wanted to travel individually, e.g. to Poland or to the Soviet Union, he needed an invitation letter, if not, individual travel was not possible.
Travel agencies also offered trips to the West, but one had to queue with a sleeping sack and spend the whole night, or have "well-known people" at the agency.
Still not everything was won.
The so-called "promise of change" that was the condition for travel to the west, was not granted to the "politically unreliable people", who thus could not travel at all.
The Czechoslovak currency was not a freely convertible currency and, with no guarantee of exchange, it was not possible to obtain the resources (the Western currency) to travel to the West.
The vast majority of the Czechs spent their holidays in their own country, so there were a large number of cottages and summer houses, which the Czechs acquired. This custom still persists.
The manner of travel of the Czechs has undergone a real revolution since 1989.
While until 1989 for us it was no longer going to spend the holidays in the GDR or Bulgaria, last year the Czechs made 4.5 million holiday trips to countries around the world.
It is a great paradox, of which today we are no longer aware, the fact that the current favorite destination, Croatia, until 1989 could not travel freely, because it was part of the Yugoslavia at that time.
In the early 1990’s the visa waiver was imposed to travel to the neighbouring states of Western Europe, which provoked a huge wave of short-term visits, especially to Vienna or Germany.
The Czechoslovaks, for a long time, didn’t look fed up with excursions to the West, everyone wanted to see it at least for a moment.
For example, in Paris the groups of Czechs slept in the parks in tents, since our income from then did not allow us to pay a hotel or pension.
A typical characteristic of the Czechs of that time was to take with them the food (preserves, sausages, etc.) to save.
Another important moment was the introduction of the convertibility of the Czech kroner, so our tourists could finally, after decades, freely buy Western currency.
Our standard of living increased and with it also average wages, which around 1997 caused an increase in demand for travel.
With the increase in demand for travel the agencies began, from 1997 regularly, to prepare travel using charter connections, which significantly lowered travel prices compared to the past, thanks to the possibility of obtaining discounts for passenger volume.
By the late 1990’s of the last century, the Czechs were already traveling without a visa to almost all developed countries except the U.S., which further simplified our travels.
Another interesting moment in post-revolutionary tourism’s history happened as early as 2000, when it was organized, thanks to a huge demand, the first direct flight from the Czech Republic to an exotic country, and went to Thailand.
Since that year, direct charter flights with Czech tourists departed every year for distant and hot countries. This winter you will already fly directly from Chequia to 12 exotic destinations.
Exotic countries currently visit about 100 thousand Czechs a year.
Another interesting moment of this era, which symbolizes our post-revolutionary development, arrived in 2008.
On November 17, 2008, the U.S. eliminated visas for Czech tourists, with what our country became among the most developed countries in the world.
In 2008 the Czechs, for the first time in its history, have spent more than half of their holidays abroad (50.3%) and for the first time in their history, more than 15 thousand Czech kroners per head have been spent abroad, including expenses on the stay site.
Convened Ordinary General Assembly to elect the president, who could end up again at a standstill.
For almost five months the Czech football has been without president and it is likely that Ivan Hašek’s successor as President of the Czech Football Federation (FAČR), will not be known even after the convening of the next Extraordinary General Assembly that will take place this Thursday in Nymburk.
In fact, no agreement has yet been reached on the voting rules to follow.
Without them, you won’t even be able to vote to choose between the three candidates.
Those interested in presiding over Czech football are Jindřich Rajchl, Miroslav Pelta and Tomáš Paclík.
Hašek resigned from office in June not before convening his colleagues with the purpose of finding a strong person who knew how to deal with football in the future.
However, after concluding the Hašek era, a controversy broke out between the two federations of the association unable to reach an agreement to choose a common candidate.
This was evidenced on September 16 when the new president had to be elected.
Delegates of the General Assembly met in vain in the National House (Národní dům), located in Smíchov’s Praguense neighbourhood, because the talks had been blocked and not only in terms of the method of choice.
No consensus was reached or as to whom in the General Assembly had the right to vote - only legal representatives or also delegates with full powers.
From that moment and due to this single point, there was a partial agreement, also supported by the recommendation of the Institute of State and Law, according to which representation by full powers is not possible.
However, disputes over the electoral code remain open.
More light on the situation could no longer shed even the Executive Committee which, in its meetings, ordinary or extraordinary, basically did not resolve anything.
The opinion on the possibility of voting has changed a couple of times already, but the nerve point remains the same.
The question is this: how to proceed in the third round to avoid the deadlock?
"The dispute revolves around whether to proceed in accordance with the statutes or find another model," says Dušan Svoboda, one of the FAČR's three vice-presidents, who, from the leadership of the association, represents professional football.
According to the model proposed in the statutes, in the third round will win the candidate who has the highest percentage of support in the federation to which he will present his candidacy.
The Region of Moravia is ready to reach a commitment so that the candidate can win with a higher total percentage of votes in both federations.
The bohemian part, however, imposes the condition that the winner, according to these percentage calculations, must have the majority of at least two-thirds.
"This would, however, take away the meaning of the previous two rounds and completely deny the concept of bicameral system", this is how Svoboda understands the objections of the Moravian side, according to which it would be best not to touch the order and vote according to the applicable statutes.
With what has been said, surprisingly, Tomaš Paclík also agrees, although it is assumed that the electoral regulations established by the statutes of all the candidates are more suitable for Pelta.
"I would like you to vote to the maximum according to the statutes."
“So you don’t invent any nonsense about the representatives as in the last General Assembly,” says the owner of Victoria Plzen, who is first running as a candidate and who directs his remarks mainly against Pelta.
Pelta remains optimistic.
According to him, in Nymburk he will decide on the new president and he believes that the candidates in the future will cooperate constructively.
“All passions will be appeased with today’s match of our national team in the European Championships.
"Football will once again be the protagonist, and this will cooperate more at ease," says the representative of Jablonice, who of the three candidates have more experience in football management, both professionally and regionally.
Rajchl is the most reticent.
"I hope an agreement will be reached, but, unfortunately, the situation is much more complicated."
“It’s not about the conflict between Bohemians and Moravians, but there are attempts to adjust the rules to their own convenience.”
"This may result in a new blockade of the General Assembly."
"Anyway, I hope common sense will prevail," Rajchl said.
There's no longer for all parties to reach an agreement.
Some negotiations had to take place in Montenegro, but who can know until the game begins if the football team will be complete?
The special plane, in which they had to travel to Podgorica, among others Vice-Presidents Dalibor Kucera and Rajchl, did not leave this morning due to technical problems in Prague, having to look for an alternative to transport part of the executive committee to the place where the qualifying match will take place.
Perhaps the decisive meeting, at last, it could be the one that will take place at the ministerial level, which, according to them, will be held on Wednesday.
Josef Dobeš, Minister of Education, Youth and Sports, would very much like to incorporate the divided parties to the most powerful sports organization in the country to reach an agreement.
There is much at stake: state subsidies, the credibility of football and also the next General Assembly of the Central Committee of the Czechoslovak Sports Federation (ČSTV) that will try to provide a new direction to the Czech sport after the financial problems of the Sazka lottery house.
Paclík will fight for the presidency, because he doesn’t find a more suitable candidate.
If Pilsen’s boss of the footballers, Tomaš Paclik, before the Extraordinary General Assembly knew the name of an appropriate candidate who satisfied all his ideas about the new president of the Czech Football Federation (FAČR), nor would he run for such a relevant position.
"If there was a proper person, I'd be fighting for her and I wouldn't run as an election candidate."
"But to this day you haven't been able to find such a person," Paclik told the Czech News Agency (ČTK)
What motivates Paclík to enter the challenge with Miroslav Pelta and Henry Rajchl is exactly the possibility of blocking Pelta, the alleged winner of the elections, who opens the way to the presidency of the Czech Football Federation (FAČR).
Paclík also points out that, in the event of winning, he would remain in the office of president only until 2013, when it would be the turn of the ordinary general assembly.
In that position he would like to see an expert and good manager who is not too closely related to football events.
"I've already talked to some people I could keep in mind."
"For me it was crazy to hear some to say that at this time they don't even want to identify with Czech football."
"I'd like to change that vision," he adds.
What supposedly bothers Paclík right now is Pelta’s relationship with the President of the Pilsen Roman Berbr Regional Football Association, a man with a controversial past, known outside the world as a powerful lobbyist capable of influencing delegate voting in the general assembly.
For Paclík it is so unacceptable to see Pelta sitting in the presidential chair.
"In case he wins, he would maintain current status and nothing in football will change."
"With the support of Berbra, an ambitious businessman, football will get even less profitable," Paclik said.
It also mentions the fact that a few years ago Pelta's name was associated with a wiretapping during the investigation of a corruption case, from which Pelta went unpunished.
"I don't know what prestige football could bring Pelta's victory."
"And with what eyes UEFA and FIFA will look at us," said Paclík, who also takes with some precaution his previous statement that "I would sell Club Pilsen if Pelta becomes president."
In the case of winning Thursday’s election, Paclik could consider cooperating with Pelta in the future.
Of the same opinion is the other candidate, Rajchl, who has been supported by Paclík before the September General Assembly that ended in a fiasco.
“He (Pelta) would be the first he would follow.”
"He's very capable."
"I could imagine, for example, as head of representation."
"but not as an association manager."
"I don't put my noses where I don't feel capable either," added Paclík, who in September raised a lot of attention for supporting the suspension of the Arbitrators Commission chaired by Ludek Macel, later replaced by Dagmar Damkova.
Paclík admits that he would dare to manage the partnership.
He resorts to his management capabilities of private companies, because since last summer he is also the Director of Pilsen, who with his support celebrated in May the victory in the first division.
"I have the strength to change a few things in the field of football and contribute to their safe practice."
"To reduce the influence of some pressure groups, which probably tend to influence the decisions of the disciplinary and appellate committee, producing unpleasant situations, Paclík said without hiding his antipathies towards Sparta President Daniel Křetínský.
One of the priorities would also be to consolidate the relationship between the FAČR and the Czech Olympic Committee, with the help of which it exerted pressure on political leaders to allocate more money to the sport of lottery taxes.
"It's one of the key issues that the new president should dedicate himself to."
"If taxes on gambling increased, as announced by political parties, I would support their arguments, but frankly, they have to tell us where all this money is going to stop," he says.
In addition, Paclík now does not even take into account his association with the Pilsner Club, although he himself claims that the President of the FAČR must be "non-partisan".
Pelta is in turn associated with Jablonec and Rajchl with Dukla of Prague.
"It's not ideal."
"But I don't see the fact of hypocritically ceding my Pilsen Club actions to someone else and then come back there after a year and a half," Paclik said.
Pelta promised the FAČR new statutes that would end the deadlock of the elections.
Two days before the General Assembly of the Football Association one of the candidates for the presidency, Miroslav Pelt, promised that, if elected, he would guarantee that the FAČR would have new statutes that would serve definitively to settle, in the future, controversies regarding the electoral regulations.
The Jablonec representative is optimistic about Thursday’s meeting in Nymburk, and believes that the pre-election conflict could move towards a solution today with the possible victory of the football team.
Already prior to non-September elections, Pelta promised to focus, if elected, precisely on representation and raising funds.
He decided to participate at the last minute in the last general assembly, so this postponement of two months could go well to defend and present, in a broader context, his ideas on the management of football.
"The people of all levels take care of football for a long time and hope to meet the new President this Thursday."
"They feel that it's time for someone to manage the association," thinks the man who associates mainly with the Jablonice Club, but who worked at Sparta and the Association's executive committee.
For this reason, he thinks he has enough experience to manage Czech football.
He is convinced that behind the current problems with the elections is the euphoria that reigned in the time of Ivan Hašek.
"Unfortunately, in the June General Assembly the statutes were approved that were not as perfect as everyone thought."
"If we exclude certain interpretations, we have a president already since September," Pelta warned.
Exactly the readaptation of the most important document of the Civic Association was used as a decoy to beat delegates.
"If I'm elected president, I'll take it as a personal challenge and I'm willing to respond if this fails."
"It's a matter of principle, without which we can't exist," Pelta says.
Moreover, in theory, he tries not to mess with his opponents.
Already in the past he had warned that he was collaborating with Henry Rajchl, whom he respects for his training in Law, being aware that his opponent would also be his first vice president.
He hadn't attacked Thomas Paclík for a long time who, unlike Pelta, didn't left puppets with his head.
"He's been in the world of football for more than a year, and he's already achieved some successes."
"Their attacks but they were embarrassing and a lot of people were shocked by the election campaign."
"That's why many of them doubt their true character," Pelta, who remains faithful to the statements already announced in September.
His most powerful weapon is, according to him, his knowledge of football, from the lowest to the upper competitions.
The latter should be a showcase for the promotion of football.
"It would also try to solve all the scandals that arose because of the Bohemians as a result of last year's corruption scandal."
"Immediately afterwards I would get involved in negotiations with the public and private sectors, to ensure that there is enough money for all football," the 46-year-old candidate told the ČTK two months ago.
He then also promised that his appointment as president of football would not entail any personal modification of the squad.
"I would have to first know the tasks of each employee," he said, referring to Strahov where, after a long period of anarchy, an understandable nervousness prevails.
Despite the current catastrophic scenarios that are predicted before the next general assembly, he believes delegates won’t travel in vain Thursday to Nymburk.
"If the only point of disagreement will be how to vote in the third round, we will certainly overcome it.
"Football needs credibility, which will recover only if it finally has a leader", still two days before the most decisive day in his representative career, Pelta remains optimistic.
Rajchl promises that under his leadership, the FAČR would start working eagerly.
If current Vice President Jindrich Rajchl as the new president of the Czech Football Association is elected in the fourth General Assembly, many people would surely be resented.
Rachchl promises that, should he be chosen, hard work would be done.
He wants to implement a systematic change and make the members of the executive committee responsible for each area of football individually.
It is also committed to solving the case of the Bohemians, improving relations with UEFA and FIFA, getting more money for football and continuing the work initiated by former President Ivan Hašek.
"As president, I'd like to dedicate myself entirely to football."
"From international contacts with UEFA and FIFA to the national team, to professional football or the training of high-performing young footballers."
“With this I don’t want to say I’d do it all alone,” Rajchl said in an interview with ČTK.
"I'm a team player and I want to divide the executive committee's competencies in such a way that each member can dedicate themselves to a specific area."
"And I will assign difficult tasks so that football can, in each of those segments, move forward with a lot of momentum."
"A lot of people this probably won't like it, because it'll mean starting to work very hard and not just criticizing each other's work."
Due to the atmosphere of criticism in the football movement, he is said to have considered the possibility of not running again as a candidate.
"Everybody knows, I work 20 hours a day."
"But I need to know that this will make sense and that there will be no people behind me who will prevent and hinder me at every turn," he explained the reasons for his indecision.
"A lot of people have told me that I should not give up, because then others would also give up."
"It's about having faith so you don't quit like that, just," he said.
According to them, it also motivates him to continue the work that already began with Hašek.
“During those two years, a lot of the work that I don’t want to be lost now ended.”
"There are processes that had begun and that will have their effect within a couple of years and I wouldn't want anyone to stop them," he announced concerned that, if they elected the other candidate, Miroslav Pelta, the situation in the association could again be as it was before Hašek's time.
"I believe in order, and it's something Mirek Pelta isn't able to maintain.
"Behind him is above all all those people who would take care of football so that it would be only a privilege for a few chosen."
"I can be blamed for a lot of things, but not the fact that I'm in someone's favor."
"I'm trying to be objective."
"I want to do a football for everyone, because just that's how everyone will feel good and not just the chosen ones," Rajchl said.
It also rejects the idea that would only be bohemian the president.
"Although some people try to state otherwise, I have in mind to do my best for Moravia."
"For example, it's time to think about dividing the second division into one Czech side and another Moravian, whose winners would go to the first division."
"There would not be the current situation in which we have only three Moravian teams, which represents a problem for young Moravian players," Rajchl said.
Althouge to the struggle of the presidential election was added Tomaš Paclík, president of the Pilsner Club, Rajchl does not stop believing in his chances of being elected.
"I still feel to have strong support from the Czech Federation, who elected me as their vice-president and who knows that behind me there are clear successes," said Rajchl, whose successes comprise saving some tens of millions of disadvantageous contract crowns, television contract negotiations and negotiations on the lottery law.
On the other hand, he recognizes his share of guilt that the federation is not united.
And while proclaiming that Pelta is "disqualified to regain confidence in Czech football", he considers the other rival, Paclík, as a strong candidate who does have something to offer football.
"No one can deny that he did a great job for the Pilsen that year."
"In the event that he is elected, he will probably follow what Ivan Hašek has already started and would not significantly change the workforce on the committees."
"I don't want to use that in my favor," Rajchl said.
The most beautiful swimming pools in Europe
Jumping blue
by Julia Stanek
Pools from an incredible perspective, sumptuous baths in Budapest: Those who visit Europe can live their blue wonder, and, after a visit to the city or a walk, rest their tired legs in bubbling baths.
A book shows where the best well-being is in water.
When Iris Meder made an investigative trip over the past year and a half, there was something that couldn't miss in his luggage, a swimsuit.
Tumbled with her back on the water, she looked at the artistic dome on top, swimmed towards the marble anglets, who waited for her on the edge of the pool and astonished in front of the meticulously restored modernist indoor pool.
He liked the bathroom, took note in his notebook at the time of all the interesting details about the style and history of the building.
The architectural historian Meder explored more than 200 swimming pools in this way and presents them in her recent book "Badefreuden": the indoor pools in Munich, the historic bathroom palaces in the Black Forest, the "concrete constructions" in Taunus.
He made his "journey for the most extraordinary baths of Central Europe" for 13 countries: In addition to Germany, Austria and Switzerland, he visited places in Italy, France, the Czech Republic, Slovakia, Slovenia, Hungary, Romania, Poland, and a city respectively in Luxembourg, Serbia and Croatia.
This 46-year-old German is a bathing culture enthusiast.
He doesn’t want to have anything to do with the sport, Meder says, only water is different.
He’s just bothered that bathroom enthusiasts have to settle for blue tiles and stench of chlorine, while running enthusiasts can breathe the fascinating smell of fir forests or listen to the cheerful trills of birds in the park.
"One day I wondered why swimming pools always have to be so ugly," she said.
"I started collecting nice swimming pools."
The surface of the water duplicates the architecture.
The splash in the swimming pool and the ethereal oils of the sauna. The well-being factor in the pools is so great because all the senses are served, according to Meder.
And if the builder strives, indoor pools can be a real gift to the view: “Instead of ground, in the bathrooms you have a water surface.”
"On the one hand it is transparent, on the other hand it reflects the beauty of the whole building."
Everything folds optically: high vaults, colored tiles, trampolines.
The book with photographs in small format "Badefreuden" provides in 190 pages very interesting facts about the architecture and history of art in the historical oases of well-being, but, mainly, it makes you want to jump to blue.
Meder, the pool collector discovers the specimens highlighted in her research in Hungary.
Next to the magnificent medicinal baths such as the 100-year-old Széchenyi bath in Budapest are the waters with nitrates in a unique karstic cavity (baths in cavities in Miskolc-Tapolca), as well as hot springs, whose swimming pools are made of alkaline waters and are located in a bottle-shaped building (Városi Termálfürdö in Jászberény).
Many uncovered pools in Switzerland and Austria offer spectacular views: While the entire city sets its sights on Zurich’s indoor pool, you can look at snow-covered Arlberb, from the outdoor swimming pool in St. Anton, and: In the Turkish bath there’s a window from which you can see the practice on ski slopes.
Perhaps more extraordinary design awaits customers in Längenfeld: Ötztal Water Cathedral looks like a UFO that just landed in the Alps.
From three open fountains it rises steam in winter, while in the background the mountains of Ötztal rise in the sky.
Alhambra in the Black Forest
Meder, the collector of swimming pools, did not invade the smell of chlorine in all pools.
Many thermal baths smell of sulphur, in Árpád’s baths in Békescsaba, Hungary, the diesel plague is so strong that people think they have reached a sanitary bath.
The smell comes from the medicinal waters that contain bicarbonate and alkali and whose legamus sticks to the skin when entering the pool, an enjoyment of well-being in a special way.
Iris Meder was also disappointed in a place in his search for beautiful places for bathing, when he wanted to visit outdoor pools and sunbathes in the Czech bath of Luhatschowitz, he found a blocked land.
Architect Duan Jurkovic’s 1902 bathroom was closed.
When Meder looked through the crumbling windows of the red, white and yellow modernist building, he saw the weeds grow between the tiles.
Decadence countered in a charming pool in the Black Forest: the Thermal Palace in Bad Wildbald.
These bathrooms have three phases of construction after itself; you can not only uncover neo-Romanic elements of their construction times in 1844, but also an Arab Alhambra-style hall, added in 1900.
Since its careful modernization in 1995, the Thermal Palace is the favorite bathroom of the collector of pools, Iris Meder, and not only because it is natural of the Black Forest.
"It's got a lot of merit to merge different architectural styles into such a fantastic unit," Meder says.
In such a palace the immersion in the thermal pool apparently does not have much importance.
Agrarian Speculation
Deutsche Bank analyses raw materials business
By Christian Teevs
Is Deutsche Bank giving up its business with agricultural raw materials?
According to SPIEGEL-ONLIN information, Josef Ackermann has commissioned a working group to verify the consequences of speculation for the world’s poor.
In January, the head of the consortium wants to show the consequences.
Hamburg,
For citizens, the issue is clear, according to one study, 84 percent of Germans don’t find it acceptable for banks to speculate with agricultural raw materials like wheat or corn.
Two-thirds of those surveyed demand that Deutsche Bank and other credit institutions withdraw from this business because it intensifies the problems of the world’s poorest.
This is the result of a survey commissioned by consumer organization Foodwatch.
The study is based on a report submitted to Foodwatch in mid-October.
Author Harald Schumann shows that betting on global commodity exchanges raises prices, and banks bear responsibility for the global problem of hunger.
The consumer defender’s campaign specifically targets Josef Ackermann, director of Deutsche Bank, although big banks like Goldman Sachs or Morgan Stanley act similarly.
Originally, wagering on raw materials was intended to protect traders from price swings.
Leading experts agree that players have decoupled from offers and demands, and profit from the costs of the poorest.
Ackermann reacted to criticism differently than U.S. competitors: He vowed to review the claims and said no business “deserves to compromise Deutsche Bank’s good reputation.”
According to SPIEGEL ONLINE information, an international working group is reviewing the demands of the Foodwatch report.
The analysis should be completed by the end of the year, then the results will be submitted to the Deutsche Bank directive, along with the recommendation of how to handle the matter.
At the end of January, the bank’s address will report the consequences.
In the end there could be a reduction of the raw materials business, or even its total abandonment.
Ackermann's trying to fix his image.
Thilo Bode, founder of Foodwatch, celebrated the news but reaffirmed his criticism of the financial sector: "Bets at rising food prices show especially drastically how ruthless banks currently harm public welfare."
Ackermann ranks as the largest banking lobbier, especially in obligations.
"The Deutsche Bank must nullify all investments in its portfolio in which it lays down money for food price bets," Bode said.
"It doesn't matter if Ackermann deals with the issue of people going hungry or Deutsche Bank's fame."
Ackermann’s inspection is surprising at first glance, but it’s clear that the consortium’s boss wants to offer a perfect image a few months before leaving office.
On Monday, Deutsche Bank released that the chairman of the board of directors will change something other than unplanned on the board of directors.
With the abandonment of the controversial agrarian speculation it can perfect its image.
And he needs to barely care about possible losses, that's what his successor will deal with.
Two-thirds would cancel the investment in commodities
Foodwatch strives to keep pressure on the manager high.
So far, more than 30,000 Internet users have participated in the organization’s campaign.
On Tuesday, Bode released the survey for which the Forsa Institute had asked 1,001 people on Nov. 7 and 8.
Only 10 percent considered it legitimate for Deutsche Bank to offer investments in which food prices were wagered.
Many clients of the bank showed personal consequences when they discovered that their bank participated in such businesses: according to the survey, two-thirds would cancel all the investments from which money emerges from agrarian speculation.
43 percent would discourage such a bank from their acquaintances, 49 percent would ask, after reflection, to cancel their account and would be exchanged to another institute.
House 21 in Vienna
The transferred pavilion
By Ingeborg Wiensowski
A history of architecture with ups and downs and a happy ending, the pavilion of the 1958 Austrian universal exhibition in Brussels won awards, was in Vienna for a museum, became empty and crumbled.
It now reopens as an exhibition house.
Excited modern post-man architecture, clear shapes, glass halls, brave curves and new building materials, typical of thinking about technical progress, for which the Austrian pavilion of architect Karl Schwanzer for the universal exhibition of 1958 is a suitable and well-known example.
The Schwanzer Pavilion then obtained the Grand Prix of Architecture for its light and floating steel and glass construction.
The construction is considered to this day a contemporary landmark of architecture.
He made the well-known architect who also built the Austrian pavilion in the following universal exhibition.
With its legendary four-cylinder tower for MBW plus the flattened adjacent building in Munich, it became internationally known.
Its pavilion, designed as a temporary construction for exhibitions was erected after the universal exhibition at the Schweizer Garten next to the southern Vienna station, was reformed in 1962 and inside was inaugurated a 20th-century museum, called the "house 20".
The building was suitable but lacked the spaces and technology suitable for a museum; without any walls to hang art, or a good atmosphere.
Despite this it was used until contemporary art in Vienna had a new home.
The building has been emptied since 2001 and increasingly decays.
Currently, architect Adolf Krischanitz renovated the pavilion, now calling it "house 21" and it will be used in the future as a museum of contemporary art.
On Tuesday afternoon the belvedere museum was inaugurated, to which the house belongs, with the first exhibition, a stroke of luck in some way.
Agnes Husslein-Arco, the new director of the Austrian belvedere gallery since 2007, achieved a new beginning.
Husslein-Arco, Ph.D. in art history with a considerable career, among other things, artistic ice skater in her youth, later in Sotheby, the Guggenheim museum and founding director of Salzburg’s modern art museum, carried out the reforms with the insistence of politicians and monument protectors, provided public funds and private sponsors and established their use.
With the exception of contemporary art, the works of sculptor Fritz Wotruba of the homonymous foundation are exhibited, there should be a cafeteria and a bookstore and an office building should be built.
Strict control, low budget
Krischanitz was the best choice to do so.
Not only because he sensitively reformed Joseph Olbrichs’ secession in Vienna, he expanded and was awarded the competition, but because he studied with Schwanzwer at the Technical University of Vienna and the pavilion was for him an “important and immense place”, in which he contemplated “almost every weekend” the exhibitions.
He knew the project and the problem of the building, which disappeared with its move to Vienna in its change of use generously and easily.
In today’s reforms there are big problems, the building was “an energy eater,” Krischanitz said.
The profile of the windows of the glass façade was changed, an insulating glass reinforced with fiberglass was installed and the glass ceiling was replaced by a special security glass.
Thermal bridges and steel beams were reinforced or replaced and asbestos ceilings rehabilitated.
Two basements were used for the new use, a light panel descended between the house and the street provides the lighting, a bridge serves to enter the almost new building, but still the Schwanzer pavilion.
The original exterior doors remain, the house is painted in mahogany antiques and protected against oxidation.
And the movie theater is exactly as it was before.
And all this with a strict control on the part of the office of monuments and a low budget.
"I often had to find material that was good, cheap and also liked the monument protection office," Krischanitz says and warns of rough flooring in the basement.
Despite all the pressures, problems and renovations, the building remains flexible and lightweight.
The pavilion "that came from Brussels" has been difficult, Krischanitz said recently, a typical "casual appearance in the history of the city".
Such an architecture is "always in the wrong place" and that's why he wanted to "awaken the right consciousness."
In this case house 21 is not in the wrong place, there has been a stroke of urban luck. The totally new and planned "neighborhood of the central station", with homes and offices, is next to the Schweizer Garten.
And perhaps another stroke of luck will happen: House 21 could receive a reinforcement of another Krischanitz construction, since the collector Francesca von Habsburg has bought the Berlinese temporary art room and, as you hear, negotiates with the city about a neighboring place in the Schweizer Garten.
Deutsche Bahn plans winter crisis package amounting to millions
The company has devised a crisis plan, which would prevent train breakdowns in winter.
The number of personnel must be doubled.
What costs millions.
In the fight against breakdowns and delays of trains caused by winter, Deutsche Bahn has spent more than 70 million euros this year, according to a news report.
The newspaper "Bild" reported, invoking an internal crisis plan of the group, of the investments planned until 2005 of a total of about 300 million euros.
For which better availability of vehicles and the rail network must be achieved even with extreme time.
Among other things, it is planned to double internal and external employees to 16,000 positions to remove snow from roads and platforms.
The goal is to clean snow and ice platforms for operation and 90 percent of the needles needed for operation within four to five hours.
Two months ago, Rüdiger Grube, president of Deutsche Bahn, warned of breakdowns and train delays also during this winter after a summit with the government and industry.
Last winter Deutsche Bahn suffered considerable problems in the transport of passengers, since, among other things, it lacked reservations to adverse weather conditions.
Many new regional trains have been waiting for years for an authorization from the federal railway office.
In addition there is a delay in the delivery of high-speed trains.
Deutsche Bahn has been working for years to check the axles of high-speed trains frequently in the workshop, after a high-speed train suffered the breakage of an axle at Cologne’s central station.
Deutsche Bank pays million-dollar fines in the U.S.
After the tumult over President Josef Ackerman, a new U.S. problem emerges, the Deutsche Bank must pay a million-dollar fine there.
As if the problems were not enough for Deutche Bank, its past in the U.S. acquires the giant of the sector, the Frankfurt Institute pays 145 million dollars (106 million euros) in an agreement, to end the contests due to the bankruptcy of five large credit unions during the financial crisis.
It’s about the sale of mortgages.
Financial markets regulator NCUA recriminates many large banks inducing credition unions with false promises to buy financial products; they have minimized risks.
Such securities lost their value during the financial crisis and dragged banks into the depths.
"We are pleased to be able to clarify the issue without the parties having to go to court," a Deutsche Bank spokesman in New York said.
The bank doesn't accept any blame for the deal.
This also applies to Citigroup, which forced a payment of 20.5 million dollars.
Debbie Matz, president of the NCUA, welcomed the deference of both financial institutions.
The National Credit Union Administration (NCUA) is responsible for U.S
The NCUA is trying to reintegrate the losses of millions originated.
Agreements currently closed are their first type.
The regulator had also asked accounts for other large banks and in the summer filed lawsuits against JPMorgan Chase, the Royal Bank of Scotland and Goldman Sachs.
In the matter of dubious mortgage securities, these are so-called mortgage-backed securities.
These are based on housing credits.
When the U.S. real estate bubble erupted in 2007, it led to the downfall of many financial institutions.
In September 2008, at the height of the financial crisis, U.S. investment bank Lehman Brothers collapsed.
Although the results happened a few years ago, a tricky wind for U.S. banks is currently blowing.
Investors and the inspection have instituted countless processes to receive indemnities or punish faults.
The Federal Housing Finance Agency (FHFA) has produced the most demands.
It reproaches the 18 major international banks that the two U.S. state mortgage agencies Fannie Mae and Freddie Mac have taken advantage of the mortgage business in a volume of approximately US$200 billion.
Deutsche Bank also has demand from the FHFA, it is businesses worth 14.2 billion dollars from 2005 to 2007.
The inspection demands that the Frankfurt Institute respond from "substantial losses", without naming an exact figure.
Deutsche Bank rejected debt as unfounded and explained that it wants to defend itself.
Warren Buffett Contributes 10 Billion to IBM
Financies guru Buffett invests strongly in information technology company IBM.
Since March it has acquired shares worth more than ten billion dollars.
Until now the great American investor Warren Buffett has always declared not to invest in computer companies or Information Technologies since the forecasts of the long-term development of companies are negative.
But he has read IBM’s business report and changed his opinion on investing in the industry.
He must have understood much earlier that IMB mainly offers services and seeks computer systems for the information technology departments of other companies.
In the financial world, Warren Buffett’s investment is regarded as an honor.
Therefore, the primitive of Information Technologies should be called "Sir IBM".
Buffett currently revealed on the CNBC economics broadcaster that it has acquired shares of IBM worth 10.7 billion dollars since March.
His Berkshire Hathaway of investments has thus risen to one of IBM’s largest shareholders with a 5.5 percent stake.
Buftt said IBM hasn't heard of the commitment.
He commended the leadership that dealt with ordinary benefits in the economic crisis.
“They’ve done a great job,” Buffett said of the strategy.
IMB has for some time established lucrative Information Technology services as the operation of computational centers.
The old 100-year-old computer pioneer also offers software and advice as well as powerful business equipment.
Bufett avoided the technology companies to date.
He said that he only invested in companies whose businesses he understood.
By contrast, Buffett seized the opportunity on a freight train, a lubricant manufacturer or a mechanical engineer.
Su Berkshire Hathaway owns about 80 subsidiaries, as well as part of an entire number of large consortia such as Coca-Cola or Munich Re, formerly Münchener Rück.
Buffett, 81, took over the small textile company Berkshire Hathaway in the 60’s and, with wise investments, expanded it into one of the world’s most valuable companies.
However, her lifestyle is humble.
That's why he's an idol for countless investors around the world.
His quick and infallible sense of making money has provided him with the nickname "Oracle of Omaha".
After discovering Buffett’s investment, IBM’s stock rose one percent.
“The blue giant,” as IBM is known, has a stock market value of more than 220 billion dollars and figures, along with Apple and Microsoft, among the most valuable technology companies in the world.
With Microsoft, Buffett said, he doesn’t want to collaborate.
Bill Gates, its founder, is a close friend.
The threat is realized
Following the discovery of "Zwickau cell", the Turkish media doubt the German rule of law.
A newspaper speaks of a "bloody ideology" that revives again.
The murders of eight small businessmen tricks and Greeks between 2001 and 2006 have a far-right background.
In Turkey and in the German population of Turkish origin it has reacted with fear and concern to this news.
For many Germans of Turkish origin returned a ghost that was believed to have been dead long ago, since the fire-shaped attacks in Mölln in November 1992 and in Solingen in May 1993, there had been no further episodes of far-righter violence against Turks.
However, there has never been any question of xenophobia in Germany.
The uncovered far-right network reveals a magnitude whose dimensions were not foreseeable.
"Is bloody ideology rekindled?" headlines the online edition of Turkish newspaper "Haberturk."
"Sabah" newspaper stresses on the contrary that one of the perpetrators of the crime was an informant of the Federal Office of Defense of the Constitution and is concerned that German authorities may be implicated in the far-right swamp.
The Turkish community in Germany reacted sensibly, the weekend reminded the victims of racist violence with a vigil in front of the Brandenburg Gate.
Two weeks earlier, the Turkish community in Berlin along with German and Turkish politicians reflected in a solemnal act of the 50th anniversary of the Turkish-German agreement.
The coexistence was staged with cheerful colors, like a story with a happy ending.
The information came to some Turkish media as a slapper: "The German gift for the 50th anniversary", reads the title of an article on the Turkish internet platform "Haber X", referring to the background of the chain of murders.
The German broadcast "Hürriyet" also does not insist on tracing this mental connection.
“It starts to smell bad,” reads Ahmet Külahci’s column title.
He emphasizes that those killed were people who paid their taxes and made an important contribution with their work to the reconstruction of Germany.
In German internet forums of Turkish origin, the behavior of the police and their subsequent success in the searches are discussed very critically.
The perpetrators are shocked that the German authorities have not searched for suspicions in the far-right circle much earlier.
Some is shown at least rhetoric in terms of drastic means: "From now here it is lost, immigrants have nothing left but self-defense and armament."
“This will be the best self-protection.”
“Germany’s internal peace strongly depends on the quality of the clarification and the adequacy of the sanction to the entire network,” “Selen writes.”
This means that, if Germany’s internal peace is in danger, Mölln and Solingen’s attacks will be clarified in ninety years.
The image of the ruins of the Solingen fire has been engraved in German and Turkish memoirs.
No other event more clearly showed the Turks living here that there is a concrete threat and, to the Germans, that mistrust and distance can lead to violence.
Attacks exacerbate the group sentiment of the Turkish side.
Don't repeat the mistakes of the past
In the Turkish press it was then mainly criticized the deficiency of German policy after the attacks, Helmut Kohl refuses to visit Mölln’s survivors.
Following the Solingen fire-shaped attack he sent a telegram of condolences to the Turkish president and Klaus Kinkel, his foreign minister, replaced him at the funeral in Cologne. Kinkel detailed in his speech what the Turks of Germany contribute to taxes.
It is clear how the value of a person is calculated according to the amount of their taxes.
The current information of the Turkish media again shows how deep is, since then, the distrust of many Germans of Turkish origin in the legality of this country.
In addition to the effort of a broad explanation and revaluation of far-right terrorism in Germany, this time there are a need for frank words.
Zurich Lake Scandal Bank
Europe’s riskiest bank is not in Italy or France, but in Switzerland.
UBS nearly went bankrupt in the financial crisis and has since slipped from one crisis to the next.
Now it's run by a German.
Surely Axel Weber didn’t think about it.
After his spectacular departure from the summit of the federal bank, in July it was known that in 2013 he would begin in a new and almost equally spectacular work: Chairman of the administrative board of the great Swiss bank UBS, an unexpected and lucrative step in his career.
Axel Weber rejoices publicly, praises Switzerland for its beauty and the UBS for its newly acquired solidity and drops friendly observations about its future companions.
"USB has achieved revitalization."
"I'm happy to collaborate with Kaspar Villiger and Oswald Grübel."
In September it became clear that everything would be totally different.
A USB financial operator in London avoided all the bank’s security systems and defrauded 1.7 billion euros.
Oswald Grübel, director general of the USB, a banker not only known in Switzerland, had to attend.
The president of the USB’s administrative board has since been considered equally touched, and everyone asked: When does Axel Weber arrive?
It became known that Weber must advise the bank since February 2012.
And he will take over the chairmanship of the board in May, not in early 2013, as the bank said on Tuesday.
It's hard for Axel Weber.
He is not a banker, rather, a professor of economics and counselor and has had a surely necessary period of adaptation.
Suddenly he is responsible for the strategy of a bank, which has just proven that it deserves the title "the biggest bank of the scandal in Europe" for a long time.
On the one hand, the USB is huge, until recently it was the world’s largest asset manager, and on the other hand, for years, it has slipped from one scandal to the next.
Nothing, it seems so, is spared the once so proud Swiss bank.
The financial crisis began.
USB had an excellent reputation until 2007.
The image of the discreet and solid Swiss goods manager, but also sluggish, was combined with ambitious goals, he also wanted to be a large number in investment banks.
On the one hand, with 1.6 billion dollars, the USB manages more money from wealthy customers than any other bank in the world.
On the other hand, he advanced as an investment bank in the league of the big banks of the world.
This impresses.
Hans Geiger, the former banker and later banking teacher in Zurich for many years explains: “Before my students, I’ve always rated USB as a model for a good bench.”
"From the current point of view, one wonders: How could I be wrong?"
He was in a big mistake.
In 2007 it came to light that something went very wrong on USB.
In the middle of the year, Peter Wuffli, managing director, who until then was regarded as a great and very smart banker, resigned surprisingly.
A few months later, the reason was suspected.
The bank’s investment fund embarked on losses with high-risk securities and had to amortize billions.
To charge with this, the USB announced an injection of capital of no less than 13 billion francs.
For this he found investors, naturally that some would look with contempt until shortly before: an Asian and opaque state fund of Singapore and an Arab state fund, so discreet, that his name had never been heard.
This was just the first blow.
In April 2008, it got worse.
The bank had to declare the loss of billions, Marcel Ospel, president of the administrative board, in 2006 on Switzerland’s second-highest-paid administrative board, resigned.
In October 2008, the catastrophe continued, in view of its mistaken investments, UBS found itself in the financial crisis in the face of bankruptcy and had to be rescued by the state.
The national bank bought problematic securities from UBS for many billions, the Swiss Confederation gave six billion francs for a obligatorily convertible bond, through which it became co-owner of the bank.
All of Switzerland was affected, it was claimed how dramatic this was for a small country, if it houses such large banks.
Finally, the sum of UBS balance in 2007 was more than five times higher than Switzerland’s economic results (see graph).
"We can't rescue UBS again," Geiger, a banking expert, said.
To the first catastrophe was joined by the second.
A few months later, UBS capitulated in a fiscal conflict with America and was willing to deliver data from wealthy customers, who had possibly defrauded taxes with the bank’s help.
In addition, it approves a figure of 780 billions of dollars, reimbursement of unjustified benefits of these businesses with compensation for payment.
With this, the UBS confessed more or less to having helped in tax fraud.
Simultaneously he lost something that had always been his capital in the competition for rich customers: the halo of discretion.
After the scandal, the secrecy of the Swiss bank is no longer the same.
This was followed by the resignation of the chairman of the board of directors and the chairman of the administrative board.
The exodus of customer funds.
In this way, in a few months the bank lost its credibility in its two central sectors: in investment banking through erroneous speculation and in the administration of assets through the help of tax fraud.
And just when it was somewhat quieter again, just when it was thought that the UBS would get it again, this unfortunate history of the operator happens, an individual operator defrauded 1.7 billion euros, before the bank realized that something was wrong.
And it unveiled, apparently, this still doesn't work well with risk management in investment banking.
"The Adoboli case states that risk controls have changed too little," says Martin Janssen, professor of economics and financies at the Banking Institute at the University of Zurich.
The chief financial officer of a large German bank is also sure: “The problem with UBS is that it doesn’t dominate risk controls.”
From what arose the idea of precaution, with which the Swiss bankers presented themselves for a long time, to be the guardians of the greatest goods in the world: no risk too great, instead, the security of money wants to fail on the other side of the Alpine republic.
The security deposits of Swiss banks were the symbol of unwavering reliability, such as Lindt and Sprüngli of authentic chocolate.
The UBS headquarters in Zurich does not fit the cliché of the solid Swiss private bank, it is only a little larger.
The situation is classic.
The bank is headquartered on Zurich’s Bahnhofstraße Street, which runs from the main station directly to Lake Zurich, framed by the snow-covered summits of the Swiss mountains.
Here the buildings are ostentatious, the rentals are one of the highest in Europe.
Here you will find jewellers like Tiffany`s among traditional Swiss businesses such as Sprüngli confectionery.
Here the Swiss banks reside for years: from the national bank, through the Credit Suisse palace, to the elitarian private banks like Julius Bär.
You enter the UBS reception room using a heavy revolving door.
Inside the walls are lined with marble, in front are large leather armchairs, next to the logo of the bench there is a large sign to indicate "security".
The UBS knows what the customer is looking for here: Security.
The two large Swiss banks, the UBS and Credit Suisse, at some point have displaced the idea of the great Swiss bank, or betrayed it, as the president of a small Swiss private bank thinks.
He prefers not to read his name in the newspaper, after all he does, like all Swiss bankers, also with the businesses of both big banks.
The UBS has become too large, he thinks, mainly in investment banking, where the Anglo-Saxons have gained ground.
There the Swiss had nothing more to say.
"And they have long become accustomed to the customs of the Anglo-Saxons."
In fact, the UBS changed Anglo-Saxon investment banking, delivered its first big profit and then came the disaster, no European financial institution had lost as much money in the high-risk jungle as the large Swiss bank.
On October 15, 2008, the disaster was evident.
"On Wednesday the government coalition of the small country of seven million decided the largest rescue operation in the history of its nation," writes author Lukas Hässig in his book "Der UBS-Crash".
Politicians granted up to 68 billions of francs or approximately 45 billion euros to save the big bank from bankruptcy.
How'd you think?
We talk to the Swiss bankers, so they see the problem in the dimension of UBS.
In June 1998, two of the three major Swiss banks merged into a powerful consortium.
A bank emerges that, due to the merger, was too large for little Switzerland, and continues to grow.
Therefore, it served that the two different precursor banks of the UBS were complemented: a rich but asleep bank with the center of the administration of goods in Zurich and a poorer but stronger internationalization.
Suddenly, internationalized investment banks arrived in a timely manner with a lot of money, with which they could roll a great financial bike.
There are two names related to this UBS increase: Peter Wuffli, head of the UBS consortium from 2001 to 2007, and Marcel Ospel, then chairman of the administrative board.
“I always thought Wuffli and Ospel were a perfect team,” says banking expert Hans Geiger.
"Wuffli, the intellectual, modest, who understands a lot of models and statistics, and Ospel, the intuitive who managed to break through from learning in the bank to the summit."
Finally, Ospel trusted Wuffli.
And in the end Wuffli probably made a decisive mistake: "He thought his models were a reality," Geiger says.
"It's a tragic case."
Tragic, perhaps.
It's clear: The destination came before the case.
UBS employees clearly remember the meetings of managers, in which Wuffli, the head of the consortium, always repeated the same motto: Lower Goldman Sach.
The U.S. bank was the most powerful investment bank in the world.
They led the "League Tables", the classifications that then documented the success of the banks.
UBS wanted to be at the summit.
Some steps went well for him.
In forex trading and stock trading it was at the top.
However, the consultants of companies in charge highlighted a weak point: In the business of international interest, in the banking language "fixed income", there is enormous compensation for the big banks.
UBS has gaps in U.S. mortgage products.
To fill these gaps, the UBS began an adventure that almost costed its existence.
Avid with growth, they invested both the same bank and one of its high-risk funds created in high-risk U.S. investments.
It was transformed into a powerful machine to seize U.S. mortgage loans and then sell them, keeping certain risks on its own account.
And also, as other banks sought the descent for a long time.
“In the summer of 2007, as the American real estate market was on fire, the UBS bought there,” Geiger, a banking expert, said.
So great euphoria, so deep the fall.
A manager had to leave, as the attempt to order proved more complicated than they expected.
Positions on UBS became projectable seats and are insecure to date.
Currently, whoever runs a big bench, has an almost impossible job.
You need to find a strategy for a bank, to which all logical business models are lost.
Geiger, a bank expert, has an idea, ending investment banking, concentrating on property management, returning to Swiss room.
The bank doesn’t want to go that far (yet).
But investment banking must decrease a lot, it’s now clear.
The following Thursday, Sergio Ermotti, the bank’s new president, will present his future strategy to the world.
In reality, it was only thought of the 51-year-old Italian-Swiss as a transitional solution, as the bank was surprisingly separated from its predecessor Grübel.
Meanwhile, he prevailed: He must permanently run UBS businesses, the Zurich bank said on Tuesday.
Ermotti wants to return the investment bank to its state in the mid 1990s.
Suppress 3,500 posts.
But this isn't enough.
UBS needs vision.
Who is it?
What do you want to make money with?
These are the questions Axel Weber is surely asked today.
So far he only says that he will first look for housing with his wife in Zurich.
The following year.
