Smart grosses spread in cities
In many places the farms are considered "problematic birds": these intelligent animals are not expelled from the cities for a long time, experts say.
Some like them, others want to get rid very quickly of them, the farms belong for years to the image of the city, but in many municipalities the complaints of the neighbors are accumulated due to crap and the noise of animals.
Although there has been no means against the "problematic birds" until now.
Cranks no longer come out of the cities, say experts in the first symposium on hats in Leer, East Frisia.
There specialists from all over the national territory discuss possible solutions.
They are looking for alternatives in cities
Environmentalists attribute cities’ problems to migrating the field from certain protected hats; chemical applications in agriculture visibly end traditional living spaces.
Hunting, the increasing building and the fall of trees that host them hinder the lives of birds.
Cranks are protected.
There are large colonies with more than 600 breeding pairs in Soest, Westphalia, among others, in the northwest, among others in Jever, Diepholz and Achim in Bremen, as well as in Leer.
There in 2005 a controversial project to kill the district’s hats jumped to negative headlines in 2005.
Critics have called the hunting of the animals through bird traps and murder with sticks as especially brutal.
"Expelling them and scaring them away in a normal way you can't get anything," said Werner Klöver, urban planner and landscaper of Leer.
Shooting the nests with water or throwing the trees are usually unintended consequences, says Manfred Kaiser, Lahr’s environmental expert, Baden-Württemberg.
"Animals deviate, large colonies divide and distribute elsewhere."
Scaring them away is only imaginable as an exception in hospitals, nursing homes or schools.
“We have to live with them, we don’t catch them anymore.”
Without the surroundings, the problem is not solved in cities, says Wolfgang Kellner, mayor of Leer.
It is possible to scare animals away from sensitive residential areas and baits to re-settle elsewhere.
"Informative panels instead of chain saws" Uta Maria Jürgens, psychologist and ethnologist advertises against how animals are treated in a totally different way: "It is best that we can withstand a beak in the face with aplomb."
Jürgens co-founded in Schleswig-Holstein the teaching circuit on Ascheberger's hats, where signs report the lives of intelligent birds.
In the meantime, the project is accepted by tourists.
Jürgens also has his own opinion on the criticism for the noise of birds: "It's all forms of communication, they have a lot to say."
The intelligence and skill of these animals are legendary, picks not only take advantage of the resources to access shale snacks, but they manufacture tools to use them as resources.
Recently ethologists from the University of Cambridge checked in four slots how far bird intelligence goes.
In one experiment, the birds quickly learned that they had to drop a stone on a platform in order to reach a moth larva.
Without much training, they decisively recognized the size and shape that the stone must have to fall on the platform.
In a second trial, cunning farms had to solve a complicated problem.
Investigators placed a small cube with a moth larva in a vertical cylinder.
Birds couldn't reach their prey with their beaks.
Investigators left them a piece of wire.
With which the four animals formed a hook, with which they fished the bucket in the pitcher of the tube.
Three of the foundrants got it on the first try.
