Massacre on a personal level
Peter Englund's Intensive and Generous New Book, The Beauty and the Sorrow: An Intimate Story about World War I, "begins with a long drama personae of the kind that can cause one's soul to fall at one's feet.
Here are a few acting tips: Gently start this page and turn it into your brand pages.
He will get to know these people very well in Mr. Englund’s novelistic narrative, and this dramatis personae will function as his GPS, a lighthouse during those few moments in which, like one of his men and women, he is confused and helpless in the fog of war.
Mr. Englund is a Swedish historian and journalist.
He is also the new permanent secretary of the Swedish Academy, which awards the Nobel Prize for Literature.
What you’ve written here is an uncommoning book, which rightly describes as “an anti-history work.”
It contains some notable names, important treatises or famous battles; there are virtually no ambassadors, elegant journalists or discussions about tactics or material.
It’s not so much a book about what happened, he explains, like “a book about how it happened.” It’s about “feelings, impressions, experiences, and moods.”
"Beauty and the pain of the battle "combines the wartime experiences of about 20 ordinary men and women, on both sides of the war, from schoolgirls and botanists to climbers, doctors, ambulance drivers and administrators.
Some of these people will become heroes.
Some will become prisoners of war, or they will lose their members, they will go crazy or die.
Their lives blink here as votive candles lit in a church; new ones are added to the mix while others go out.
Mr. Englund's book is a departure from standard history books.
It is also corrective of the notion that the First World War was only the dire trench war on the western front.
"Beautity and the Pain of the Battle" moves expertly through other theaters of war: the Alps, the Balkans, the Eastern Front, Mesopotamia, East Africa.
The soldiers in this book fall over the hives; one spends Christmas in Egypt under the pyramids; tse-tse flies are an intractable problem.
This is a poignant book, almost from the beginning.
War floods these people’s lives as a natural catastrophe, a Hurricane Katrina that succkes chordite.
When artillery fire is heard in the distance, and you're a woman who's at home with your children, do you stay or flee?
Who's coming anyway?
Hardly anyone understands what's going on, not even why you have to fight this war.
"Lack of facts," observes Mr. Englund, "has been filled with conjectures, assumptions, hopes, fears, fixed ideas, conspiracy theories, dreams, nightmares and rumors."
"Beautity and the Pain of Battle" follows individuals such as Florence Farmborough, an English nurser in the Russian army, and Richard Stumpf, a young German sailor from the offshore fleet.
Their stories have been obtained for the most part from memoirs, letters, and other material previously published.
The explanations of their lives can be terrifying or moving, but they are more full of life in the accumulation of small moments, lost details, of Mr. Englund.
Many of these are about deprivations and getting them right with what’s out there.
About telling the story of a German schoolgirl says: "Succedenaries, everywhere succedaneous."
"Coffee substitute, fake aluminum, imitation rubber, paper bandages, wooden buttons."
He continues to talk about Swedish food in Germany.
It describes "meat made from cooked rice and pressed in ram fat (and topped with a false bone made of wood); tobacco made from roots and dry potato peels; shoes with wooden soles".
There are, he observes, "837 permitted meat substitutes in the production of sausages, 511 registered coffee substitutes."
Some stories are about honor and value.
An American acknowledges his own impetus towards savagery and manifests about war: "You feel that, after all, this is what men are designed for and not to sit on armchairs with a cigar and whiskey, the afternoon newspaper or a best seller, and pretend that that varnish means civilization and that there is no barbarian behind that starchy and studded chest."
Instead a British soldier realizes that he will probably die, and that no one will find out or care.
“Once one resigns oneselves to the thought of sacrificing oneself, one would like to think that it could happen in front of the public,” he writes.
Instead, it’s horribly resemblance “to a condemned man being strangled in secrecy.”
Other observations are about an old Europe disappearing, and about new types of terror.
“Conflict has increasingly become an economic competition,” Mr. Englund writes, “a war between factories.”
It reflects the arrival of what he calls "a new species in the bestiary of the young century: The eloquent mass murderer and ideologically convinced with good-made clothes that execute his butchery while sitting behind a desk."
People behave in unforeseen ways; there is both innoble and heroic behavior.
Mr. Englund talks about the soldiers who actively tried to catch a venereal disease of prostitutes as a way to evade service at the front.
"The most grotesque expression of this can be seen the trade in gonococcal pus, which the soldiers buy and embarrass their genitals in the hope of ending up in the hospital," he writes.
"Those who are truly desperate rub it on their eyes, which often results in lifelong blindness."
In this Swedish translation by Peter Graves, Mr. Englund's prose agile but not extravagant is perfectly adapted to his human task.
In dozens of small scenes it reflects the way in which the war of "exceptionally uncontrollable forces: extreme nationalism, social revolution, religious hatred."
People are starting to ask why their leaders are making them fight.
The best books about the First World War have often been indirect, such as Paul Fussell’s The Great War and Modern Memoir, or novels such as Unnevelty on Erich Maria Remarque’s front, rather than integral stories.
Mr. Englund's volume joins an unconventional pantheon.
His book has one of the most devastating endings I can remember in a non-fiction work.
I won't reveal it.
But it's like I've come from his book, ripped from your fingers that page of dramatis personae, and burned it with a match.
After a misstepass previous, a planned assault to the minute
Hundreds of police officers were involved, some of them wearing riot helmets.
It was chosen Monday night to Tuesday because Zuccotti Park would be emptier.
The operation was kept secret except for a few high-ranking officers, initially telling the others that they were joining an exercise.
Police Inspector Raymond W. Kelly was at the center, his presence underscoring how the operation plagued challenges for the police department.
Episodes of recent weeks could not be repeated, such as spraying pepper spray on protesters, violating the department’s rules and creating a storm of public sympathy for the “squatters.”
And that is why the police operation to clear the Zuccotti park of protesters was revealed after two weeks of planning and training.
Agents had prepared to see how occupations had unfolded in other cities.
A major catastrophe drill was taking place on Randalls Island, with an eye staring towards Zuccotti.
Officers increased so-called disorder training — counter-terrorism measures that involved quickly mobilizing a large number of police officers — to focus on Lower Manhattan.
The last training session was on Monday night on the Manhattan side of the East River.
Orders to enter the park came in the "last minute," said someone familiar with the orders, who referred to the mission only as "an exercise."
"To the few cops I know who were called for this, they didn't tell them it was to get into Zuccotti Park," said the person, who spoke anonymously.
"The only people who knew they were going into Zuccotti Park were at the highest levels of the department."
One reason for secrecy was a lesson learned by the city.
On 14 October, officers wanted to clear the park, but gave up when hundreds of protesters surfaced early after hearing about the plans.
Tuesday’s operation involved agents from several police units, including special forces from across the municipal district — dozens of mobile agents who routinely flood neighborhoods with high crime rates.
Mr. Kelly said that many people, such as those who travel daily to work, had been going back and forth from the park during the day, making 1 a.m. a good time to get in.
"It was appropriate to do it when there were the fewest people in the park," he said.
Emergency Service Unit trucks with spotlights and speakers gathered at Pike Slip’s crossroads with Franklin D. Roosevelt Drive, near the Manhattan Bridge, before leaving.
Lights and pre-recorded messages rumbling from the speakers seemed to intimidate many protesters.
Communit affairs agents went to the park with its light blue windbreakers, many protesters simply picked up their stuff and left.
No stores were touched until 1:45 a.m., the police said, giving protesters time to pick up their belongings.
Other teams of agents were seen meeting at the perimeter to enter if arrests were needed in the park.
Journalists in the park were forced to abandon him.
Paul J. Browne, the police department’s chief spokesman, said it was for his safety.
But many journalists said that attempts were made to prevent them from seeing police take action in the park, and that they had been treated harshly by officers.
Mr. Browne said mobile television units on Church Street, along the western edge of the park, were able to capture images.
As police moved west through the protesters’ dense tangle of personal effects, including luggage and trash bags of paper and plastic clothing crowded, Department of Cleaning teams followed, collecting what was left behind.
Some demonstrators who refused to move were dragged outside, appearing images on the Internet shortly afterwards.
A central group of protesters took positions near the camp’s kitchen area, near the park’s center.
Some made a barrier, and the police came in to arrest them methodically.
About 10 people in the epicenter of the camp hung through their arms.
And two people chained themselves to trees, Mr. Kelly said.
Emergency officers were called to cut the locks.
No arrests were made in the park until about 3:30 a.m., Mr. Kelly said.
The clearance operation was complete about 75 minutes later, police said.
Mr. Browne said about 142 people were arrested in the park.
Most of the arrests were for “alternation of public order and resistance to authority,” he said.
The highest-ranking agent at the scene was Joseph J. Esposito, the head of the department, the uniformed senior officer in the department.
Phil T. Pulaski, the department's chief detective, was also there.
Mr. Kelly, while present, "wasn't directing the operations," Mr. Browne said.
A seconded group of officers waited along the eastern side of Broadway, between Liberty and Cedar streets, in case they had to enter.
Barricades were erected on Cortlandt Street, a block north of the park, and on Pine Street, a block to the south.
From those positions, the police were seen widening the perimeter even beyond Zuccotti Park.
At one point, a number of agents, many of them with shields, were seen pushing people out.
There were about 28 people on the northern perimeter.
Some of the noisiest action of the night took place south of the park.
At around 5 a.m., south of Pine Street, a protester jumped on the hood of a police car, and others were seen removing the air from the wheels of a police van.
At one point, a piece of plywood flew out from the crowd.
In the end, an officer and a protester were hospitalized.
The support workers union will sue the city for layoffs
The union representing hundreds of school support workers who lost their jobs last month plans to sue the city on Wednesday, claiming the layoffs were unnecessary and discriminatory due to their disproportionate impact on schools serving poor students.
The lawsuit is possibly the last weapon available to the union, of municipal district 37, in its effort to overturn layoffs.
Six hundred and seventy-twhose school aides, parent coordinators and other employees who were among the city’s worst paid lost their jobs on Oct. 7, in a move that union leaders have described as political revenge for their refusal to allow the city’s access to a health care fund in charge of working groups to close their budget gap last spring.
Municipal authorities have categorically denied that accusation.
At a City Hall hearing last month, education adviser Dennis M. Walcott said the layoffs were part of the budget approved in June.
One member of the Council counterattacked by telling him that nothing in the budget specified that school support staff members would be dismissed.
City District 37 officials said they filed the claim in writing, warning the Department of Education that it would be sued. A news conference will be held Wednesday to announce the lawsuit’s official filing at the State Supreme Court in Manhattan.
The lawsuit will include Mr. Walcott and the city’s Department of Education as defendants, and has eight workers fired as plaintiffs, all black or Latino women, as well as the majority of attendees who lost their jobs.
Elizabeth Thomas, a spokesman for the city’s Legal Department, said the city had not seen the lawsuit and therefore could not comment on its specific allegations.
According to a draft lawsuit obtained by The New York Times, the union has focused on the number of layoffs in the city’s poor schools to support an unfair treatment claim, saying poor schools lost more attendees, as they had been chronically undersubsidized.
It is pointed out that while there were no layoffs on Staten Island and a few layoffs in schools on the Upper East Side or TriBeCa, 17 school aides lost their jobs in District 23 in Brownsville, Brooklyn, and 46 were fired in Districts 8 and 9 in the South Bronx.
Considering the loss of five of the eight assistants employed by Public School 36 in Harlem, where 68 percent of students live in poverty, demand says, “This is not a neighborhood of wealthy parents who can raise funds to cover the budget deficit.”
P.D. District 36’s budget dropped by 3.26 percent this year, and it also lost money when the city changed the way it calculates additional funding for each enrolment per poor student in schools.
The union also claims that high school principals may have been deceived by language in a memorandum authorizing dismissal from parent coordinators.
The memorandum used the word "surpending" to describe the action, even though "surpsure" more often describes the act of moving a teacher of the payroll from a school to a job board, whose salaries are paid by the headquarters.
“Sixty-six parent coordinators were fired,” the draft complaint reads, “and not only in surplus.”
Mission Completed, Congress Ready to Reach Agreement on Libya
It seems that Republicans and Democrats on Capitol Hill are willing to agree on something related to the uprising in Libya.
All that was needed was for the rebellion to succeed, for veteran dictator Muammar Gaddafi to be captured and killed and U.S. involvement came to an end.
On Tuesday, the Senate Foreign Affairs Committee will consider a resolution, sponsored by President John Kerry (Democratic for Massachusetts), along with Senators John McCain (R-Ariz.) and Joseph I. Lieberman (independent for Connecticut), applauding the Libyans for their successful rebellion and U.S. troops for their "courage."
"This resolution is to honor Libya's braves as they begin to rebuild their country," Kerry said.
“It’s an affirmation of bipartisan support for their democratic aspirations.”
Language may seem harmless, but nothing about the debate over Libya has been easy until now.
This summer, lawmakers from both parties were fighting with each other and with the White House about drafting a series of resolutions to approve or reject the role of the U.S. military in the NATO mission in Libya.
In June, Kerry and McCain introduced a resolution authorizing the limited use of U.S. military forces in Libya.
The bill was defeated in the House by a large bipartisan margin, which was never voted in the Senate.
But on the same day the bill was rejected in the House, the chamber also rejected a measure that would have slashed funds for U.S. operations in Libya.
That left the U.S. mission in a kind of legislative purgatory — with Congress upset enough to criticize Obama’s handling of the mission, but not so angry as to actually mow the grass under his feet.
Next, congressional indecision was overcome by events.
Tripoli fell into the hands of Libyan rebels in August, and Gaddafi was killed on October 20.
NATO's mission officially ended on October 31.
Now the Senate could be ready to act.
The Foreign Affairs Committee's resolution "congratulates the people of Libya for their tremendous courage and extraordinary resistance to liberating themselves" and "praises the men and women of the U.S
The resolution also "declares the national interest of the United States in a successful and irreversible transition to democracy in Libya."
Will the new measure win the full support of the Foreign Affairs Committee, or will it divide legislators as the June bill did?
The last version was rejected by Senator Richard G. Lugar (Indiana), the main Republican of the committee.
Lugar’s spokesman said on Monday that he had not yet seen the final version of the new bill.
On the other side of the Capitol, the House will vote this week on a constitutional amendment demanding a balanced budget, a legislative priority for almost all Republican congressmen and some Democrats.
The measure, sponsored by Republican Bob Goodlatte (Republican for Virginia), will be the first constitutional amendment on the budget or any other issue that in fact wins an assembly-wide vote in the House or Senate in this Congress.
Other legislators have not been successful so far to get their amendments to the assembly, but not for lack of attempts.
Goodlatte’s is just one of 68 constitutional amendments that have been introduced in this Congress, covering a wide range of issues.
Some are duplicates - the same bill is proposed in both the House and the Senate - and others with slight variations from each other.
There are at least 15 versions of the budget balance amendment just in the House, and another handful that “will control federal spending.”
Some of those bills are also identical to each other, but they give their sponsors — especially novice Republicans looking to refine their resumes — the opportunity to boast that they were the “authors” of that measure.
The Budget Control Act, which was passed in August, required both the House and the Senate to vote on a budget balance amendment.
In March, 58 senators voted in favor of a non-binding resolution supporting the idea of that amendment.
Beyond the budget, several members want to limit the number of terms members of Congress can serve, while Rep. José E. Serrano (Democrat for New York), wants to remove the two-term limit for presidents.
Rep. Jesse L. Jackson Jr. (Democrat for Illinois) has proposed a number of amendments that guarantee the right to quality education and health care, among other issues.
An amendment prohibiting same-sex marriage is a perennial proposal, as well as one prohibiting the burning of flags.
Some lawmakers also want to allow the repeal of federal laws if they are rejected by two-thirds of states.
So enthusiastic that it has been the members when it comes to proposing amendments, they are not moving at a faster rate than in previous years.
In the 111th Congress, legislators proposed 77 amendments, and submitted 66 in the 110th.
The pace is unacceptable compared to that of the 1990s, when members regularly submitted more than 150 amendments every two years, according to figures provided by the Senate Library.
But they didn’t have much more success then than they have now.
The last amendment that was addeded - 27 - was ratified in 1992, but in fact it was proposed by Congress in 1789.
"Struggled but not defeated," Giffords speaks in his first TV interview since the shooting
Ten months after a gunman shot at point-blank range at a constituency rally in Tucson, Rep. Gabrielle Giffords (Democrat for Arizona) appeared on national television on Monday night, sang accompanied by the Broadway melody "The sun will come out tomorrow" and said she wanted to recover more than she wanted to return to Congress.
In his appearance on ABC News "20/20", with Diane Sawyer, Giffords struggled to form prayers and needed walking help.
The program documented his difficult path back from the damage caused by a bullet that fractured his skull and pierced the left side of his brain, and passing from the front to the back of his head.
Three months ago, he returned by surprise to the House to cast his triumphant vote on the debt ceiling agreement.
In his first television interview since the January shooting, which killed six and wounded 13, Giffords smiled, laughed and sang - and described his recovery as "difficult."
When Sawyer asked him how he feels, Giffords, 41, replied, "Pretty good."
The interview with Giffords and her husband, astronaut Mark Kelly, was part of an hour-long “20/20” program, which aired on the eve of the publication of the couple’s new book, “Gabby: A Story of Courage and Hope.”
The special program looked at Giffords’ recovery since the Jan. 8 shooting.
In videos recorded by Kelly in the first few weeks, she is seen in her hospital bed, with a shaved head and a long scar piercing her forehead.
She can only keep one or two upright fingers, while her husband encourages her.
Months later, Giffords is seen sitting in a wheelchair while therapists teach him how to perform simple actions.
A head movement.
Frunch of the lips.
And then his first word: "What."
In a few days, Giffords uttered another word – “roasted,” a request to change his breakfast menu.
Videos released by ABC show that the music played a central role in Giffords' recovery: Congresswoman is seen singinginging along with therapists the songs "Girls Just Wanna Have Fun" by Cyndi Lauper and "Free Falling" by Tom Petty.
In an interview with Sawyer, Giffords, who does two hours of therapy every day, said he doesn't remember the day of the shooting.
Her husband said she was reading Giffords a newspaper article about the March 12 murder when she first arrested him to inquire about the six who had died in the incident.
Suspicious gunman Jared Lee Loughner is being held in a Missouri prison and is being forcibly medicated while awaiting trial.
"A lot of people died," Giffords told Sawyer.
"Hard, hard, hard."
Kelly told Sawyer that at one point in his wife's recovery, she said, "I've been defeated."
"So, I said, 'Gabby, you haven't been defeated," Kelly said.
“You’ve just been beaten.
And you're coming out of this, and you're gonna recover and you're gonna come back stronger than ever."
Troops make you feel more sorry than respect
The event was a Wall Street gala that raised millions of dollars for homeless veterans in New York.
Kid Rock sang a ballad about impotence, frustration and loss.
At that time, several hundred soldiers, sailors, aviators and Marines broke into their posts around him.
The etiquette crowd stood up and clapped.
“Men and women were regarded as heroes,” said David Saltzman, who organized the spring fundraiser.
A high-ranking military officer at the gala, also known as then-Joint Chiefs of Staff Speaker Mike Mullen, saw the role of troops differently.
“They showed them as a kind of orphaned child,” the officer wrote in an email.
I'm sure the organizers had good intentions.
I know they had them.
But it wasn't respect, I really was sorry.
Markedly contradictory impressions illustrate the difficult relationship that has gripped the military and an often-distant, sometimes,-adoring American public.
Troops are lavished with praise for their sacrifices.
But praise comes at a price, service members say.
The public increasingly acts as if they are punished by those uniformed.
“We’re not victims at all,” said Brigadier General Sean B. MacFarland, who commanded troops in Iraq and will soon depart for Afghanistan.
"But it seems that the only way for some to show their support is to put us on the role of helpless souls."
The issue is a very sensitive issue for military leaders, who don’t want to seem ungrateful or at odds with the public they serve.
They also realize that the anger faced by troops who returned in the last years of the Vietnam War was much worse.
As a result, most conversations about pity take place quietly and privately among war veterans.
After his two sons returned from fighting with the Marine Corps, retired Colonel Mark Cancian warned them that people apart from the military would consider their service from two perspectives.
Some would look at them with a sense of fear because they confronted the insurgents and traveled to exotic places.
Others would wonder if there would be an “angry and violent veteran beneath the surface,” said Cancian, who fought in Iraq and returned to high-level government work in Washington.
During his job search, he said, he felt some interviewers had subtly asked him if he would be able to endure under the pressure of demanding work from Washington immediately after his combat tour.
"When you're talking about your service, you need to deal with negative impressions," Cancian recalled telling his children.
Military unease arises, in part, from American indifference to wars.
Achievements on the battlefield are rarely chosen for praise by a country that has unfamiliarity with the military and sees few direct benefits from the wars in Iraq and Afghanistan.
"We, as a nation, no longer value military heroism in ways that were entirely common in World War II," said retired Lieutenant General David Barno, who commanded U.S. troops in Afghanistan.
On the other hand, the praises of politicians and the public are largely focused on the depths of the suffering of a service member.
Troops are recognized by the number of tours they have endured, the number of friends they have lost, or the extent of their injuries.
The army is facing increasing pressure to end rape
This week, a historic hearing will decide whether 28 women and men have a case against the military for their alleged passivity in the face of rape.
If not, hundreds of plaintiffs are organizing for the next one.
First class soldier Elizabeth Lyman was 25 years old and 11 weeks pregnant the night she says she was raped by a partner.
It was October 2008, and he was on his way back to his barracks at Miramar Air Base north of San Diego after dinner with a friend when he met a colleague carrying a package of 12 cans of beer.
He asked if he could join her in his room for a drink.
“An alarm signal sounded,” Lyman says.
However, the Texas native set aside her concern.
I thought, no, it's 19.
“He’s from Texas,” he says.
"I wish I had listened to my instincts."
Within a few minutes of entering Lyman’s room, the man approached her from behind, throwing her down and raping her.
This week, Nov. 18, Lyman and 27 other current and former military personnel eagerly await a court decision at a landmark trial in Arlington, Virginia, which will decide if the lawsuit being filed against former Defense Secretary Robert Gates and his predecessor, Donald Rumsfeld, will go to trial.
The lawsuit alleges Gates and Rumsfeld failed to reduce widespread rape among the military, in violation of soldiers’ constitutional rights.
Filed in February by Susan Burke, an attorney from Washington, D.C., the lawsuit initially implicated 17 plaintiffs, but grew to 28 in the following months, 25 women and three men, all of whom claim they were raped or sexually assaulted by their peers, and that the military failed to investigate, prosecute, or deliver adequate justice after the alleged assaults.
Burke says he's going after the army's major plane, because the problem starts with them.
“The army is a top-down structure,” he says.
"Which people are in a position to eradicate the culture of reprisals? The high command."
Burke also says that since February, about 400 more survivors have contacted her, many of whom could be part of future lawsuits.
His strategy: Instead of accumulating all the plaintiffs in this case, putting all the eggs in the same basket, so to speak, it will present several cases, if necessary, to maintain the pressure.
The defense team for the Department of Defense, led by U.S. Attorney Neil MacBride, has filed a motion to dismiss the case.
The court’s documents, obtained by Newsweek, detail the defense strategy – in essence, that the military cannot be sued by current or former soldiers for injuries sustained in the armed forces.
For a 1950 Supreme Court ruling called the Feres Doctrine, the federal government is not liable for damages suffered by active-duty personnel.
“The alleged damages are part of the plaintiffs’ military service,” the documents say.
The Department of Defense declined to comment for this article.
Things have changed since the 50's.
While women are still officially forbidden to serve in combat, this distinction is only nominal: Modern wars are rarely clear the front lines.
In both Iraq and Afghanistan, teams of female soldiers are playing increasingly important roles, especially to reach local women in war zones.
The result: between those two wars, 150 female soldiers have died and two-thirds of them in combat situations.
It's time for the army to modernise too, Burke says.
She’s not alone in her struggle, and three other lawsuits are underway besides hers.
At Yale Law School, the Veterans Legal Services Clinic is preparing a case against the four major military academies for allegedly promoting a misogynistic environment.
Moreover, the non-profit organization Vietnam Veterans of America is composing a lawsuit against the military that focuses on "personality disorder" diagnoses assigned to rape victims in order to discharge them from service - a common event, according to activists, such as Anu Bhagwati of the Women in Service Action Network, a human rights group.
His group filed a lawsuit in December against the Veterans Administration, accusing it of discriminatory practices in how it handles benefit claims for people who say they’ve been sexually assaulted in the service.
Nine separate bills have been introduced in Congress by a bipartisan mix of senators and representatives, proposing a series of corrections.
In 2010 there were 3,158 reported sexual assaults, according to the Department of Defense’s Office for the Prevention of Sexual Assault and Response.
However, the assaults are remarkably under-reported, and by the Pentagon’s own estimates, which figures at about 13.5 percent of the estimated 19000 incidents that occurred that year.
The report, published in March, also examines processing rates: in 2010, 20 percent of cases reported in the military went to trial – half that in the civil justice system.
The plaintiffs in Burke’s case describe his attacks as only the first in a series of traumas.
In Lyman’s case, he denounced the alleged violation of military police less than an hour after it occurred.
That night, he says, he was asked to recount the details of his rape 11 times other than a crowd of police, doctors, and commanders.
She underwent a rape kit, and the blood of her assailant — from a cut to her arm — was found in her bed.
Six months later, in April 2009, Lyman attended a hearing before a judge, during which she was asked that she had had sex with her boyfriend on the day of the attack.
Six people testified as personality witnesses on behalf of the alleged perpetrator, who was eventually acquitted.
“I remember the day the verdict was read,” Lyman says.
"I thought I'd go to work - I found myself screaming from the room."
"They put me in the psychiatry unit, and when I came out, I remember I told my command officer, "That wasn't their judgment. That was my judgment."
Lyman was ordered to visit a military psychiatrist, who eventually diagnosed him with personality disorder, which made her unsuitable for service.
In January 2010 it was dispensed from the service on less-than-honourable terms, preventing it from receiving any benefits.
As to whether the case filed by Lyman and his suitmates continues, it’s a very remote possibility, says John Turley, a law professor at George Washington Law School.
However, he notes, “In my opinion, they should be able to move forward.”
What stands between them and a verdict is this doctrine that has been criticised since it was first published.
I've been a permanent criter of the Feres Doctrine.
The military is decades later because it doesn’t have the same incentive and deterrence it raises responsibility.
“Odds are strongly against them ... but it’s important for them to try.”
These things only change when good people are willing to struggle.
If the hearing doesn’t go her way, Burke, the plaintiff’s lawyer, will queue her other hundreds of plaintiffs for future cases.
“We’re going to continue to fight the military on this issue until the reform occurs,” he says.
"Or until we die, whatever happens first.
Walls Have Eyes: How Researchers Are Studying You on Facebook
Before becoming the far-right’s new face in Europe, Anders Behring Breivik was simply another guy who aired his anti-immigration views online.
On Monday, Breivik, who admitted to carrying out a massacre in Norway in July, which left 77 dead, faced his first public court hearing.
While Breivik may have acted alone, he was far from being alone in cyberspace: He had spent most of the time that preceded his attack in front of his computer, chatting with some of the millions of nationalists supporting right-wing groups on social networks.
After this summer’s tragedy, investigators wanted to know more about these people.
But how to find them?
Easily - you just have to get into Facebook.
“We realized that it wasn’t so difficult to reach them,” says Jamie Bartlett, lead author of a recently published report on European digital populism by Britain’s DEMOS think tank.
Facebook’s cache of personal information is so encyclopedic, Bartlett says, that researchers could simply use the advertising tool to determine its desired demographic characteristics with scientific accuracy — the way marketers have been using for years.
Bartlett’s team found half a million fans of right-wing groups across Europe and then led them ads, but instead of linking them to a new band or diet product, the ads invited users to complete a survey asking about their educational level, attitudes toward violence and optimism about their own future.
Arriving through Facebook, Bartlett and his colleagues were able to study more than 10,000 followers of 14 far-right parties in 11 European countries – all without leaving the office.
"It's an all-new way to do an investigation," Bartlett says.
Some of the results aren’t quite surprising: Online supporters of right-wing groups tend to be young, men, and angry about immigration.
“Those who combine their activism online with offline activism are more reasonable, more democratic, and less violent than those left behind the computer screen.”
DEMOS’ work is just an example of how Facebook is becoming an important new tool in the hands of scientists.
Research centers, medical researchers and political scientists are using the site to study everything from health issues to social trends as expressed in “Likes,” wall messages and status updates.
With more than 800 million active users adding an average of three pieces of content a day, Facebook’s “data supernova” is generating a research boom, driving the number of scholarly jobs with the site’s name on the title to nearly 800% over the past five years.
For some researchers, the beauty of Facebook is that it allows you to study people you couldn’t approach with a folder on the street.
"Before, when you were trying to survey people from the British National Party, it was very difficult to identify them."
“You’d have to go through the game, and they’d never allow you access,” Bartlett says.
“Facebook removes all of that – you go to them directly.”
Others say the site could provide a way to identify and address health-social issues.
A recent study conducted by Dr. Megan Moreno of the University of Wisconsin-Madison and her colleagues found that college students who talked about their drunken exploits on Facebook had a significantly higher risk of drinking problems than students who remained silent about the issue.
Moreno suggests that student classmates, such as residential counselors, could control the site and intervene to help a student posting too much status updates with their drunkenness.
"You can't treat a problem if you can't diagnose it," said Dimitri Christakis, Moreno's co-author and director of the Center for Children's Health, Behavioring and Development at the Seattle Children's Research Institute.
"We have found a way to identify children at risk who would not otherwise be diagnosed."
Facebook is also constantly “puncturing” its own population to obtain data.
The site’s Data Team has compiled statistics on the status of relationship and Valentine’s Day, the expectation of mid-legislation voter turnover in 2010, and a National Happiness Index (mainly using the information that can be accessed—defenders of digital freedom long ago who argue that the site should make available to others its large amount of data for the investigation).
However, Facebook users know that the site is keeping an eye on them, whether they like it or not – the sacrifice of being able to play Scrabble with a friend thousands of miles away is allowing the site to mine your personal information.
But what happens when Facebook users become part of a survey they don’t even know?
While researchers have well-defined guidelines on how to collect their data offline, online it’s an outbar for everyone.
"I don't think a lot of users have even thought about the fact that a researcher might be examining their profiles," says Neil Selwyn, a sociologist at the London Knowledge Laboratory, about a 2006 study he conducted with Facebook's public walls of students.
“As far as they were concerned, it was only between them and their friends.”
Then there’s the question of methodology.
Even offline, there’s no guarantee that a research topic is being completely honest.
On Facebook, it’s impossible to know that some of a user’s profile information and wall messages are true.
“What you’re saying on Facebook and what you do outside of Facebook are two completely different things,” Selwyn says.
That’s why, despite his years as a technology researcher, Selwyn believes a paper clip and pen are still the best research tools anyone can use.
“There’s no substitute for getting into the real world and talking to real people,” he says.
"Social research is supposed to be about the social - and a lot of the social still happens offline."
"Cold and Inhumane: Anders Behring Breivik Makes His First Public Appearance in Court
Anders Behring Breivik, the man who confessed to being the mastermind of the two attacks that killed 77 people in Norway this summer, has described himself as a "resistance" fighter during his first public appearance in Oslo court.
"I am a military commander in the Norwegian resistance movement and in the Knights Templars of Norway," he told a room full of 500 people.
"I oppose the court as it was mandated by organizations that support the ideology of hate and because it supports multiculturalism."
"I acknowledge the facts but do not plead guilty."
Judge Torkjel Nesheim interrupted Breivik during that monologue, because "he didn't want to give Breivik the opportunity to use this hearing as a platform to express his views."
For the same reason he refused to allow Breivik, who brought a written speech in advance, to address the families of his victims at the end of the hearing.
Nesheim also ordered police to keep Breivik in custody for an additional 12 weeks, banned him from accessing the media for four weeks, and said authorities would have to have strict control over all visits and correspondence for eight weeks.
About 30 survivors and relatives of the victims attended the hearing.
Some came hoping to look Breivik in the eye, others to confirm that he is locked up.
Everyone attended with the aim of moving towards the conclusion.
"I thought it looked cold and inhumane," a Utoya survivor told Norwegian broadcaster NRK.
"It was awkward, but for me it was a step forward after seeing and hearing the suspect."
Dressed in a dark suit and blue tie, Breivik remained calm and professional throughout the audience and looked journalists and survivors in the eyes as he walked in and out of the building.
Despite Breivik's strange notion of being part of a major "resistance" movement, the judge determined that he is not mentally ill, and said there is no evidence that he acted with accomplices.
It is possible that some are comforted that the attacks of July 22 appear to be the work of an extremist instead of a group of radicals.
But that doesn’t automatically soften the pain and confusion that an entire nation still feels.
"I'd like it looked like a monster, but it doesn't look like it," said a relative of a victim.
“It would be a lot easier if it looked.”
7 Lessons from Steve Jobs' Lost Interview, Shown in Theater This Week
After the death of Steve Jobs on 5 October, there were some who compared him to Henry Ford - distinguishing the tech genius as the Great Modern Inventor.
But what Apple fans might find more revealing, in a recently rediscovered interview with Jobs that has been dusted as a preview of a series of special projections in the biggest markets this week, is how much this creative mind understood about business processes and product workflows.
He was certainly a self-recognized designer, dreamer and hippie, but he was also a meticulous organizer and a yonki of flowcharts - a man who believed that many executives suffered "a disease consisting of thinking that a great idea is 90 percent of the work ... [but] there is an enormous amount of work between a great idea and a great product."
In 1995, Bob Cringely was developing the television series The Triumph of the Nerds about the dawn of the personal computer, and sat down for more than an hour with Jobs for an extensive and rare conversation.
At the time it had been barely 10 years since Jobs had been forced away from Apple, and already worked intensively in his new computer company NeXT, waiting for the popular adoption of the Internet.
While a small portion of Cringely's interview was used in Nerds, he said the interview originals disappeared during their transportation.
It was only after Jobs’ death that a full copy was discovered on VHS in the director’s garage.
This slightly republished archival is the basis of Steve Jobs: The 68-minute lost interview coming to Landmark theaters on Wednesday and Thursday.
For someone known for hating in-depth interviews, Jobs here seems surprisingly willing to expose their technological philosophies and business strategies.
And very thoughtful.
In four different points throughout the interview, there are great silences between the questions and the answers – from 10 to 15 seconds of reflections, where Jobs is clearly weighing his thoughts, looking for precision.
There are also times when Jobs’ perfectionism comes to the surface, where it seems impatient due to Cringely’s questions, prompting him to go more quickly towards the most important issues or arguments.
Filmed before the era of Pixar, iPod or iPhone, there’s something strangely prophetic in The Lost Interview, when Jobs assesses what he sees other companies do right and wrong, and offers his own vision of computing’s future.
It’s one thing to read posthumous assessments of Jobs’ career, but there’s something raw and inspiring about the Jobs we see here, the anxious dreamer on the verge of greatness.
He has a vision of how things can and should be.
Not long after explaining everything in detail here, it was and did.
A quick summary of seven notable sequences of The Lost Interview:
On how you learned to run a business:
“Over the years in business, I always asked, ‘‘Why do you do things?’ and the answers you’re invariably given are, “Oh, that’s just the way it’s done.”
No one knows why he does what he does, in business nobody thinks about things very deeply.
That's what I found.
Jobs takes his efforts to simplify Apple’s accounting in detail.
Baffled by how costs were recorded - often starting with a "standard cost" that was made in the eye of a good bucket, and then adjusted with a "variable" - developed an automated factory that ensured that they could determine the commercial costs in detail.
About the promice to the Pope:
Jobs narrates the "Blue Box" he built with Steve Wozniak - a device that allowed people to effectively hack the phone company and make long-distance calls for free.
It has been written extensively about the device, but Jobs’ euphoria here in describing the Blue Box as an act of empowerment is contagious.
It describes how he and Wozniak tested the box by using a pay phone, making a call, and then connecting from one AT&T network to another, while creating a loop with as many satellites as possible.
“We were wrapping things around the world half a dozen times and yelling through the pay phone and going back a minute later to the pay phone next door,” Jobs says with a giggle.
“We were young and what we learned was that we could build something ourselves that could control billions of dollars in infrastructure around the world.”
The two of us didn’t know much, but we could build a little thing that could control a giant thing – it was an incredible lesson, and I don’t think it would ever have existed without it.
Josks continues to describe a glorious joke that he and Wozniak almost abandoned, calling the Vatican through the Blue Box in the middle of the night and requesting to talk to the Pope, while doing his best imitation of Henry Kissinger.
As several members of the Catholic hierarchy were summoned in the middle of the night to talk to the U.S. diplomat, the two erupted in laughters just before the pope himself was awakened to answer the phone.
About falling in love with technology (and selling by phone with Bill Hewlett):
At the age of 12, hunting for spare parts to build a frequency counter, he searched Bill Hewlett in the phone book and made a call.
Shortly after their 20-minute phone conversation, Jobs got a part-time summer job at Hewlett-Packard.
"That was a remarkable influence on me, it was the only company I'd ever seen at that age and formed my vision of what a company was and how well they treated their employees."
Jobs later became one of HP’s employees who visited the company’s research labs in Palo Alto, where he saw “the first table computer ever made.”
It was as big as a suitcase, had a small cathodic tube screen, and I fell in love with it.
She would make a trip to HP as a teenager and waste time with that machine and write programs for her.
About improvising innovation:
At various points in the interview, Jobs talks about inventing new products on the fly.
Soon in his career, as he dedicated himself to selling a handful of motherboards, he was asked by a client if he could assemble the entire team.
Working with only 30 days of credit, I had to find out the assembly and delivery of the finished devices.
Later, while he was at Apple, he recalls the fights he faced in the development of a computer mouse:
"I remember having dramatic arguments ... they yelled at me that it would take five years [to create a mouse that would cost 300 bucks], and I got fed and went out and found a designer.
Ninety days later, we had a mouse that we could build for 15 dollars and that was totally reliable.
About large companies losing strength:
Long before he led Apple’s rebirth, Jobs prophetically envisioned how many industry leaders would stumble on his vision, and lose control of their market share.
"Say you're working at IBM or Xerox, so you make better photocopiers and printers, so what?"
You have a monopoly on market share, so the company is not more successful.
Sales and marketing departments increase success, so it is these kinds of people who end up running the companies and the people in charge of the products are expelled from the forum for decision-making.
The genius of the products that led to that position of monopoly rots because of people who do not have the notion of good products vs bad products - the knowledge of the necessary trade.
That's what happened at Xerox...
Xerox could have taken over the entire computer industry.
It could have been ten times its size, it could have been the Microsoft of the 90's...
They took the defeat of victory.
On innovation as an artistic expression:
“There’s an enormous amount of work between a great idea and a great product ... as the great idea evolves, it changes and grows.”
You learn a lot about the subtleties of it.
There are sacrifices that you have to make - some things that you can't do that make the electrons, the glass makes them, the robots make them, the factories make them.
You have to keep 5000 things in your brain - these concepts - adjust them all at once...
In the end everything comes down to taste - it's reduced to trying to expose yourself to the best that a team of humans has done, and then trying to adapt those things you're doing.
Picasque said that good artists copy, great artists steal.
We’ve never been embarrassed about stealing big ideas.
What partly made the Macintosh great was that the people who were working on it were musicians, poets, artists, zoologists, historians, who were coincidentally the best computer scientists in the world.
If it weren’t for computing, I could be doing wonderful things in other fields.
Scalia and Thomas dine with health care law rivals as the court accepts the case
The day the Supreme Court met behind closed doors to examine the politically controversial question of whether they would accept the defiance of President Obama’s health care law, two of its judges, Antonin Scalia and Clarence Thomas, were entertained at a dinner sponsored by the law firm that will expose the case before the high court.
The occasion was last Thursday, when the nine judges met at a preliminary hearing to carefully study the requests for review.
One of the cases in question was a lawsuit filed by 26 states defiing total health care reform passed by Congress last year, a law that has been a rallying cry for conservative activists across the country.
Magistrates agreed to adjoin the lawsuit and, in fact, a historical argument of 5 and a half hours is expected by March, and the result is likely to further muddy the 2012 presidential race, which will be in full swing at the moment when the court’s decision becomes known.
The lawyer who will appear in court and allege that the law must be dismissed will likely be Paul Clement, who served as U.S. attorney general during the George W. Bush administration.
Clement’s law firm, Bancroft PLLC, was one of nearly two dozen companies that helped sponsore the Federalist Society’s annual dinner, a group that has spent many years dedicated to defending conservative legal principles.
Another firm that sponsored the dinner, Jones Day, represents one of the trade associations that defied the law, the National Federation of Independent Business.
Another sponsor was pharmaceutical giant Pfizer Inc., which has an enormous financial interest in the outcome of the litigation.
The dinner was held in a Washington hotel, hours after the court’s preliminary hearing on the case.
Among attendees were, among others, Mitch McConnell, the most important Republican in the Senate and an outspoken opponent of the health care law.
Scalia and Thomas special guests at the dinner.
It’s nothing new: The two judges have been attending Federalist Society events for years.
And it’s not something that conflicts with ethical norms.
In fact, the judges are exempt from the Code of Conduct that governs the actions of lower federal magistrates.
If they weren’t, they would arguably fall under the Canon 4C Code, which reads: “A judge may attend events to raise funds from law-related organizations and others, although the judge may not be a speaker, a guest of honor, or appear in the program of such an event.”
However, Scalia and Thomas’ absolute proximity to two of the law firms in the case, as well as to a company with enormous financial interest, was enough to alarm ethics activists in the government.
"This impressive gap in ethic and indifference to the code goes against the lawsuits of several magistrates who abide by the court for the same rules that apply to all federal judges," said Bob Edgar, president of Common Cause.
"The judges were entertained in a gala fundraiser with attorneys who have cases pending in court."
Its emergence and fundraising attendance for this event undermines any demand for impartiality, and is unacceptable.
Scalia and Thomas have shown little interest in criticism that they say too easily mix the court’s activity with the agenda pushed by groups like the Federalist Society.
And Thomas’ wife, Ginni, is a leading conservative activist.
On the other hand, the conservatives argue that it is Judge Elena Kagan, who has an ethical problem, not Scalia and Thomas.
Kagan worked as Attorney General of the Obama administration when the first legal challenges to the law were presented in trial court.
His critics have lobbied for Kagan to refrain from seeing the case, saying she was too involved in upholding the law to now be impartial.
Kagan hasn't given any indication that he's going to do it.
Boeing Receives Record Order of 18 Billion Dollars
The airline Emirates asks for 50 Boeing 777 double aisle aircraft, with an option for 20 more.
Boeing also sells six Boeing Dreamliner aircraft to Oman Air, an operation valued at more than a billion dollars.
Rarely is news when a company gets an order of 50 units of its product.
But it’s a big transaction when the company is aircraft maker Boeing Co. and the selling price for all 50 planes amounts to a record US$18 billion.
It’s as large as the place where Boeing received the order: the Middle East, a growing gold mine of future aircraft orders.
In a study unveiled Monday, the Chicago company estimates that over the next 20 years airlines in the Middle East will need 2,520 aircraft worth 450 billion dollars.
Boeing announced over the weekend its largest order in history for commercial planes for their dollar value - 50 double-aisle Boing 777 jets.
The successful order came from Emirates in Dubai.
The airline also has an option to buy 20 more aircraft, which would raise the total selling price to US$26 billion, Boeing announced at the 2011 Dubai Air Show.
Then, on Monday, Boeing said Oman Air had ordered six Boeing 787-8s, the so-called Dreamliner, which boasts fuel economy and spectacular passenger services.
The selling price for six of them is more than a billion dollars.
"This clearly has an enormous positive effect for Boeing," Morningstar analyst Neal Dihora said.
"The quality of these particular customers is very strong."
Many Middle Eastern airlines are owned by their oil-producing nations.
Therefore, not only do they have the money to pay planes, but an increase in fuel prices – usually detrimental to airlines, because it is one of the biggest costs – it’s not so detrimental, because the country is making money from rising oil prices.
That’s important because they’re less likely than other airlines to cancel or delay flight orders when fuel prices rise, Dihora said.
"They have natural protection," he said.
The UAE’s order solidifies Boeing’s leadership in the wide-body aircraft market and helps keep its Airbus SAS competitor’s attempt to invade its dominance with the A350 passenger aircraft.
Boeing’s victory at the air show comes four months after Airbus said it would delay the debut of the largest A350, which competes directly with the 777-300ER.
He delayed the debut to add more momentum after customers demanded an increase in payload and range.
Boeing Co shares rose 1.5% to US$67.94.
The sister of the president of Mexico, apparently defeated in Michoacán's vote
Luisa María Calderón claims that drug traffickers helped her PRI opponent, Fausto Vallejo, in the governor’s contempt.
President Felipe Calderón’s sister appears to have lost her candidacy for governor of Michoacán during the violent state’s elections, and she claimed Monday that drug traffickers helped tilt the race in favor of one of her opponents.
The preliminary results gave the lead in the race for governor of the Western state to Fausto Vallejo, of the Institutional Revolutionary Party, or PRI.
"The intervention of organized crime throughout the electoral process and, especially yesterday, is alarming, not only for Michoacán, but for the whole country," Luisa María Calderón said in a radio interview a day after Sunday's vote.
"They threatened our candidates, our electoral workers . . . they seized the polls, set up barricades ... and ordered people to vote" in favor of the PRI.
The PRI ruled Mexico for seven decades until it lost its presidency in 2000.
However, it’s the staging of a comeback, and victory in Michoacán is an important step in that effort.
The PRI is hoping to win the presidential election in July.
Vallejo seems to have narrowly outpaced Calderón, who had led the polls before the election.
Calderón, a candidate of his brother’s conservative National Action Party, or PAN, refused to recognize Vallejo as the winner.
Silvano Aureoles, the left-wing candidate for the Democratic Revolution Party, or PRD, who currently holds the position of governor, came in third, a great defeat for the divided and unlucky left before the 2012 presidential elections.
Aureoles also declined to acknowledge the preliminary results.
Vallejo, former mayor of the capital of the state of Morelia, denied links to drug trafficking and urged the other candidates to accept the results.
Michoacán has long been dominated by drug cartels specializing in marijuana, heroin and methamphetamines.
It is President Calderón’s home state, and he chose Michoacán to launch a military offensive targeting traffickers in December 2006.
But the violence has persisted.
A PAN mayor was killed a week before the election while campaigning for Luisa María Calderón, and numerous candidates abandoned local candidacies out of fear.
Although for the PRI, no matter how unpleasant Michoacán’s elections may seem, a victory will help boost the party.
The man who is supposed to represent the PRI in the presidential election, Enrique Peña Nieto, speaking from Washington, congratulated Vallejo and said: "I think this victory should be very encouraging, facing next year."
The BAsD urges Asia to help rescue the eurozone
The Asian Development Bank (BAsD) has called on India and China to prepare to help rescue the eurozone from its sovereign debt crisis to avoid a long-term recession that would impede the growth of Asian economies.
Rajat Nag, director-general of the Manila-based Asian Development Bank, said the world's two fastest-growing major economies had to "do everything they can" to accelerate the recovery of the economic bloc either through the International Monetary Fund or directly through bilateral agreements.
He warned against the BRIC [Brazil, Russia, India, China] countries that look at Europe’s difficulties in an “impartial” way and said Asia’s financial assistance coupled with European leadership and resources would help avoid a long-term crisis in the global economy.
“We’re all together on this.”
“So anyone who can help Europe get out of the crisis is helpful,” he told the Financial Times in an interview at the World Economic Forum in Mumbai.
“Asia can be protected to some extent, but it can’t be immune.”
“So if China and India can help, let it be by all means.”
The European monetary union has been severely undermined by a sovereign debt crisis in recent months.
In recent days, Italy’s borrowing costs have skyrocketed dangerously and Prime Ministers of Italy and Greece have resigned.
Europe’s ongoing sovereign debt crisis has raised fears that it could push the global economy into recession and prompted calls for major emerging economies to come together to help the eurozone find a solution.
Mr Nag predicted that any support from Asia would be channeled through the IMF, but said that bilateral assistance – such as the purchase of bonds from the EU bailout fund, the European Financial Stability Fund – offered greater bargaining power for Europe’s Asian partners.
Anand Sharma, Indian trade minister, said "India is going to do everything possible" to help the eurozone as its own economy was now suffering from falling exports and depleting foreign capital inflows.
"No one wants the eurozone to remain unstable and turbulent," he said.
“We have monumental challenges and we have to maintain a high level of growth.”
"It's not an option, it's an imperative, because where do we find employment for tens of millions of our young men and women."
Others argue that developing economies such as India have no responsibility in helping rich Europeans when faced with their own deep economic problems.
Ashutosh Varshney, a professor at Brown University in the U.S., said it would be politically very difficult to sell aid to Europe for India’s one billion two hundred thousand people, of which up to 800 million live with about 2 dollars a day or less.
"At some point they'll realize that the Greeks retire at the age of 50 and go on vacation to the beaches and that won't leave well," he said.
Lee Howell, managing director of the World Economic Forum, also wondered why India’s reserves should be used to keep the many well-paying Greek public sector workers in jobs from poorly-managed companies, and drivers of losses such as railways.
Mr. Nag said that the eurozone crisis threatens "significant chain effects" across Asia.
The Asian Development Bank’s forecast of 7.5 percent economic growth in Asia for 2011/12 is now facing “downside risks” due to Europe’s threat.
He said that vulnerable emerging markets needed to make "contingency plans" to protect themselves from a crisis and significant capital outflows from their economies.
Serious Pentagon warnings about possible defense cuts
Defense Secretary Leon Panetta warmed Congress on Monday, warning that looming automatic budget cuts could undermine national security and provoke a chain financial backlash from the Pentagon’s aisles, to Afghanistan’s battlefields, to civilian assembly lines.
The Pentagon is already digesting US$450 billion reductions over the next decade, but it now fears an additional reduction of 600 billion or more in cuts that may be imminent if Congress fails to reach an agreement on spending.
"The impact of these cuts would be devastating for the department," Panetta said in a letter to Senators John Camegin, R-Arizona, and Lindsey Graham.
He said the failure of Congress to reach an agreement on the budget and its consequence, the so-called “kidnapping,” would lead to 23 percent of global reductions and a halt to many new projects.
“Such a large cut, implemented in this indiscriminate way, would mean that most of our naval and construction projects cannot be executed – you can’t buy three-quarters of a ship or a building – and seriously damage other modernization efforts,” Panetta wrote.
"We would also be forced to remove many of our civil servants involuntarily and, because the reduction would prevail so quickly, it is almost certain that we would have to license civilians in order to meet the goal."
"These changes would break the faith with those who maintain our army and seriously harm good disposition."
Cuts over time would reach combat troops, Panetta said.
“While wartime funding in overseas contingency operations accounts is not directly affected by kidnapping, war efforts would be negatively impacted by serious disruptions in grassroots budgets,” Panetta warned.
"The recruitment of personnel would be reduced, which would lead to delays in contracts and the supervision of the contract supporting the war."
"Staff on payroll would be reduced, leading to delays in payments to suppliers in wartime, and legal and political support would be interrupted."
The two senators had written to Panetta 10 days ago, asking for information about the possible impact of automatic cuts in the Department of Defense.
“The consequence of an abduction at the Department of Defense would lead to a rapid decline of the United States as the world’s top military power.”
“We stand firmly against this draconian action,” the senators said in a joint statement Monday afternoon when they released Panetta’s letter.
"This is not a result with which we can live, and it is certainly not one that we must impose on ourselves."
“Abduction is a threat to U.S. national security interests, and it should not be allowed to occur.”
If Panetta’s letter and recent warnings from senators will increase pressure for a budget compromise or intensify calls to exempt the Pentagon from the cuts remains to be seen.
Panetta has been increasingly frank about possible cuts, even though he came to work at the top of the Pentagon with years of experience in budgets in Congress and the White House and knowing he was facing difficult decisions.
At a press conference last week, the defense secretary painted a bleak picture of what might be ahead – an army with a shell, but not a core.
“It’s a ship without sailors.”
"It's a brigade without bullets."
"It's an aerial wing without enough trained pilots."
"It's a paper tiger, an army of barracks, buildings, and bombs without enough soldiers trained to fulfill the mission," Panetta said in his opening remarks at the Pentagon.
"It's a force that suffers from low morale, poor preparation, and it's not able to keep up with potential adversaries."
"Indeed, it invites aggression..."
In an appendix to his letters to McCain and Graham, Panetta states new details of how the reductions "generate significant operational risks: delay in response time to crises, conflicts and disasters; severely limits our ability to be deployed and previously compromised around the world; and poses an unacceptable risk in future combat operations."
And Panetta said that some of the biggest defense projects could tackle the axe, including those that are already being tested and some only in the early stages of planning.
This list includes the F35 Joint Strike Fighter, a planned new bomber, the next-generation ballistic submarine, the new littoral fighter ship and the new ground-based fighting vehicle that the Army and Navy need to replace, the humvee.
Stopping the development and further testing of the F35 could generate some US$80 billion in savings over 10 years, but supporters say it’s the next vital step to improve and cope with potential threats from China and other rivals.
How to detect a lie
A look at the most recent headlines tells us how serious and ubiquitous deceit and lying are in daily life.
Republican presidential candidate Herman Cain is busy exchanging allegations of sexual harassment with several women; each party accuses each other of lying.
Pennsylvania State University administrators have been accused of perjury for allegedly concealing reports saying that a retired soccer coach was sexually assaulting children.
Last week, French President Nicolas Sarkozy was "caught" with an open mic phone claiming U.S. President Barack Obama that Israeli Prime Minister Benjamin Netanyahu is a liar.
Lying has destroyed races and convulsed countries.
New York Congressman and online exhibitionist Anthony Weiner made a ridicule with his denials quickly rebuked for irrefutable evidence.
Former presidential candidate John Edwards has been accused of campaign-financing violations related to the cover-up of an extramarital relationship.
And again, no one who lived it will ever forget the media circus unleashed by President Bill Clinton by lying during his second term about his sexual relationship with Monica Lewinsky.
Legends about cheating in school and college exams are widespread.
There have been cases where teachers have given exam responses to students in order to be best seen in their performance assessments.
Mentors who should be teaching otherwise are sending the message that lying and deceiving are acceptable.
How much deceit do we find?
On any given day, studies show you can lie anywhere 10 to 200 times.
Surely, many of those lies are pious lies.
Another study showed that strangers lied three times in the first 10 minutes of meeting.
Detecting lies, or “locating lies,” is an essential skill that we all must acquire, for both personal and professional reasons.
Far from being a board game similar to, for example, Charada, where the goal is to exclaim: "I have you", the detection of deception is a branch of serious knowledge based on scientific data obtained during the last six decades in prestigious universities that carried out in-depth research projects, especially in psychology and physiology.
One of the results of the research is that the old myths about lying have been rebutted.
Liars do look into their eyes.
They don’t always stutter, babbling, blushing or restless.
It cannot be concluded that liars are difficult to detect or unmask.
A trained lie detector can come to the truth by learning about the structure of the statement, microfacial expressions, question formation, and times.
I spent several years surveying scientific discoveries about deceit in the vast and ever-emerging body of knowledge, and it was clear that deception detection is a modern technique that is easy to learn and helps navigate our complex world – especially if your professional responsibilities include hiring, interviews, or managing negotiations.
Good liars have the ability to read well to others, making them feel at ease, handling their own emotions and intuitively feeling how others perceive them.
We know from the investigations that extroverts lie more than introverts, that men say more “self-oriented lies,” while women say more “other-facing” lies—normally to protect someone’s feelings—that married people lie less often to their partners than people who are unmarried (but lies they tend to be “huge”).
We also know that if you are perceived as an evildoer, others will feel less guilty in lying to you.
How do you know if someone is lying?
First, look at the subject’s usual behavior.
This is called “creating a baseline.”
It helps to provide benchmarks to then measure changes.
Look at the subject’s posture, laughter, and vocal characteristic.
It's better to know if someone normally hits the foot all the time so they don't make unfair accusations when they see their feet pounding in the middle of the meeting.
Next, look for deceptive, verbal and non-verbal behavioral groups.
Consider these behavioral groups as warning signs, not as proof of deception.
Deceiving people can freeze their upper body by trying to remember their history, they can point their feet at the door, lean toward an exit, change their posture significantly, or exhibit a “post-interview relief” — that exaggerated sigh of relief and change of posture once all the difficult questions are over.
Often interrogators falsely show that the interview has ended just to look for that post-intervist relief.
It also pays attention to the subject’s vocabulary.
Scott Peterson screwed to the bottom and used the past while claiming that his murdered wife was alive, throwing a nationwide quest for her.
Deceiving people may also use distance language: "I didn't have sex with that woman... Miss Lewinsky" or repeat a difficult question entirely.
The most common verbal indicators are subtle.
Someone can use a bunch of "restrictive language" when they answer a difficult question: "Well... to tell the truth... to what I understand..."
This makes the answer a perception rather than a fact and is often an alert signal.
There is no magic formula for detecting lies, but developing skills to uncover deceit is possible.
These skills will improve anyone’s chances to avoid being victimized by scam artists in their professional and personal lives.
Controversial pipeline plan diverted after U.S. delay threat in approval
Days after the Obama administration threatened to delay the approval of a planned pipeline from Canada to the Gulf of Mexico — angering unions, while appeasing environmentalists — the company looking to build the pipeline says it’s willing to redirect the project to get it back on track.
TransCanada said Monday night that it will move the planned pipeline out of Sandhills’ ecologically sensitive area in Nebraska, and is confident the project will gain approval.
The company announced the decision at the press conference at the Nebraska Capitol.
TransCanada executive Alex Pourbax says the company maintains its confidence that over time the pipeline will be approved, although it is true that with a different route.
This follows last week’s announcement by the State Department that they would delay the decision over a federal permit for the project until new potential routes to avoid the Sandhills areas of Nebraska and the Ogallala aquifer, a vast supply of groundwater, are being studied.
"This is a real avenue of progress," Rep. Lee Terry of Nebraska told Fox News.
The State Department’s new environmental overhaul is possibly split any decision until 2013, after next year’s election, though officials denied that the policy was involved in the decision.
Obama, in a written statement last week, described the decision as the State Department’s request, and voiced their full support.
The 1,700-kilometer pipeline would extend from Canada to the Gulf of Mexico.
It would take 700,000 barrels per day from the province of Alberta to refineries in Texas.
For this, it would be necessary to cross six states.
It is hated by environmentalists, but loved by working groups, who have opted for an estimate of 20,000 jobs linked to the pipeline.
But Nebraska legislators opposed to the plan are weighing legislation to force a departure from the Sandhills region and the Ogallala aquifer, the largest source of drinking water and irrigation.
The State Department’s current environmental impact study found that the project only posed limited adverse environmental impacts, but the energy industry source said the department’s inspector general has ordered independent testing of the review process, focusing on two issues.
One is if TransCanada-hired lobby member Paul Elliot, who was Hillary Clinton’s campaign adviser in 2008, represents a conflict of interest for the program.
The other is whether a firm that was hired to run the original studio was an inappropriate choice because it was linked to TransCanada.
Patti LaBelle sued for allegedly causing a child to vomit out of fear
A New York family claims the diva of disco music, Patti LaBelle, behaved like a madwoman in the lobby of her Manhattan building, terrifying her young daughter to the point that she vomited in fear.
"Lady Marmalade's "Lady Marmalade" diatribe scared 18-month-old Genevieve Monk so much that she suffered "personality changes, sleep disorders" and "increased her fear of strangers," her family says in Manhattan Supreme Court.
It was November 10 last year when leggist Kevin Monk, his wife, Roseanna, a kindergarten teacher, and Genevieve were preparing for a family trip.
As Kevin Monk took the car, Roseanna Monk stepped down into the lobby of the Riverside Boulevard building with her luggage, carrying Genevieve.
Roseanna Monk said she stood near the door and put her daughter on the floor for a moment.
"Someone came up from behind and said, 'You know what your daughter's doing?' I said, 'Yes,' and I went to catch her," he said.
LaBelle admonished the pregnant woman.
“He said in an aggressive tone, ‘I shouldn’t have left your daughter near the door,’ Roseanna Monk said.
"I said, 'I have no interest in what you say or think."
He was enraged and began to use bad words - word C and word J aloud...
He had a bottle of water and he started throwing the water out of the bottle over me."
He said Genevieve became hysterical.
"I told this woman, "Look what you've done to my daughter," the girl's mother said.
LaBelle pounced on the mother -- who was still holding Genevieve -- and had to be restrained by her surroundings and retired to a car waiting for her, Roseanna Monk said.
The little girl was crying so loud that she vomited, she added.
Later, the family sent a note asking for an apology from LaBelle, but received no response.
LaBelle’s lawyer and representatives did not return the calls to comment on what happened.
The lawsuit seeks unspecified damages, but the family’s lawyer, Sam Davis, said he took the case for free and that the family plans to donate all the money it gets to a support association for children with cancer.
"The purpose is to hold Patti LaBelle responsible for her conduct," Davis said.
"That type of behavior is completely unacceptable, especially when addressing a kindergarten teacher holding an 18-month-old boy in her arms."
Climatic Adversity Slowered the Economy
During Mario López Valdez’s first year’s management, unemployment and informality have risened in Sinaloa, lowered foreign investment and the economy contracted
2011 is a year that will be remembered in the economic history of Sinaloa, it is the first year of government of Mario López Valdez, self-described of change, which generated great expectations regarding the advances in the economy, but given the climate adversity at the beginning of the year, there is no growth, instead there is a deterioration in the main indicators of economic development.
The low temperatures of February, in addition to causing millionaire losses for the agricultural sector, limited the possibilities of the state economy to grow, caused a contraction of overall economic activity of 3.6 percent in the first half of the year, justified mainly by the historic fall of 31.16 percent in agriculture, which affected the dynamism of the other economic sectors.
According to the report of effects by the frosts, issued by the Mexican Council for Sustainable Rural Development, 582 thousand 835 cultivated hectares were damaged, of which 78.03 percent suffered total damage and the remaining 21.07 percent had partial damage, the main crops lost were those of vegetables, corn and chickpeas.
The fall in economic activity has generated, until November, a constant increase in unemployment in the state, according to the National Institute of Statistics and Geography the rate of state unemployment during the first three quarters of the year accelerated growth was observed, since from January to March 55 thousand 053 Sinaloans were unoccupied, with a rate of 4.53 percent of the Economically Active Population, for the second quarter the rate rose to be 5.28 percent
Lower liquidity
Unemployment in Sinaloa during the year is multifactorial, for the economist Gerardo López Cervantes, director of the Faculty of Economic and Social Sciences of the UAS, the growth of unemployment can be explained indirectly, depending on the actions that have been carried out since public policy.
"What you're seeing is an unemployment growth that has to do with several factors, including the loss of liquidity in the market due to the effects of a restriction of resources by the legislation that was approved in Congress to combat money laundering, already for people who illegally seize dollars it is not easy for them to work, invest and use them in something," he said.
Detained migration
López Cervantes pointed out that in the increase in unemployment there is also a demographic factor.
"The number of people leaving the state in search of work abroad, in the U.S. has been restricted, first due to the difficulties to enter, and second because entering they already already have many difficulties to find work, those people are staying and are demanding sources of work here in the state, which causes unemployment to rise," he said.
Informality
The analysis of the Sinaloan labor market is aggravated by considering the high level of informality that predominates between working conditions, according to INEGI, 60.38 percent of the employed population in the state works without being contributing as a worker before any social security institution.
In addition to unemployment and informality, another of the state’s labor lags are low wages, the entity has the lowest remunerations in the country, according to the records of the Mexican Institute of Social Security and the Ministry of Labor and Social Security.
The average monthly income of Sinaloan workers was, until September, 5 thousand 352 pesos, well below the national average, located at 7 thousand 375 pesos.
IED Contraction
In addition to the fall in economic activity and the increase in unemployment, during 2011 there is the largest decrease in Foreign Direct Investment in the last 10 years, according to the Ministry of Economy from January to June of the state FDI added 630 thousand 697 dollars, which represents a fall of 87.74 percent compared to 5 million 143 thousand 312 dollars registered during the same period of the previous year.
For its part, the Secretariat of Economic Development of the State Government counts a total investment of 17 thousand 280 million pesos in the year, which occurred mainly from companies in the tertiary sector.
The Debt
Faced with the scenario of economic contraction, the State Government requested in July to the state congress the approval of a credit for 3 billion pesos, which would be destined the investment in infrastructure projects in the 18 municipalities, in order to reactivate the economy, the approval of the local congress was for an amount of 2 billion 600 million pesos, and until November they have not observed the benefits of indebtedness in any sector of the state economy.
Reactions
For representatives of the business sector, the first year of government of Mario López Valdés shows a lack of government management capacity.
Miguel Loredo López, president of the National Chamber of the Transformation Industry, said that during 2011 he lacked the harmonization of wills of the different sectors, including the State Government itself.
"The performance of the economy in Sinaloa during this year has really been very poor, we have to work intensively to recover the ground that we lost this year, in 2012 we have to redoublate efforts, we need to align the effort of everyone and above all the already synchronized good progress of this new government team, we hope that next year will be much better," he said.
Economic slowness
Loredo López said that events such as the frost at the beginning of the year stopped the state economy and restricted its performance and growth.
"The low economic flow is the product of several events, including frost, one of the main effects has been a sluggish economy that does not turn fast enough to generate an economic dynamism that allows us to have an acceptable level of flow in processes, products and services and therefore, at the end of the year we are closing well below the expectations we had," he said.
Redefining vocation
For Teodoro Salazar Uriarte, president of the Confederation of Employers of the Mexican Republic in Sinaloa, he said that it is necessary to prosecute the state towards productive activities of greater dynamism.
"In general terms we have observed that it has been working, we believe that it is necessary to develop a long-term program in which the vocation and economic direction of the state is redefined, because it is important that the full potential of Sinaloa's material and human resource is distorted," he said.
work to do
The president of Coparmex stressed the importance of establishing a vision of the future that better channels the efforts of the Government.
"In general terms I would say that the government has been striving to give good results in different areas, in terms of economic development there is some progress but it is important that a long-term program be developed," he said.
He pointed out that the current administration has shown progress in its way of conducting public policy.
"There are elements that allow us to say that the government is being more inclusive, what I can say is that there is work done and I think the government is aware that there is work to be done," he said.
He is convicted of 8 kgs of marijuana
To five years in prison and 100 days of fine a neighbor of Navolato was sentenced for the possession of eight kilos of marijuana.
The Office of the Attorney General of the Republic reported that the narcomenudista sentenced is Omar Alexis Valenzuela Sandoval, for the crime of against health, in his variant of possession of marijuana for trade purposes.
According to AP/SIN/CLN/687/2010/M-I, Valenzuela Sandoval was arrested by elements of the Mexican Army on June 7, 2010, in a surveillance operation on Cipriano Valdez street, in front of post 80, in the town of El Castillo, Navolato.
The narcomenudista circulated in a pick-up truck, white, where he carried a black bag in the box of the vehicle and in the seat of the co-pilot a small backpack, where the military found eight kilograms 23 grams of marijuana.
For these reasons, the insured was made available to the Second District Judge and the sentence remains interned at the Legal Consequences Enforcement Center.
He owes contributions for works
The social works contemplated in programs such as Habitat, Rescue of Public Spaces and 3x1 for migrants are at risk of not being completed or having serious delays due to the lack of economic resources, said Miguel Ángel López Miranda.
The director of municipal Social Development indicated that although until now the construction programs have advanced, they are at risk because the State Government has not complied with the contributions that correspond to those that add up to a resource greater than 11 million pesos in the three programs.
He pointed out that in total the investment in these three programs is almost 50 million pesos, of which 25 percent corresponds to the State and that until now only the Federation and the Municipality have contributed resources for the works, so the possible delay in the conclusion of these becomes valid.
"There are almost 12 million that they have to contribute and despite the fact that the year is about to finish they have not given us a fifth, when since July they had to give us a part," he said, "we in order not to delay we have started the works and they are very advanced, but we need the money."
He mentioned that last year, in the administration of Jesús Aguilar Padilla, the State did not complied with their contributions, so at the beginning of the administration the Municipality had to apply for a credit to cover the more than 7 million that the state administration did not contribute on that occasion.
"It already happened to us in the previous six years because the State did not have the economic capacity to participate and the programs continued walking, and this year we to be able to access these programs we had to get the resources to cover what the state left pending," he said.
Although there is a commitment of the state authorities to comply with the delivery of these resources, he commented that the request has been made to the Secretary of Administration and Finance, Armando Villarreal, to rush the efforts so that the percentages that correspond to them arrive as soon as possible.
"It's almost 12 million that they have to contribute and despite the fact that the year is about to end they have not given us a fifth."
Court validates "analog blackout" in Mexico in 2015
The replacement of analogue-to-digital signals is one of the most important changes that the world has undergone in recent years
The Supreme Court of Justice of the Nation (SCJN) dismissed the constitutional controversies filed by the Senate and the Chamber of Deputies against the presidential decree that advances the so-called "analogue blackout" from 2021 to 2015, by not meeting the eight votes necessary to invalidate it.
A minority of four ministers, including the president of the Court, Juan Silva Meza, considered that President Felipe Calderón did not exceed his powers by issuing the decree detailing the actions to follow in order to finalize the transition to digital terrestrial television.
With that result, the constitutional controversy promoted by the Senate of the Republic, was dismissed for not reaching the qualified majority of eight votes for the Court to rule on the constitutionality or unconstitutionality of the decree, and this remains in force.
The draft judgment, prepared by Minister Olga Sánchez Cordero, proposed declaring the decree unconstitutional, because the regulation of radio and television matters is a power reserved by law with the Federal Telecommunications Commission (Cofetel), in contravention of the principles of hierarchical subordination and reservation of law.
In favor of the project only Zaldívar, Jorge Mario Pardo, Margarita Luna and Sánchez Cordero himself voted.
With this argument, both the Senate and the Chamber of Deputies promoted two constitutional controversies to challenge the decree, and by admitting the demands, Minister Sánchez Cordero suspended the application of the decree and all the actions in it provided for the digital transition.
The plenary of ministers also decided to dismiss the demand filed by the Chamber of Deputies, without entering into the discussion of the project, also in charge of Minister Sánchez Cordero.
In his speech, the minister president said he did not agree with the arguments of the project, in the sense that the Federal Executive cannot structure through general rules the measures so that the public administration is prepared.
"I also do not believe that if it did, the autonomy of the regulatory body would be affected," and recalled that the planning of public policies in strategic areas such as the telecommunications sector correspond to the Federal Executive, who has by constitutional mandate, the rectory of the State.
The replacement of analogue and digital signals is one of the most important changes that the world has undergone in recent years, and translates into a better use of radio spectrum, better signal quality and more channels.
To postpone the change, he said, was to postpone the benefits of digitization, which implies a decision of public interest, which does not only have a technical dimension, "because it covers economic, social, political and national security issues."
These issues, he stressed, "require the operation of the entire established system, in accordance with the principle of economic rectory of the State, encompasses a group of powers that the Constitution leads to the faculty of the State Executive."
He recalled that a large number of countries around the world have already concluded the digital transition, and has done so through the Executive, despite having highly specialized technical bodies.
It cannot be concluded that the Executive, in issuing the contested decree, which invades in some way the sphere of competences of Congress, since the observance of the laws issued by the Legislature is being provided in the administrative sphere, he said.
Analyze possibility of a "vote-by-vote" count in Michoacán
According to the data yielded by the PREP, the difference between first and second place of the election of governor is only 27 percent, even without counting the votes corresponding to 879 of the 6 thousand 74 total minutes.
The possibility of the "vote-by-vote" count is a reality in terms of the electoral process this year in Michoacán, where according to the data released by the Preliminary Electoral Results Program (PREP) the difference between first and second place of the election of governor is only 27 percent, even without counting the votes corresponding to 879 of the 6 thousand 74 total minutes.
In this regard, the president of the General Council of the Electoral Institute of Michoacán (IEM), María de los Ángeles Llanderal Zaragoza, recalled that a few days ago, just on November 9, the electoral body approved a series of guidelines in order to harmonize local norms with the federal reform in electoral matters.
As will be recalled, Michoacán did not homologate its Political Constitution or its State Electoral Code or its Electoral Justice Law, since the State Congress approved the reforms shortly before the conclusion of the legal deadline so that they could enter into force in the present electoral process and Governor Leonel Godoy Rangel omitted its publication within the deadlines provided for in the Official State Newspaper Act, with the argument that he had some objections.
On November 9, the Supreme Court of Justice of the Nation (SCJN) resolved the constitutional controversy promoted by the local Legislative Power and described as "invalid and illegal" the vote of the state president to the constitutional reforms, since he does not have the right to "sanction or veto them".
Interviewed about the possibility of "voting by vote", María de los �Ángeles Llanderal explained that thanks to the approval of the corresponding guidelines by the General Council of the IEM it will be possible to carry out partial and total counts of the vote by the local electoral body.
In this way, the partial counting of votes, of one or more electoral packages, may occur in the assumptions that: the results of the minutes do not coincide; there is not the act of scrutiny and calculation in the file of the box or not in the possession of the president of it; or, when there are errors or obvious alterations of the minutes.
In addition, the total recount of the vote received in all boxes of the election may take place when there is an indication that the difference between the presumed candidate winner of the election in question and the one who obtained the second place in the vote, is equal to or less than one percentage point; provided that at the beginning of the session there is the express request of the representative of the party and/or coalition who applied to the second of the indicated candidates.
The total counting of the vote may also take place if at the end of the ordinary computation it is established that the difference between the candidate allegedly winner and the one located in the second place is equal to or less than one percentage point, provided that there is an express request of the representative of the political party whose candidate was second, in which case the electoral packages that have been partially recounted will be excluded.
It should be mentioned that in this situation is the election of Morelia, where according to the PREP the difference between the first and the second place is only 0.16 percent, althus without counting the votes of 129 of the 923 total minutes.
Your Last Will
Their ashes will be scattered in the Zapalinamé mountain range, the family confirms.
The remains of pilot Felipe Bacio Cortés arrived in Saltillo, his hometown, this Sunday night, on Monday were carried out reconnactions and a requiem mass.
According to his relatives, the last will of the lieutenant colonel was that his ashes were scattered in the Zapalinamé mountain range.
After receiving a tribute on the side of the others killed in the accident, Bacio Cortés’ family returned to Coahuila’s capital aboard a Mexican Air Force aircraft, the closest people attended the ceremony at Campo Marte.
At twelve noon the ashes of the lieutenant colonel arrived the chapel of wake-up located on the Nazario Ortiz Garza boulevard, where family and friends arrived.
At 5:30 in the afternoon he was given a recognition at the Technological Institute of Saltillo organized by his former teammates of the technological baccalaureate and of the American football team, of which Felipe Bacio was part during his preparation of high school.
"His will was to return to Saltillo and have his ashes scattered in the Zapalinamé mountain range, he always expressed it to his family and his friends," Sandra Bacio Cortés told the pilot.
For her, the lieutenant colonel had fulfilled all her dreams, considers that she was in the fullness of her life, "he was a successful man, extraordinary as a father, as a son, a loving brother and aware of his family."
Felipe Bacio Cortés came out very young from the city of Saltillo to enter the Colegio del Aire in Zapopán, Jalisco.
Some didn’t believe it because they said he was even dizzy in the truck; however, he always sought to fulfill his dream of becoming a pilot.
His nephew Jorge Alberto Dávila Bacio remembers him as a very good person, "barely was in Saltillo the whole family wanted to greet him, like the time he brought Martha Sahagún to Saltillo."
The young man remembers that even when he was seven years old he traveled to Zapopán for the graduation of his relative, so he knows that his uncle died doing what he liked most in life: flying an aircraft.
You're given the last goodbye
With honors, Lieutenant Colonel of the Mexican Air Force, Felipe Bacio Cortés, was dismissed in a tribute headed by Governor Jorge Torres López, and who gave him people and Government of the State, the Mexican Army, through the Sixth Military Zone, the Mexican Air Force itself, directors and students of Saltillo Technology, where he studied in 1985 and stood out both in the study and in sport.
Bacio Cortés died in recent days when the helicopter crashed, along with the Secretary of the Interior, José Francisco Blake Mora, and six more people, all officials of that unit.
At 18:05 the remains of the saltillense Bacio Cortés (cremates) arrived, to be given by the funeral home to members of the Mexican Air Force, headed by Major Jaime Martinez, on the esplanade of the campus, where it was already expected by his relatives, including his daughter Amanda and his wife Cristina, his mother, brothers and other relatives, students and teachers.
They locate three abandoned corpses in Cadereyta NL estate
Hours prior to the discovery, the military authority presented to the media eleven people who were arrested in the same municipality for crimes related to organized crime.
Three men were found shot dead in different parts of the body and with the shot of grace, in an abandoned estate in the municipality of Cadereyta Jiménez, Nuevo León.
The discovery happened at kilometer 1.5 of the road from Cadereyta to Santiago, at the height of the community known as El Castillo.
Several people who passed through the place observed that in the fifth, whose fence is beige with coffee, was the gate open wide.
When they looked inside, about three meters away, they located the bodies of three people, so they immediately gave notice to the corresponding authorities.
One of the individuals wore a yellow t-shirt with dark denim trousers, without footwear; beside him was another one with white t-shirt.
Just one meter was the body of another individual with red t-shirt and blue denim trousers.
All three were face down.
According to the data obtained at the scene, the three people would have been killed elsewhere, since no casings were found at the site.
In the fence of the fifth you can see legends that are directed from one group of organized crime to another, in addition to several bullet holes in it and the gate.
Captured at 11 in Cadereyta
The Ministry of National Defense made the presentation of 11 people, including a minor, who were part of a criminal group that operated mainly in the municipality of Cadereyta and who are associated with kidnappings, homicides, "hawking" and clandestine burials.
According to the information released, in a first event on November 11 in the Los Alveros colony, eight people were arrested and one who remained deprived of his freedom was rescued.
They were assured three properties and one cell phone; they claimed to be members of the criminal group called "Los Zetas".
In another event that occurred on November 12 at 06:00 hours, military personnel carried out a surveillance tour in the town of Rancho Viejo, in Cadereyta, detecting a car without plates and with two subjects on board.
By marking them the stop and conducting the inspection, the military found a long weapon, a magazine and 18 cartridges.
Later and after these statements, on November 13 at 14:00, one of his accomplices was arrested in La Frauda ejido, which when he saw the military tried to flee.
Clandestine burials are credited in the municipalities of General Terán, China and Cadereyta.
The new Italian government will be formed on Wednesday
Prime Minister Mario Monti will meet tomorrow with Italy’s president to present the new government that will face a crisis that has put that country on the brink of economic disaster
Italian Prime Minister Mario Monti will meet with Italy’s president on Wednesday to unveil the new government that will face a crisis that has placed Italy on the brink of economic disaster and has jeopardized the entire eurozone.
A statement from the presidential palace announced that Monti, named on Sunday, will meet with President Giorgio Napolitano on Wednesday to confirm that he can form a government.
It is expected to present a cabinet composed mostly of technocrats, although it is not clear when the new executive will take office.
Monti said he will present to the president the outcome of his talks with political parties in his attempt to form a government.
"I want to confirm my absolute confidence in our country's ability to overcome this difficult phase," the former European commissioner told reporters.
Monti did not explicitly say that he will be able to form a government but the tone of his comments indicated that the obstacles have been overcome.
The "framework is now well delineated," he added.
Monti has completed the process of forming a government in less than three days, much less than normal, as Italy combats a political and financial crisis that has boosted debt costs to unsustainable levels.
The new administration led by former European commissioner Monti must approve a harsh austerity package demanded by European leaders to regain beaten confidence in Italy.
Emphasizing pressure on Monti to hurry up, market instability placed the yield on 10-year Italian BTP bonds above 7 percent, the level at which Greece and Ireland were forced to be rescued.
Emma Marcegaglia, leader of the Italian employer Confindustria, told reporters after meeting with Monti: "We said that we would support this government a lot."
We believe that this Government is the last chance for Italy to leave this emergency situation.
Monti’s chances of success were greatly boosted by Silvio Berlusconi’s PDL support, whom the crisis forced to resign on Saturday.
"We believe Professor Monti's efforts are meant to have a good result," Angelino Alfano, the center-right party's secretary, told reporters.
The support of the PDL, Italy’s largest party, is significant because until now many of its members had opposed the technocratic-majority government that is forming Monti.
Parliamentary support
Monti’s new government must have strong parliamentary support to implement what will likely be unpopular austerity reforms.
Any blockade or delay in your efforts could lead to a devastating new attack by financial markets.
The Italian association of foreign banks added to the pressure by warning that a failure of Monti would be a disaster.
Monti began consultations with political parties, trade unions and business groups on Monday, as well as youth and women’s organizations.
He'll end his round of meetings Tuesday night.
Monti was named Sunday by Napolitano, who has enabled an extremely rapid transition in response to the crisis.
After a brief respite at the end of last week, when it became clear that Berlusconi would resign, Italian debt costs have now returned to critical levels among the uncertainty about whether the new prime minister will succeed.
Rescuing Italy, with a public debt of 1.8 trillion euros, would be too much for today’s eurozone financial defenses.
Monti has said that his government should last until the next elections, scheduled for 2013, despite widespread predictions that politicians will only give it time to implement the reforms beforearing the elections.
Monti has said that he would like to include politicians in his cabinet, but the big parties insist that he must be made up only of technicians, an indication of his qualms in the face of a process forced by financial pressure.
Political sources noted that mutual mistrust and disagreements between parties are complicating an attempt to include political figures.
