Quinteto included the country in its two-decade-locked celebratory tour
"Going to Costa Rica is a dream come true."
In an exclusive interview with Viva, Pearl Jam's bassist Jeff Ament said the band is ready to offer one of their best concerts next Sunday at the National Stadium.
There have been more than 20 years of waiting, but the band assures that for them it will also be to turn the dream of playing in the country into reality.
This was stated by the bassist and founding member, Jeff Ament, who spoke exclusively with Viva, from Brazil, last week.
The group is in the South American wing of its two-decades-long celebrating tour of work and on Sunday 20 will make its only stopover in Central America.
That day they will be presented at the National Stadium, starting at 7:30 p.m., together with the Angelian band The X and the nationals of Las Robertas.
This is an excerpt from the conversation, whose complete transcription is in The Nation online.
For his Costa Rican followers, it’s a dream come true that Pearl Jam will play in Costa Rica, are you aware of what his fans are experiencing?
Going to Costa Rica is a dream come true for us too.
This is only our second time in South and Central America and so far it has been great.
I’ve been in Costa Rica only once and on vacation; it’s such a beautiful country and I think we’re going to be able to stay a few days there.
So we're excited to bring our music there.
I wonder why there’s a kind of devotion to the band, the fans live intensely the days before.
What do they say to their most fervent followers?
Thank you for staying by our side through all these years.
It took us almost 21 years to get to Costa Rica; thank you for having waited so long and we regret that it took us so long to get there.
Some people even already refer to their concert as the biggest show of the year.
Does that add a little pressure to your visit?
Some of the biggest shows we’ve played over the past 21 years happened over the last couple of weeks.
We played a couple of gigantic shows in São Paulo and we hope that that put us in shape for this concert in Costa Rica.
We're really looking forward to playing there.
We love that country, we love surfaring and the jungle; for both, Costa Rica is among the best in the world.
How did they discover Costa Rica and its waves to surf?
We have a lot of friends who are surfers and I have a great friend who lives in Tamarindo and the waves there are amazing.
My friends were always going to surf Costa Rica and Nicaragua, along with Mexico, they are great places to surf; that’s how we found out, through people who love surfing.
Seeing the tour, they’ve been handing out huge shows with a lot of songs.
What have you planned for presentation in Costa Rica?
Since we’ve never played there before, then we don’t have any restrictions on what we can do.
We’ll try to give the best show possible with a good variety of songs; we’ll try to play a couple of songs from each album and give our fans a good, diverse repertoire.
You never repeat the repertoire.
What's due to this?
I think it’s partly because we have nine albums and we can play more than 120 songs and everyone likes different songs, so it’s difficult to choose which songs to play every night.
When we have two or three songs in the repertoire that we have not played for a while, that keeps us focused and also keeps us surprised because it reminds us of the reasons why we liked those songs and that we could have forgotten.
That makes the show more interesting for us and, therefore, I think it also makes it more interesting for people.
With this tour, they are celebrating 20 years of experience.
Looking back, is it hard to believe everything they’ve accomplished?
Yes. Absolutely.
First of all, I think none of us imagined we’d be in a big rock band and done it for more than 20 years it’s looked like a dream.
We continue to recount our blessings and we are very excited that we can continue to make music together.
We all love each other and we love creating music.
We are lucky to go to places in the world that we always wanted to go to.
Things couldn't be better for us.
What did it mean for you to have been a fundamental part of the emergence of the grunge?
It’s always been an honor to be part of that group of great bands.
We're close friends with the people of Soundgarden, Mudhoney and Alice in Chains.
It’s amazing when a movement arises from a group of friends and it’s uncommon.
From time to time, a band from a city emerges, but it’s much stranger when five or ten bands come out at the same time from the same city.
We are proud and honored to be part of the Seattle group.
We still touched some of them from time to time.
Last year, we were at a festival with Alice in Chains and were in Canada with Mudhoney, we saw Soundgarden on their tour, so we’re lucky to be friends with all of them yet and we’re honored to be part of that Seattle sound that emerged.
It’s also very strange for a band like yours to stick together with the same members for so long.
Yes, four of us have been together for 21 years and Matt (Cameron) joined us 13 years ago, which, by itself, is much longer than many bands last.
First of all, I think we’re all lucky to still be alive and, second, we all care about others.
We are all very close friends and, thanks to that, musical avenues have also been opened that give freedom for everyone to experience.
We entrusted one another with each other’s music and it couldn’t be better.
Your relationship with the word fame has not been simple; however, thanks to your success, you have been able to do whatever you want.
How do you find a balance?
As we have grown, it has been easier to find a balance.
We make sure that not being too toured because it’s easy to fall into that game of the big crowds and people who love your music.
As long as one remembers that people are there for the music, it reminds you that you must respect the music and what brought it, without becoming infatuated by being famous and being a rock star, something that none of us really cares about.
How do you remember your other Mother Love Bone project?
I think we wrote very good songs and at first it was a great thing.
We were a band for almost three years and never released an album until after Andy (Andrew Woods) died.
There was a lot of frustrations because it would last so long and the most important thing is that we are still very close friends of Bruce (Fairweather) and Greg (Gilmore), who started in the band with us and when I heard a song or I see a photo I think of the incredible person that Andy was and how it made me laugh, it was always a pleasure to go to work, laugh, talk about music and football and much more.
He was an amazing human being and we missed him.
The national band Las Robertas will be in charge of opening the show in Costa Rica.
Is it true that you know her work, especially Eddie Vedder, and that's why she was chosen for the opening?
Yes, the production sent us videos with several proposals and that was the band we liked the most, so we are eager to see what Las Robertas can offer live and must be very entertaining.
Why don't Yellow Ledbetter lyrics always change live?
That's a question for Ed (Eddie Vedder), so I don't know.
He sings it and I think he does it with the first thing he can think of.
As a bassist, what are their influences?
It's so many.
Among the first ones I can think of are Geezer Butler, John Entwistle, C. J. Ramone, John Doe, Paul McCartney, Chuck Dukowski...
It's so many I could talk all afternoon about the bassists I love.
How has Pearl Jam managed to keep the grunge relevant despite not being a fashion anymore?
I think all of us always felt a little disconnected from the word grunge.
When I think about it, it reminds me of the bands I mentioned before, like Mudhoney, Soundgarden, Nirvana and all those that came up at the same time, and, if there’s relevance now, I think it’s a wonderful thing because it’s music that’s worth listening and bands like Mudhoney are now making better music than ever.
At first, his music had darker content and has now become more positive.
To what is this change due?
It’s interesting because I think there’s a couple of songs on the new album that may seem lighter, but there are also songs, like The End or Just Breathe that qualify as very dark songs although they may sound a little positive.
With both of them, when Ed touched me, I cried when I listened to them and they're deeply dark songs.
I think when you grow up and there’s some trauma in your life and you lose a family member or a friend, those things begin to influence your art and your music and those two songs certainly represent that to me.
Also, I think you can be a happy person and still be able to make music that is dark.
Peru:
Fujimori is hospitalized again for medical examinations
Former President Alberto Fujimori was admitted Tuesday to a public hospital to be evaluated for the loss of muscle strength in his legs, his GP reported.
Fujimori, 72, who is serving a 25-year prison sentence for human rights violations committed during his administration (1990-2000), suffers several ailments, including tongue cancer for which he has been operated four times and kept under control.
Congressman Alejandro Aguinaga, who is his personal doctor, told CNN that Fujimori will be screened for three days at the National Institute of Neoplastic Diseases to determine the reason for the pain and loss of strength of its lower limbs.
Aguinaga commented that Fujimori in his opinion would be a candidate to obtain the benefit of the pardon for humanitarian reasons, however, he said that he does not want it because it is known innocent.
He underwent surgery four times of cancer to the tongue, has a weight loss of 18 kilos to which other pathologies such as severe erosive gastritis, pancreas cyst, kidney stones, hypertension and circulatory problems in the legs have been combined, said the doctor.
Fujimori has been in detention since September 2007 in the National Directorate of Special Operations of the police.
In April 2009 he was sentenced to 25 years in prison for the deaths of 25 people at the hands of an army annihilation squad that operated clandestinely in the first years of his government.
Mujica travels to Mexico to strengthen political ties
An official delegation, led by Uruguayan President José Mujica and composed of several ministers and businessmen, will begin on Tuesday a visit to Mexico in order to expand trade between both nations and address political issues in the region.
Mujica will meet next Wednesday with his Mexican counterpart, Felipe Calderón, the highest point of an extensive agenda of meetings with political, commercial authorities and a meeting with the collectivity of Uruguayans residing in Mexico, according to the agenda released by the Uruguayan Presidency.
The meeting between the two presidents, in the city of Guadalajara, will be "propitious for the exchange of information in all that are the political affairs between the two nations", as published by the Presidency of the South American country on its website.
According to local media, Mujica would seek to get Calderón to support Uruguay in the incident with France when French President Nicolas Sarkozy included the South American country in a list of tax havens, at the G20 meeting in the French city of Cannes.
In his speech, Sarkozy threatened to exclude from the international community those countries that remain tax havens, a statement that provoked the rejection of Uruguay and the call for consultation of its ambassador in the European country.
Mexico has chaired the G20 since November, a group composed of the most powerful emerging and developed countries in the world, and has in force with the South American country, since January of this year, an agreement to exchange tax information in order to avoid double taxation.
The Uruguayan government has tried in recent years to expedite the realization of fiscal exchange agreements in order to leave the "gray list" of the Organization for Economic Cooperation and Development (OECD), formed by countries that did not implement all international standards of cooperation in tax matters.
On the trade level, Mexico and Uruguay have been in force since 2004 a Free Trade Agreement (FTA) that has increased the exchange of goods between both nations.
The whistling plumber passes the three dimensions with "Super Mario 3D Land"
Super Mario has struggled, tirelessly, over 25 years to save Princess Peach from the clutches of the evil Bowser, but he had never done so in three dimensions until now: in "Super Mario 3D Land" he will fight for the girl's love in stereoscopic technology.
The title, which arrives this month in stores around the world, has been designed from the beginning to take advantage of the features of the Nintendo 3DS portable console, which allows you to play in three dimensions without the need to use glasses.
"Mario is the character who has to say how stereoscopy should work without glasses," said Omar Álvarez, head of specialized press of Nintendo in Spain, during the presentation of the video game in Madrid.
Álvarez indicated that "Super Mario 3D Land" is "the first Nintendo game originally designed for that support", since until now hits such as "Star Fox 64 3D" or "Zelda: Ocarine of time 3D" were adaptations of existing titles.
The Nintendo spokesman has maintained that, despite being in 3D, "Super Mario 3D Land" is an "accessible" game that allows you to enjoy a quick and fluid game.
Álvarez has claimed that "Super Mario 3D Land" contains two games in one: the first part is "simple" to engage less skilled users.
Once overcome, the most experts will be able to take a "second lap" to the game levels, which this time present greater difficulty.
In addition, users will be able to choose in real time between an "aggressive D" or one that only affects the depth of the action, but it will be necessary to play in three dimensions to be able to overcome the different challenges and "not succumb to optical illusions".
This foray of Super Mario in the three dimensions also supposes the return of the character to a portable console, a fact that did not occur since 2005.
In this game Nintendo winks to the followers of the saga and repeats the formula of the success of the previous installments, consisting in that the plumber of the mustache will have to traverse mined platforms of dangers with the aim of releasing Princess Peach.
Although the player will find novelties in movements, environments, costumes and enemies and also a "playable" Luigi.
Colom endorses Portillo's extradition to the United States
President Álvaro Colom announced on Tuesday that he proceeded with the extradition of former Governor Alfonso Portillo to respond in the United States for the conspiracy to launder US$ 70 million.
Thus ends the process for Guatemala to respond to the request of the northern country that claimed Portillo to judge him there.
In a press conference, Colom affirmed that his decision is due to the independence of judicial and executive powers, taking into account that a sentencing court had already ordered extradition.
He asserted that during his tenure "he did not put his hands" into court rulings and processed all the extraditions he has known.
In addition to Portillo, Sergio Ruano Marroquín will be extradited for murder and serious injury, and Edgar Estrada Morales and Víctor Estrada Paredes, both for drug trafficking.
Colom implies an executive procedure after all judicial and constitutional bodies were exhausted to prosecute the former president of between 2000 and 2004.
According to resolutions of the Fifth Criminal Court and the Constitutional Court (CC), Portillo must be extradited once he has settled his legal situation in Guatemala and his human rights are guaranteed.
Portillo was acquitted of the embezzlement of Q120 million in the Ministry of Defense when he was president, which was the process that preceded extradition, but the Public Ministry appealed and reactivated the case.
Trinijove has helped 6,000 at-risk youth
The entity was born to avoid the fall in the marginality of children without training
The balance of the first 25 years of life of the social support entity Trinijove is important: 6,000 young people at risk of exclusion have received help to overcome a difficult situation throughout this quarter of a century.
To this is added the hundred long jobs currently obtained for this social group.
The anniversary served yesterday to take stock at an event held in the former Fabra i Coats factory complex attended by more than 300 people.
Trinijove was born in the Trinitat Vella neighborhood in 1986 with the aim of helping young people with little training, without work, with problems of dogradiction or in a situation of falling into marginalization.
Yesterday’s celebration in Sant Andreu concentrated a large number of leaders of entities and institutions that in these 25 years have collaborated with the association.
The varied political representation, in full electoral time, showed the unitary character of Trinijove’s work.
Together with the president of the Generalitat, Artur Mas, and his predecessor Jordi Pujol, attended the event the former Minister of Socialist Education, Ernest Maragall, and the former deputy mayor and current socialist councilor Ricard Gomà.
Also attending the great hall of the Fabra i Coats the current councilor of Benestar Social i Família, Josep Lluís Cleries, and the general director of the social work of La Caixa, Jaume Lanaspa.
Mas said that Trinijove was born in a very difficult time and that the current one is too, but added that the work done so far "is a good basis".
The president put Trinijove as an example for knowing how to fight against bad circumstances and to give hope to people with difficulties.
"The crisis is not something new for the entity and for the work it does," he said.
Evicted a squatted estate where macro parties were made
The invaders had taken over 11 of the building’s 13-store, in Balmes, 51
The 12 occupants left the farm at noon yesterday without offering resistance
Everything seems to indicate that the nightmare experienced by the neighbors of 51 Balmes Street has come to an end.
Twelve people voluntarily abandoned the property they occupied illegally at noon yesterday, and where they organized macro parties that despaired the neighborhood, by court order and without the need for police intervention.
When the operation was over, the latch of the door was changed to prevent the situation from being repeated.
"From now on there will be an access control," the administrator said.
The squatters, who had invaded 11 of the 13 floors of the building -the rest are inhabited-, took the whole electrical installation and part of the water, in addition to painting the walls and leaving waste of all kinds, especially cans of alcoholic beverages.
The first three floors were the scene of the multitudinous festivals, which lasted up to two days and desperated the tenants.
Next to the building there is a nightclub, Balmes 51, which has also been damaged.
Its owner, Maria Pantinat explained to Europa Press that since the building was occupied the business has fallen by 80%.
The owner of the nightclub points out that the invaders of the farm made him unfair competition, since they sold the cups at three euros, while in the nightclub they are worth 10.
In addition, the owner of the entertainment venue had had to respond to neighborhood complaints of noise, while, he said, the inconveniences are not caused by the customers of the nightclub, but the inhabitants of the occupied house and, above all, the attendees of the parties that were organized there, which had increased their frequency and intensity in recent weeks, according to the angry residents of the area.
The head of the club explained that the inconvenience was so great that she even reached an agreement with the owner of the property and nine months ago that she "forgives" the rent.
The Urban Guard also confirmed that in recent weeks it had received more complaints from neighbors about the situation.
The president of the municipal group of the PP in the city council, Alberto Fernández Díaz, had claimed last week "immediately evicting the property to end the problems of coexistence and civism that produce the neighbors, as well as the degradation of the area."
The popular leader explained that before the summer there was only one floor of the occupied building, but over the last few months the squatters had been entering all the floors of the building and on almost all the floors, and that there have even been robberies.
In this sense he added that, "it is necessary to end impunity and blunt against the incivism that has been generated on the farm."
Also yesterday, the Mossos evicted a building in Nou Barris.
At 15.30 hours the police went to 35 Mont-ral Street, after receiving the call from a neighbor who had observed that four people were accessing the building.
All four were arrested at the time.
Park or mountain?
The architects agree on the opportunity of the call beyond controversies about fees
The municipal contestect on the 16 doors reopens the debate on the role of Collserola in Barcelona
In the middle of the 80's, in full swing of the already extinct Metropolitan Corporation of Barcelona (CMB), this Administration printed some promotional pasquins of Collserola.
The pamphlet defined the mountain as the great park of metropolitan Barcelona.
Moreover, it was accompanied by a comparison, in figures, with the New York Central Park.
With the death of the CMB, in 1987, the park repositioned, in the imaginary of many, as the upper limit of Barcelona, being the Catalan capital of the few that are not oriented on the north-south axis but above (mountain) -below (sea).
Collserola’s rating as a natural park results in this idea.
The reference was no longer Central Park but Yellowstone, with the boars in the role of the Yogi bear.
In these it was until, in September, the city council convened a megacompetition composed of 16 contests for as many access doors to Collserola.
A door refers to the idea of a park, without a doubt.
Either that or someone intends to put doors to the field.
The question comes out alone: Collserola, park or mountain?
The contest, both because of its purely organizational aspect and what it supposes of reflection on the city is the subject of conversation among all Barcelona architects.
It is almost a recurring theme to use when two members of this old caste are recognized in an elevator.
And this fact gives rise, precisely, to one of the few coincidences between what three Barcelona architects think, and university professors, who have presented themselves.
Miguel Roldán, Daniel Mòdol and Marta Bayona: the contest is timely.
For Roldán, after addressing the coastal front "it is necessary" to discuss "the mountain front", since the speech of Ildefons Cerdà, that of green apples, has not been carried out.
"It must start from the basis that the most extensive park in Barcelona is the beach.
Among other things because of its great accessibility.
Collserola is more complex", declares the architect for whom the title of "doors of Collserola" contains a "lexical trap": we must not think of them "like access to a park, but the delimitation of areas where you have to act".
Mòdol, as Roldán and Bayona with experience in the urban world, calls the proposal an "intellectual challenge" with "some dangers".
Dangers derived from how "uncertain it is today to project the public space" after the experience lived in the 80's and that it had in the area of the Fòrum "its swan song".
The first fear is one’s delimitation of borders over where it can be built.
"Limiting something is to fix the next step," that is, its overshoot.
"You have to think about new urban planning instruments, because the current [in reference to the Metropolitan General Plan (PGM) ] can give rise to real disasters."
Closer to the question, Mòdol believes that, simplifying, the Vallesan slope of Collserola "can be understood as a park, that of Barcelona, not", for a purely topographic issue.
Mòdol recalls that there is already a work done by Collserola's Patronet that "should not fall apart", although he appreciates the opportunity that the city council offers to its guild to intervene in this project "from the first moment".
Moreover, he understands that the proximity of the mountain with the city is the safe conduct that allows architects to enter the debate, but "doubts" about whether it is this technical profile that must lead the multidisciplinary teams ("a success", he points out) that have had to be created: "The architects should only be a tool".
Bayona suggests that the answer to the question that gives title to this piece is complex, because in the bases of the contest a global vision of Collserola is missing: "16 transversal contests have been convened -he says- in the mountain-sea sense, but there is no longitudinal reading, which sees and understands the area as a whole."
This causes, at least in your case, that you have chosen to participate in the 16 contests.
Otherwise, a project for one of the doors can collide in program and definition with that of the architect of the next door.
In his opinion, yes, the city council should guarantee that the strip immediately superior to the Ronda de Dalt should provide, to the fullest extent, for an area of equipment that would make a smooth transition between the city and the green.
Casino forgives Jonás Larrazábal and he will go free
After 72 days of arraigo, the legal agent of the Casino Red granted forgiveness to the brother of the mayor of Monterrey of the process that was followed by alleged blackmail
After 72 days in prison, Manuel Jonás Larrazábal will be released after the legal agent of the Casino Red granted him the pardon before the Second District Criminal Court, which follows a trial for blackmail against the brother of the mayor of Monterrey.
Following the attack at Casino Royale, the owner of the Casino Red, Sergio Gil García, published a series of photographs and videos in which Manuel Jonás allegedly demanded the payment of an economic fee so that the Regio-Montano city council allowed the clandestine operation of the bookmaker.
After the public complaint, the State Attorney’s Office opened criminal proceedings against the brother of the panista mayor and on September 2 he was arrested and rooted for 30 days.
After four hours before the Agent of the Public Prosecutor’s Office 3 in Heritage Crimes, he was transferred to the House of Arraigo 1, located in the Old Quarter of the state capital.
In that place he remained for the following 49 days when he was issued a second arrest warrant after the agent of the Public Prosecutor's Office dismissed the crimes of bribery and organized crime.
On October 19, he was transferred to Cadereyta’s prison allegedly for blackmailing with a million and a half pesos to the owners of the Red Casino; however, he only spent a few hours in the middle-security prison since there were death threats against him.
The authorities allowed him to follow the judgment in the municipal cells of San Nicolás de los Garza.
On October 25, an arrest warrant was issued by the secretary of the Second District Criminal Court, Jorge Yánez, who said that almost two months after inquiries, enough evidence was verified to determine the guilt of Jonás, such as the collection of 1.5 million pesos to the representatives of the bookmaker in exchange for allowing the operation of it.
On Monday, the legal agent of Casino Red, Víctor Aldo García Gómez, went before the judge José Luis Pecina to request the pardon for the brother of the mayor regio; so in the coming hours he could recover his freedom.
During the legal diligence, the agent of the Public Prosecutor’s Office attached to the Court, Ramiro Arias, questioned García Gómez if the damage had been repaired and who of the partners of the betting house was the one who granted the pardon, although the lawyer tried to reserve the name.
On the basis of file 197/2011, legal pardon is granted to Jonás Larrazábal and henceforth no claim can be made about the process that was followed; in addition, he will not be required compensation for damage or punishment.
After the hearing of the legal agent of the bookmaker, around 15:50 the immediate release of Larrazabal Breton was ordered.
The secretary of the Court, Jorge Yánez, moved to the prison of the municipality of San Nicolás de los Garza to notify Jonás that he has been legally forgiven and his file will be filed.
It will also notify you of the immediate release order issued by the judge.
Secretary Dionisio Pérez Jácome gives details of the plane crash where the Secretary of the Interior, Francisco Blake, and seven other people lost his life
The Ministry of Communications and Transport (SCT) reported that the results yielded on the accident where the Secretary of the Interior, Francisco Blake Mora, and seven other people lost his life, indicate that the helicopter was directly impacted on the ground in complete structural integrity, without the pilot being able to attempt a hard landing.
During a press conference, Dionisio Pérez Jácome, secretary of Communications and Transport, mentioned that before taking off the aircraft, the pilot found that the weather conditions were adequate to fly.
"The elements that seem to indicate that the unit had several contacts with the terrain, the blades had contact with trees at 25 meters where the unit lost the fuselage."
He also reported that the unit was designed for 19 people, so it had the necessary requirements to transport personnel.
In addition, he said that in the investigations made in the area of the impact, the appearance of pieces on the ground is not reported.
"In a search it is reported that there are no parts or components of the ship elsewhere, the impact was presented structurally.
"The design of the helicopter was for 19 passengers, it had adequate requirements to transport passengers.
The terrain presents a slope of seven degrees to 30 degrees of inclination.
At the moment, the secretary assured that it is a priority to have the results before possible; however, the investigation can last for several months, it could even reach a year.
For his part, Gilberto López Meyer, general director of Airports and Auxiliary Services, said that at the time of the impact, the helicopter of the Presidential General Staff operated in cruising speed and that this happened in an ascending soft terrain.
"This information strengthens the hypothesis of a normal cruise operation at the time of impact," he said.
In the press conference, SCT holder Pérez Jácome listed six relevant elements in the investigation:
Visual flight:
According to aeronautical regulations the flight was carried out under the rules of visual flight, this implies that the crew must have visibility during flight and terrain.
Weather conditions:
Reports in the AICM placed conditions suitable for take-off, as the aircraft advances towards the border area between the DF and the State of Mexico there were layers of cloud cover at low altitude.
Selected routes:
It is likely that due to the conditions of clouding the crew has sought an area of lower elevation and greater visibility towards the Cuernavaca valley.
location of the impact.
The impact coordinates and last record of the radar aircraft coincide, probably before the impact there was no loss of control or alteration in the flight path.
Dispersion of aircraft debris in a reduced zone:
The information available so far allows us to assume that the impact occurred in a straight and side trajectory.
The scattering pattern of wreckage of the aircraft makes it possible to assume that the helicoper was impacted on the ground in conditions of structural integrity.
No evidence of fire or explosion has been detected:
Evidence remains unrevealed at the site of the wreckage of some sort of explosion or fire.
Latest results of the investigation indicate that he may have had multiple contacts with the terrain.
During the fieldwork pieces of the aircraft have been found at an elevation of 9 thousand 200 feet above average sea level, the terrain presents a slope ranging from seven degrees to 30 degrees of inclination.
The photographs show the sequence in which it was predictably dismantled along that path.
The information provides elements that appear to indicate that the helicopter may have had multiple impacts on the ground.
After the thorough search in an area wider than the cordoned site, around the accident, no scattered parts of the aircraft have been found, which strengthens the presumption that the impact occurred under conditions of structural integrity.
MSS Announces Protocol for Obesity Gene
Social Security directs research to uncover the obesity gene in children and young people; it is supported by IPN specialists
The Mexican Institute of Social Security (IMSS) announced that it directs research to discover the obesity gene in children and young people, called protocol 'Genetics of obesity of childhood and adolescence', which will allow to have markers associated with overweight.
In a statement, the head of the Medical Research Unit in Biochemistry of the Specialty Hospital of the Siglo XXI National Medical Center, Miguel Cruz López, explained that a multidisciplinary team of doctors, nurses, nutriologists and social workers was integrated.
This team is supported by specialists and graduate students of the National Institute of Public Health (INSP) and the Center for Research and Advanced Studies (Cinvestav) of the National Polytechnic Institute (IPN).
In the research it is invited to participate to the population whether or not entitled, go to the participating sports units of the IMSS in the Valley of Mexico, where the tests are carried out on minors, every Saturday from 8:00 a.m.
He stressed that the protocol began in the Cuauhtémoc Sports Unit (State of Mexico Poniente) and that since October 8, the activity is carried out in the Nezahualcóyolt Sports Unit (State of Mexico Oriente).
Once the objective of gathering 500 or 600 minors is met, the study will continue in the Independence Sports Unit (south of the Federal District) and finally in the Morelos Sports Unit (north of the Federal District).
He explained that the purpose of this research is to know the components and importance of genetics with respect to the development of obesity and to identify in Mexican children and young risk factors to prevent the onset of chronic degenerative diseases.
As World Diabetes Day was celebrated on 14 November, he said that the objective is to reunite three thousand children and young people between six and 14 years of age, with the consent and supervision of their parents, for the application of specific feeding questionnaires.
They will also be asked about the physical activity and hered family history of diseases, in order to avoid risk factors that trigger conditions such as diabetes or kidney failure.
He explained that on Saturdays anthropometric measurements (weight, height, waist circumference), blood pressure, determination of glucose, triglycerides, cholesterol and insulin and genetic levels are applied to all participants (it seeks to identify genes that are associated with childhood obesity).
Cruz López pointed out that this research is due to the concern of health professionals about the alarming weight gain in minors.
"Health surveys in 1999 and 2006 indicated that in just six years there was an increase, in the case of boys, 77 percent in obesity, and of girls of 47 percent; in terms of overweight there was also a dramatic increase," said the IMSS specialist.
He indicated that the justification for the search for markers is based on the fact that the country’s population differs from others because of their genetic history.
He reported that on average, the Mexican has 65 percent American Indian inheritance, 30 percent European and five percent African and this condition is reflected in the genes that are associated with diabetes and excess weight.
He explained that in general, the genetics of obesity results in the inability to burn the excess energy we consume (calories) and that is stored in the form of fat.
The researcher explained that the direct benefit for the participants is the comprehensive evaluation of the children, which allows to detect if they have any degree of overweight or obesity.
He even said, the presence of dark-colored (acanthosis) folds in the neck and forearm, which indicate the likelihood of metabolic alterations and even prediabetes.
The information is handled confidentially, the doctors deliver it in writing to the parents, who are explained each of the measurements and lifestyle changes they must make to prevent diseases.
Cruz López explained that there are three main components that cause overweight, obesity and other chronic diseases: sedentary lifestyle, which is the lack of daily physical activity, staying most of the day sitting in front of the TV and using means of transport rather than walking short distances.
It is also due to the high caloric intake, i.e. the increased consumption of sugary beverages, carbohydrates (tamales, bread, pizza, burger, etc.) with high fat and protein content and the genetics that are analyzed in this protocol.
UNAM asphalt would avoid bumps
Rafael Herrera, a researcher at the Faculty of Chemistry at UNAM, develops asphalts modified with polymers to produce a blend with better performance, regardless of environmental and temperature conditions
If the signals sent by the pavement were addressed, it would not be necessary to repair about 200 thousand bumps each rainy season in the Metropolitan Area of the Valley of Mexico, warned Rafael Herrera Nájera, researcher of the Faculty of Chemistry (FQ) of the UNAM, who assured that if the process of construction and maintenance of an asphalt pavement is adequate, the potholes could be reduced in quantity or even eliminated completely.
In his lecture A technological opinion of the potholes, given in the framework of the International Year of Chemistry, announced that the laboratory in charge develops asphalts modified with polymers to produce a mixture with characteristics similar to those of asphalt, but with greater mechanical resistance, both high and low temperatures.
Herrera Nájera estimated that this type of modified asphalts could be used in places where the weather is extremely hot, such as some cities in the north of the country, to improve their performance and, therefore, pothole training.
He explained that asphalt, the heaviest part of oil, is a set of hydrocarbon molecules, some of high molecular weight, called asphaltenes, and others of low molecular weight, called maltenes, which together give the pavement its characteristic viscoelastic behavior.
The viscoelastic behavior of asphalt consists in that at high temperatures, about 180 degrees, it behaves like a liquid, at 120 degrees Celsius its viscosity is very high and at 50 degrees Celsius it has an almost solid state, while at very low temperatures, the asphalt becomes brittle, he added.
This means that in the streets the resistance of asphalt is not uniform, but is modified during the day according to environmental and temperature conditions, warned the chemical engineering specialist.
The FQ researcher observed that Mexico City’s streets are mostly covered by flexible pavement, which consists of six layers of materials, of which the last three involve asphalt.
Among the most superficial layers is the asphalt folder, made up of small asphalt-drenched and then compacted stones.
Each of the pebbles that constitutes the folder, he said, is bathed with asphalt.
By transiting the vehicles and generating efforts, it allows the forces to spread very well on the pavement.
If it were modified asphalt they would diffuse even better, the chemical engineer said.
On the asphalt folder, he abounded, a last layer is placed called rolling folder, which is made of a fine stone material, that is, a sand that is also bathed in asphalt.
As it is the layer that is going to be in contact with the tires of the vehicles, it must have more mechanical force than the asphalt folder and provide friction on the way.
Being the part of the pavement that is going to receive and diffuse the efforts, the rolling folder also requires having the appropriate thickness and you must take care of the material from which it is made.
The climate, especially humidity and temperature variations, are also a very important factor to consider, especially water, he said, because it considerably affects the performance of the asphalt folder.
When the soil is moistened, the water diffuses into the folder and it is difficult for it to leave, this causes a softening of all layers of the pavement and, therefore, the formation of potholes.
In the process of forming a pothole, he said, the first to wear out is the bearing film and only then the asphalt folder is affected, but the damage does not happen instantly, but is a process that should be addressed to the first signals with adequate maintenance programs.
The also member of the National System of Researchers explained that vehicular cargo is another important factor in the formation of potholes and, therefore, the thickness of each of the layers of the pavement must always be in relation to the vehicular load and ensure that it is fulfilled, to avoid that potholes are formed frequently.
Rafael Herrera warned that there is still much to investigate and work on asphalt, for example, asphalt emulsions, which in other countries are already located even on the market and allow to repair potholes easily and without the need to use high temperatures.
What did you lack for the candidate of the Valley to be the new Miss Colombia?
Analyses of the factors that affected Melina Ramírez Serna did not reach the crown of Miss Colombia.
The Valley receives its #14 Princess title.
Again the Valle del Cauca stays with the desire of the crown of Señorita Colombia.
This time Melina Ramírez Serna had to settle for being on the podium of winners, wearing the silver crown and the band that accredit her as the new princess of Colombia.
With the result obtained last night in Cartagena, where Miss Atlantic won the crown and the scepter, the department already adds 14 titles of Princess and Melina joined the legion of Vallecaucan women who have been close to the crown but who were proclamations princesses among them Miriam Ospina Benoit (1947), Clara Domínguez Borrero (1949), Patricia Bellini Ayala (1979), Lorena � � Álvarez (1981), Rose
Today the great question that many are asking is what was missing?
Because the result represents the defeat of a great favorite that in each presentation showed that she had everything to be the new Miss Colombia, not in vain she was elected Queen of the Police and Best Face Jolie de Vogue, titles that obtained queens like Taliana Vargas or Natalia Navarro who took the crown of Señorita Colombia.
The first factor that is considered key in the decision made by the jury to designate her First Princess lies in the fact that she did not agree with her answer to the question "What is that book that has marked you and why? made by Martín Murillo Gómez, of the Leamos Literary Cart.
Upon interrogation the Vallecaucana replied:
"I read a lot, I think a lot in literature, I think it's a way to culturalize, I definitely believe that the book that marked me from childhood is "The Little Prince", is a rather profound book.
Currently I read "Crime and Punishment", it is a book of universal literature that we all must read; and today I am reading the book of Irene Nemirovsky, a Russian, a very beautiful book that is called "The Ardor of Blood".
An answer that little liked first by the term "culturalizing" and second for not explaining in a concrete way how each of the books he cited have marked it.
But at the end of the ceremony Melina said he felt calm and happy with the result "the universe is wise what has to happen.
And for all the people, please read a little, "The little prince" is a great book"
Among the hypotheses launched it is said that Valle was burned by favoritism.
A Bogotan journalist hinted that being the favorite, the ovation and the candidate who wore all the preliminary prizes (Oster gastronomy challenge, vote of the public for the best artisan costume, and Queen Mother), "that aroused the jealousy of the other candidates, stressed the queen and that's why she didn't shine on stage."
And that was noticeable in his first swimsuit presentation on Hilton Beach and his two parades in the coronation evening.
Although in both looked safe, it was noticed that he lacked the strength and impetus that the representatives of Magdalena and Atlantique did demonstrate.
Ariel Osorio, a journalist for Canal RCN’s “Co-Home” program, said the fact that Melina was designated a princess “checks that the candidate who wins the most prizes during the contest doesn’t look like queen.”
In addition, Valle showed himself to the last very elusive with the press, and there he was wrong.
Another hypothesis is that Valle failed to convince the jury about its interest in social work, which is ultimately the essence of the National Beauty Contest.
For something, two days before the election the international jury Lucy Doughty said that they were looking for a woman committed to the social needs of her country.
"Beauty alone is not enough in a Miss Colombia, you have to look beyond the physical and focus on their social sensitivity, that before entering the Contest they are already involved in projects," he explained.
It is also speculated that Valle sinned for her defects in the derriere and the fibrosis that was noticeable in her abdomen, but it is contradictory because precisely the jury chose her as the candidate 'Natural beauty' with the healthiest body, good nutrition and responsible physical work.
Queen’s coach Jorge Hernán Orozco acknowledges that Melina didn’t have the best body, that there were better, “but her problem of fibrosis in the abdomen, that the truth for me wasn’t very noticeable, lowered stitches on him on the last day.”
Meanwhile the journalist Guido Hoyos, of the magazine Cromos considers that the error of the vallecaucana were the surgical interventions that was practiced.
"Surgeries are fine, but Valle couldn't do them.
Her body was never her fort and was charged here."
For this award, which for the first time presents the National Beauty Contest, after the parade in swimsuit a cruel prediction emerged that last night was fulfilled, because it was said that this was the content that Raymundo granted to Melina so as not to give him the taste of the crown.
Reality or not, the truth is that Melina from today begins a year of reign, not as a sovereign, but as a princess, a position that gives her the possibility of representing Colombia in one of the international competitions by designation of the National Contest itself.
However, she will go down in history as one of the queens who despite her overwhelming passage through Cartagena, she was dodged the crown.
This was the work of the Caleño who identified the body of Alfonso Cano
A Lophoscopist from Cali went down in history as the man who confirmed the death of "Alfonso Cano".
The work of these officials is key.
On November 4, a Lophoscopist of the CTI of Cali went down in history as the man who confirmed the identity of the chief of the FARC: "Alfonso Cano", through the comparison of his fingerprints.
Of the seven lophoscopists who work in the CTI sectional, that week it was the turn of "availability" to Eliazar González, a man from Puerto Tejada who has been working in the area of judicial investigation for 21 years.
At the beginning of his career, González worked in municipalities of Chocó, where many times he had to enter for more than five days in the jungle to get to the place where the corpses that had to be identified.
In 2000 he moved to the capital of the Valley and, after performing the corresponding courses, he specialized in lofoscopy, which is the study of friction skin, i.e. of the palms of the hands and feet.
The week of the operation against alias Alfonso Cano told Eliazar that he would wear clothes for at least three days.
Something big was waiting for him.
On Friday night he left for Cauca in a helicopter from Palmyra.
He knew that he had to identify two bodies and that it was suspected that one of them was of the man who directed the oldest guerrillas in the world, so he was asked to carry Guillermo León Sáenz’s decactillary card (his real name).
However, when he arrived with the investigative team at the place where the body was located he did not recognize it because of its physical appearance, because it was very different from the one he had seen in the media.
He was unbearded, dressed as an area farmer, no glasses and much thinner.
But the fingerprints don't lie.
Accompanied by a Galtonian magnifying glass, ink, a transplanter and a flashlight, Eliazar compared the body’s fingerprints to those of the card.
His eye didn't fail him: it was "Alfonso Cano."
That same night, the man from Puerto Tejada was in charge of confirming the Minister of Defense, Juan Carlos Pinzón, that he was the head of the FARC.
In search of the footprint
For a dactiloscopist of the Sijín de Cali the recognition made by President Juan Manuel Santos to Eliazar González, in the press conference for the operation against "Cano", is very significant.
A lot of people don't know our work.
Dactyloscopy is an exact sciency, with no margin of error, which can become key in solving crimes.
Apart from the identification of corpses, Lophoscopists support investigations into crimes such as theft and homicide and confirm the identity of the people captured.
When captured, many criminals have false cards or have no papers.
We take fingerprint printing and look for them in the database.
The investigator said that, in a case of theft, he had to remove a piece of wall from a house, because in that place the thief had supported his hand to take out a safe full of jewelry and money.
"The walls are difficult because they are not appropriate surfaces to make a finger impression, but in that case we were able to obtain the fingerprint of the whole hand and identify the offender, who had participated in different thefts," he recalled.
Footprints can also rule out or reassert suspects in homicide cases.
They recently found a dead man with bottles of liqueur around him.
By means of the investigation it was determined that a relative could be suspicious of the crime and, indeed, a print on the bottle coincided.
Although this doesn't guarantee that he was the murderer, he does place him at the crime scene.
A CTI dactyloscopist explained that in the objects "the fingerprints are extracted with chemical or physical reagents".
When they appear, a translator or tape is used to lift them.
After this, the impressions are sent to the Asif, which is the criminal database, or to the Registry.
"The problem is that there are still many undocumented Colombians," the CTI expert said.
He clarified that not everything is as shown on the CSI TV show.
Just as not everything that shines is gold, not everything that is touched becomes footprint.
However, if it can be used, if the respective matching is performed.
"Outrages" defy authorities in New York
Hundreds of protesters crowded in front of Zuccotti Square for camping.
A larry part attended the eviction order and moved to a nearby park.
Occupy Wall Street members on Tuesday defied New York authorities by trying to re-enter, under the protection of a court order, into Manhattan Square where they were evicted at dawn and while trying to occupy another Big Apple park.
Hundreds of protesters crowded before Zuccotti Square, where they wanted access thanks to a court order that allows the "indignados" to re-encamp on the site, while another part of the movement responded to the eviction by occupying a park further north of the city, where there have been new arrests.
“Our idea is that, if the court doesn’t allow us to re-camp in the square, we’re going to settle somewhere else and we’ll continue the legal battle to return to Zuccotti, as well as through sit-ins and peaceful demonstrations,” said one of the spokesmen for the “Occupy Wall Street” Mark Bray movement.
Bray explained that the response of the "outraged" to the eviction of their camp dictated personally by the mayor of New York, Michael Bloomberg, reached the courts, where Judge Lucy Billings issued an order prohibiting the forced evacuation of the place and allowed the protesters to stay camped out.
In addition, the "indignados" proceeded to the occupation of a new park located at the confluence of the central Canal Street and Sixth Avenue, very close to the entrance to the Holland tunnel, which connects New York and New Jersey, and owned by Trinity Church parish.
For at least an hour they managed to occupy the place, but then the police came and proceeded to arrest some of the protesters, although Bray could not confirm the specific figure, while he assured EFE that he saw several journalists who were also handcuffed.
The organization assures that there are several hundred people who had settled at that point, where a delegation of religious leaders has already been displaced in support of the members of the movement and has tried to mediate with the police.
Meanwhile, Zuccotti Square -which is a privately-owned precinct - is still closed pending the celebration of a judicial hearing scheduled for this same Tuesday in which the legality of the evacuation this morning must be analyzed.
Some of the demonstrators displaced there carried and distributed copies of that court order at this time and even handed them over to the agents who guarded the square, which provoked "some small clashes," said Bray, who denounced that some officers beat the protesters.
The injunction prohibits authorities from evicting Occupy Wall Street members or enforcing “laws that were issued after the occupation began or protesters from re-accessing the park with stores or other properties they previously used.”
Mayor Bloomberg told reporters that, due to that court order, the city had suspended the reopening of space to the public and the protesters, to which he warned, however, that local laws do not allow them to reinstall with tents and sleeping bags.
The mayor personally defended the eviction carried out in the early morning of Zuccotti Square, an operation in which about 200 arrests were proceeded and which was decided because the protesters were "violating the law".
Bloomberg explained that he gave the order to evacuate the square because it was becoming "a place where people did not come to protest, but to violate the laws and, in some cases, to hurt other people", since "some businesses had received threats" and the neighbors feared "for their quality of life".
"The eviction this morning was disgusting and demonstrates that Bloomberg is more interested in preserving the financial interests than those of U.S. workers," the movement's spokesman added, accusing the mayor of "lacking respect for freedom of expression."
"Occupy Wall Street" officials also indicated that they plan to organize "great actions" by Thursday in collaboration with community organizations and unions, to commemorate the two months of the protests, which began Sept. 17.
Pamela Anderson will be the Virgin Mary in a Christmas television special
Along with Michael Bublé and other prominent Canadians, he will appear on "It's a Russell Peters Christmas" on December 1
Canadian Pamela Anderson has been Tim Allen's "Home Improvement", lifeguard in "Baywatch", bunny of "Playboy", the wife of rock musician Tommy Lee and now, the actress and model will be the Virgin Mary in a Christmas special on Canadian television.
Private television channel CTV today announced that Anderson, along with "crooner" Michael Bublé and other prominent Canadians, will appear on the TV show "It's a Russell Peters Christmas" on December 1 giving life to a very particular Virgin Mary.
CTV today distributed images of the television program in which Anderson, also known for the extensive online distribution of a pornographic home video in which she appears together with her then husband, the drummer of "Mötley Crüe", Tommy Lee, appears dressed as the Virgin Mary holding in a manger a doll representing Jesus.
On the scene, Peters, a well-known Canadian comedian of Indian origin who recently starred as a presenter in part of Charlie Sheen’s “My Violent Torpedo of Truth” tour, appears as Joseph.
In addition to Anderson and Peters, CTV’s Christmas special features Canadian singer Bublé, who at the end of March married Argentine model Luisana Lopilato in Buenos Aires, and which is not odd to controversy.
Recently, Bublé called the American Kim Kardashian "slut" during one of her concerts in New York.
Japan is reborn after the earthquake
GDP grows by 1.5% thanks to domestic consumption and ends the spiral of falls that began in March
Since last March the Japanese economy was paralyzed, still convalescing by the devastating earthquake that became a nuclear crisis, the worst catastrophe that the Japanese country suffered since World War II.
The first estimates by the Government of Tokyo figured material losses at 300,000 million dollars, a blow that, despite the difficult global context, seems to have dimmed.
After three consecutive quarters of declines, the Gross Domestic Product data between July and September served to turn the page, definitively: GDP increased by 1.5% compared to the previous quarter and 6% in annualized rate.
That registered in the second quarter - for the Japanese, the fiscal year begins in March 2012 - also represents the highest rate of growth from January to March 2010.
The recovery is explained, in part, by the rebound of the purchase of machinery to restore areas ravaged by the earthquake and the subsequent tsunami.
In fact, companies increased their investments by 1.1%, compared to the 0.9% decrease in capital expenditures observed from January to March, the first decline after six months of uninterrupted increases.
However, the real revulsion of the Japanese economy is not based exclusively on the Keynesian impulse that invites to take advantage of the crises to reinforce - or rebuild - the infrastructures, to use the gaps to grow from scratch.
Japan has aroused, above all, thanks to the evolution of domestic consumption, which currently represents 60% of Japanese GDP.
This variable climbed by 1%, pushed by the increase in spending on vehicles, travel and leisure activities.
Stigma of Yen
Tokyo has been entrusted to the internal market, when the yen - the Japanese currency - has become one of the "refugement values" chosen by investors fleeing the debt crisis in the euro zone and the murky U.S. forecasts, a privilege that weighs like a slab in the accounts of exporting companies.
With a stronger currency, the products are more expensive abroad, a serious setback for a country whose line of economic flotation is determined by the health of large technological groups.
Toyota or Sony, for example, blamed its weak results between July and September on the yen’s listing.
As markets continue their siege to the EU’s peripheral countries, Yoshihiko Noda’s government doesn’t lower its guard.
"You have to keep an eye out for the risks that persist, such as the deterioration of some foreign economies, the impact of Thailand's floods and the rapid appreciation of the yen," Economic Policy Secretary Motohisa Furukawa said yesterday.
Indeed, the prospects are not encouraging.
The Organization for Economic Cooperation and Development (OECD) said that the majority of member countries in September showed sharper signs of economic slowdown.
The indicator anticipating the tipping points in the global economy highlighted that the major powers are slowing down.
As it fell four tenths to 100.4 points, compared to 100.8 in August.
The euro zone even stood below the average, which is at level 100, with eight tenths to 99.13 points precipitated.
Athens feeds the doubts again
Papadimos says the adjustments will be approved, but conservatives won’t sign any written commitments
With peripheral risk premiums triggered and uncertainty reinstalled in the parquets, Greece continues to entangle in the labyrinth leading towards the exit of the great European debt crisis.
The feeling that the "consensus" of the new Hellenic transitional government is more imposed by the demands of Brussels than by a genuine conviction of the political forces begins to be confirmed with facts.
While the new prime minister, Lukas Papadimos, warned yesterday that there is no alternative for the structural reforms and that the one hundred-day period agreed in his appointment may fall short to implement them, the leader of the conservative New Democracy party, Antonis Samaras, assured that he will not support new austerity measures in any case and reiterated his demand for new elections for the scheduled date, February 19.
"The main task of this Government is to implement the decisions of the summit on October 26," Papadimos said, opening yesterday a parliamentary debate that will culminate tomorrow with a vote of confidence to the new Executive.
A package with 24 programs to boost employment, the reform of the disciplinary code of officials and new measures against tax evasion will be the first initiatives.
However, Samaras’s message doesn’t clear doubts about his stance: “We are committed to helping the transitional government, but we haven’t committed to anything else.”
The Conservative leader was a step further in his pulse to the EU and the IMF, the bodies that must unlock the 8,000 million Athens urgently needs, and he made it clear that he will not give his go-ahead to any letter of commitment to comply with the adjustments.
But the European Commission, still slaughtered by the ordago of the Papandreou referendum, reiterated yesterday that its demand is firm: it will not unlock the aid if the new government and the leaders of the main Hellenic parties do not sign "an unambiguous written commitment and as soon as possible".
The spokesman for Economic Affairs, Amadeu Altafaj, explained that the inspectors of the troika will return "very soon" to Athens to discuss with the new government "what is needed to pay for the sixth tranches" of 8,000 million, although there is also no definitive date.
The Greek skein still has thread for a while.
Doña Sofía: "They are instruments for progress and hope"
The Queen highlights Spain's "preponderant" place in relation to this type of finance
Spain "has reached a predominant place worldwide in the role of Microfinance".
Queen Sofia recalled yesterday that the country has become in these last two decades in the second donor of the proposal in relation to this intrument of cooperation, whose "essence" said that it lies in being an "instrument for social progress, justice and the hope of a better future for all humanity, based on trust in human beings."
The Queen inaugurated yesterday the Fifth World Microcredit Summit in Valladolid with the conviction that "there is no doubt" that this type of operations are "absolutely indispensable" to achieve the Millennium Goals established by the United Nations with a 2015 horizon, and, "very especially, in which it has as its goal the halving of the volume of people living with incomes less than a daily dollar, with which the "threshold of poverty" is fixed
Spain "continues working" to expand the network of beneficiaries "to all corners of the planet, and assumes this task with "responsibility", said Doña Sofía, who hoped that the initiatives and contributions that originate in the Summit, which will last until Thursday, "ensure the creation of new capacities and better opportunities for the poorest, and especially for women", main beneficiaries of these loans.
There are "many achievements achieved" and "many of the challenges we face".
The Microcredit Summit is proposed not only as a forum for debate, but also as the platform from which to promote "two basic goals".
Thus, His Majesty explained that at the end of 2015, 175 are the millions of poor families who have accessed these financial services and that one hundred million of these households leave the poverty line at which they are plunged.
The impetus in the conception of microcredits in Spain and in the rest of the world "has followed the path initiated years ago in a pioneering way" by Muhammad Yunus, highlighted Doña Sofía as an "act of justice and due recognition".
And he described the father of this tool as an "admirable idealist in the universal cause of the fight against poverty", whose work he has known for 15 years, when the Queen traveled to Bangladesh to know on the ground her loan policy.
This experience was followed a year later by the first World Summit in Washington and other meetings such as those held in Côte d’Ivoire in 1999, Canada 2006, and “in all I have been able to verify the continuous evolution”, explained His Majesty, whose link with this project yesterday earned him the qualification of the “head of family” gathered in Valladolid by the “baker of the poor”.
Finally, Queen Sofia concluded her speech by encouraging those present to participate in the debate and in the search for solutions to advance the fight against poverty.
"Imperative ethics"
The Minister of Foreign Affairs, Trinidad Jiménez, warned that solidarity "is an ethical imperative" for every democratic state.
In this sense, he explained that it would be a mistake to link cooperation with developing countries to times of economic bonanza, since it is a "permanent obligation".
Jiménez considered that microcredits represent "a serious and rigorous policy dedicated to eradicating poverty", and now "more than ever" the governments of developed countries must work under criteria of solidarity and not let it fall victim to the cuts.
For Spain, microcredit consists of "one of the main lines of development cooperation".
For her part, the Secretary of State for International Cooperation, Soraya Rodríguez, applauded "the success" of "trusting" people "excluding those who all close the doors"; and the mayor of Valladolid, Francisco Javier León de la Riva, welcomed those present to a city that "opens its doors infected with the solidarity spirit".
CDU proposes to elect European President by universal suffrage
On the first day of the congress of the Christian-Democratic Union (CDU), on Monday, November 14, in Leipzig, it has passed without great difficulty for Angela Merkel, Chancellor and President of the Christian-Democratic party.
The future of Europe had not initially been included on the agenda, but the issue prevailed in autumn, in particular to satisfy the militants who consider that the government’s policy is currently too unstrict with the Greeks and it could be so in the future with the Italians.
The motion on the Presidency of the European Commission, proposed by management, was adopted almost unanimously.
Of the 1,001 delegates, only nine votes against and ten abstentions were registered.
The several-minute ovation standing that was won, with his interventions, Angela Merkel and, after several hours, Wolfgang Schäuble, the Europhile Minister of Finance, clear any possible doubts.
The key idea of the Chancellor, and of the motion, are summed up in a motto: the answer to the crisis "is no less Europe, but more Europe".
Not only because Europe supposes peace, but also because "nine million jobs depend directly on the euro".
For the Chancellor, "it's time to make way for Europe."
This could be manifested in amendments to the Treaty of Lisbon to strengthen the European Union's control over the budgetary policies of euro area countries, although it could also lead to the election of the President of the European Commission by universal suffrage to "give a face to Europe".
In any case, that is one of the proposals of the motion adopted by the Congress, clearly the idea of Wolfgang Schäuble.
Undoubtedly, it is unlikely that the twenty-seven will quickly reach an agreement on this radical change: Britain is hostile to him and CDU leaders seem to know that Nicolas Sarkozy too.
But the ball's on the ground.
On the other hand, delegates have rejected by an overwhelming majority the proposal to weight the weight of each country of the directive of the European Central Bank according to the weight of each economy, which would obviously reinforce Germany’s weight.
Eager to calm the spirits inside her party, the Chancellor has increased references to Konrad Adenauer and Helmut Kohl. But it is not that she has tried to beat her congress at the expense of "small" European countries or countries in difficulties willing to criticize arrogant Germany.
Although the motion on Europe devotes a section to the importance of "French-German friendship", Angela Merkel has not lied to France.
Exceep in passing.
Recalling that, two centuries ago, a bloody battle ensued in Leipzig.
Indeed, it was one of the most humiliating defeats for Napoleon’s army.
The incidence of fraud in sick leave
After the subsidies, the UMP and the Government undertake an offensive against sick deaths.
Nicolas Sarkozy will be in Bordeaux on Tuesday, November 15, to visit a box responsible for family subsidies and deliver a speech on the subject, already recurrent since 2007 of the fight against social fraud and help.
In addition, to accompany such an offensive, which starts from the willingness to save rigorously, but still reminds of an axis of the right-wing presidential campaign, the majority wants to focus on sick leave.
Firstly, to implement budget cuts: the Government plans to impose on private sector workers a fourth day of lack before they are able to take leave.
In short, unless there is a specific company agreement, a worker who falls ill would lose four days of salary, that is, a minimum of 15% of their monthly income.
It is a measure that could raise a wave of anger, but that would save 280 million euros, according to Les Echos.
In addition, it would be accompanied by another measure with a more symbolic scope: to impose on civil servants a day of lack without remuneration in case of work leave (so far, they have no shortage, compared to the three days that are applied in the private sector).
As these issues are debated, the majority also puts the issue of fraud on the table, this time oriented towards the abuses of job losses.
"Controls are going to increase considerably and, in addition, if you discover someone committing a fraud, you will have to reimburse the amounts," Xavier Bertrand warned speaking in the RTL on Sunday, November 13.
As in the other cases, the effects of the announcement appear slightly disproportionate to the reality of job loss fraud.
In fact, since 2002 this issue is constantly monitored and a number of political offensives have been launched, which have normally resulted.
The French do not get sick more than in the rest of Europe.
Let’s look carefully, first and foremost, at national figures.
According to the French Institute for Social Security (CNAM), in 2006 more than 237 million euros were paid in benefits for work leave.
According to the French Institute of Research and Documentation in Health Economics, the subsidies due to casualties accounted, in 2008, 5% of health expenditure, amounting to 11,300 million euros.
Of this amount, 46% corresponds to maternity leave and occupational accidents, while 54% is related to sick leave, a figure that amounts to 6.200 million euros.
Consequently, 2.5% of health expenditure.
In 2010, the French took an average of 14.5 workdays, compared to 17.8 in 2009, according to a study by the Alma Consulting group.
An average that undoubtedly hides deviations: another survey, published in 2007 by Syrian professional management Monster.com, showed that 75% of the 40,000 French employees surveyed claimed that they had not caught a single day of sick leave.
Another study, in this case of Assurance maladie, indicates that in 2010 37% of sick leave lasted less than 8 days, 22% of between 8 and 14 days, 15% between 15 days and one month, 15% of between one and three months and 11% were longer.
Comparing them to our European neighbours, French workers are no more likely than in other areas to catch sick leave.
A lengthy study carried out in 2010 by two CNRS researchers indicated that, between 1994 and 2001, the overall absence rate of work (both for health and other reasons) ranged, in France, from 10% to 11%, compared to 20% and 28% in Denmark, 15 per cent in the United Kingdom or between 16% and 18% in the Netherlands.
Fraud in sick leave is not particularly significant compared to undeclared work.
Fraçade has a rather limited scope.
Strengthening legislation, since 2002, has led to more than 45-day sick leave checks being systematic.
In 2008, out of 1.5 million inspections, CNAM found, in 13 per cent of the 285,000 carried out, that short-lived casualties were “unjustified or too long”, i.e. 37,050 cases.
In the case of casualties of more than 45 days, subjected to systematic inspections, it was considered that 11% of cases were "duly adapted or unjustified; a figure that, with a total of 1.2 million, amounts to 132,000.
Consequently, a total of 169,000 "unjustified" cases are reached among several million workers who took a sick leave.
Undoubtedly, an insignificant figure in comparison, for example, to black work.
According to UMP deputy Dominique Tian, author of a report on this issue in June, this practice would represent tax revenue losses of between 9,000 and 15,000 million euros a year, that is, more than the total cost of low subsidies and, undoubtedly, considerably more than fraud in the latter area.
Papadémos: Greece must implement a new adjustment plan
Greece will need a new adjustment plan to tighten its economy.
This is declared on Monday, 14 November, Prime Minister Lucas Papadémos before Parliament; he also added that the hundred days that have been granted to his union government would not suffice to achieve such an objective.
"To make efforts that lead to the recovery of the economy, we need the support of our European partners and a new budget adjustment program," said the head of the government, who delivered his first public speech during the inauguration of the debate on the vote of confidence in Parliament on Wednesday.
The execution of the decisions taken during the Euro Area Summit on October 27 will be the "main task" of the new government, since "the country's permanence in the euro area is at stake," he added.
Consequently, Papadémos has foreseen that the country’s public deficit will be reduced “to about 9%” of GDP before the end of the year, after reaching 10.6% in 2010 and 15.7% in 2009.
Right rejects new austerity measures
Antonis Samaras, the head of the right-wing group, on Monday showed his support for already-approved measures to try to get out of the debt crisis, but has warned that his party will not commit to additional austerity measures.
"We will not vote in favor of new measures," he said during a meeting with deputies of his "New Democracy" party.
He pointed out that he agreed with the objectives of reducing the deficit and debt, as well as combating waste, but against any policy that impedes the economic recovery.
Olli Rehn, European Commissioner for Economic and Monetary Affairs, who warned that the IMF and the EU would not unblock the loan of 8,000 million euros necessary without a written guarantee of the support of all parties to the planned measures, Antonio Samaras has replied that his word would be sufficient and that he would not sign anything because of external pressure.
A committee of experts says euthanasia and assisted suicide should be legalized
Canadians live denying death, according to the Royal Society of Canada report
Regarding palliative care, the committee calls for governments, health care institutions and physicians to work together to ensure the best palliative care, and not only in cancer cases.
Canadian society is denying death.
In fact, only 9% of Canadians are willing to talk to their doctor about the conditions in which they wish to die and make arrangements for that purpose.
It is one of the conclusions of an imposing report by the Royal Society of Canada, which involved six experts from various fields, including ethics, legal and medical.
This committee recommends that the Government amend the Criminal Code in such a way as to allow assisted suicide and euthanasia, when the patient considers this option and has been judged competent to make the decision.
To begin with, experts cite a recent Economist Intelligence Unit survey comparing the quality of death in 40 countries around the world.
Although Canada ranks tenth, the study notes that “the medicalization of death in Canada has generated a culture in which people are afraid to deal with the issue of death.”
With medication or not, 77% of Canadians do not have access to palliative care, according to experts selected by the Royal Society of Canada.
And, even though most Canadians want to die at home, 68.6% of them end up dying in the hospital.
Taken together, the highest rate in this regard is recorded by Québec, where 86% of deaths occurs in the hospital.
With regard to the sensitive issue of euthanasia and assisted suicide, the authors of the report reached conclusions that did not coincide with the position of the Federal Government, which has already stated that it does not wish to amend the Criminal Code in relation to euthanasia and assisted suicide.
However, according to experts, who have also studied the situation in all countries where such practices have been legalized or do not constitute a criminal offence, there is no evidence that the fact that they are not considered a criminal offence has not provoked an abuse of its application, i.e
Jocelyn Downie, co-author of the report, said yesterday by teleconference that, on the contrary, there were more excesses in those countries where euthanasia or assisted suicide have not been legalized.
"Euthanasia is practiced in Canada, although it is clearly illegal."
"Assisted suicide is practiced in Canada, although it's clearly illegal," Jocelyn Downie explains.
Canadians favorable to euthanasia
Remember that 85% of Canadians have already spoken in favor of euthanasia, since they believe that it would allow people whose death is nearby to alleviate their suffering, according to an Angus Reid survey carried out in 2010.
In addition, 66% believe that legalizing euthanasia will not imply that the lives of the elderly or disabled people have less value.
Regarding people who have practiced assisted suicide, 41% of respondents believe that proceedings should not be initiated against them.
In relation to the maintenance or interruption of health care to a patient, the situation is not so clear, according to the authors of the report.
In addition, its recommendations suggest that it is indicated in the Criminal Code that the withdrawal of health care, if they have been legally rejected, does not constitute a criminal offence.
The Committee of Experts also calls for health professionals to be trained in the duty to respect the fact that a patient reject end-of-lived assistance without fear of dealing with a complaint.
Regarding palliative care, the committee calls for governments, health care institutions and physicians to work together to ensure the best palliative care, and not only in cancer cases.
Finally, with regard to palliative or terminal sedation, which is already widely used in health institutions in Canada, the committee requests that, unless it serve to reduce physical pain, it is regarded as euthanasia and subjected to the same procedures.
Other experts express reservations
Other experts in the field of health law have already expressed reservations regarding the findings of the Royal Society of Canada report.
Pierre Deschamps, specialist in health law, has described as "extreme" the position of the experts who sign the report, among other things because the practice of euthanasia is not limited to patients in terminal phase.
"This leaves the door open to a situation where someone who is tired of living and is more than 18 years old can ask for help to commit suicide," Deschamps, who consulted a summary of the report yesterday.
Mr Deschamps also stresses that the authors of the report appear to give priority to personal autonomy over any other value of society.
However, he adds that "in society, there are labels and limitations."
Health law specialist Margaret Somerville also expressed her "strong opposition" with respect to the report's conclusions, which she called a "pro-euthanasia manifesto."
Margaret Somerville, who also belongs to the Royal Society of Canada, especially calls into question the lack of abuse in certain countries where euthanasia and assisted suicide are legal, such as in the Netherlands and Oregon.
The King of Jordan Urges President El Asad to Leave Power
Damascus denounces "plot"
Syria is becoming increasingly isolated
Bashar El Asad partisans demonstrated yesterday before the building of the Ministry of Foreign Affairs.
Yesterday the diplomatic pressure was intensified on the Syria of Bashar El Assad who, with the support of Russia, resists and denounces that there is a "plot" against the country.
Two days after the suspension of Syria by the Arab League, the European Union has tightened sanctions against Damascus, while Turkey and Jordan, neighbours of Syria, have spoken out in favor of President Assad’s abandonment of power.
King Abdullah II of Jordan was yesterday the first Arab leader who called on President Bashar El Assad to "leave power" in Syria, where a quarantine of civilians and members of the regime's forces have already died.
"If Bashar really cares about his country, he should resign, in addition to creating the conditions necessary for a new phase of Syrian political life," Jordan's king, Syria's neighboring country, said in an interview with the BBC.
For his part, the head of Turkish diplomacy, Ahmet Davutoglu, has lamented that the mediation efforts that Turkey has been carrying out since the beginning of the year with the Syrian regime have ended a failure.
"Those who are not at peace in the Middle East with their people and cannot satisfy them will depart," he said, alluding to Syria, a neighboring country with respect to which Turkey is taking an increasingly tough stance.
For its part, following the decisions of the Arab League and the European Union, the United States has welcomed the "strengthening consensus against Assad and the actions of the Syrian regime".
“The international community, the United States, the EU, the Arab League and other countries, such as Turkey, are adopting an increasingly harsh tone” with regard to the crackdown in Syry, says Mark Toner, a State Department spokesman.
Meanwhile, 16 civilians and at least 19 members of the regime’s forces have already been killed in southern Syria’s Deraa region, the Syrian Observatory for Human Rights (OSDH) has announced.
Two other civilians have died "during bombings and shootings with heavy machine guns in Jobar" in the besieged city of Homs.
During a press conference, Syrian Foreign Minister Walid Al Mualem said the country "is moving towards the end of the crisis."
In addition, he has reacted strongly to the decision of the Arab League to suspend a damask of his duties, which in his opinion constitutes "a dangerous step".
Syria "won't falter," he added, ensuring that "the plots plotted against Syria will fail."
Following the decision to suspend Syria, the Arab League is currently studying a "civilian protection mechanism" and wishes to send 500 members of Arab human rights organizations, the media and military observers to the country.
French Foreign Minister Alain Juppé has also expressed support for sending U.N
An extraordinary reunion of the League is scheduled to be held tomorrow in Rabat to develop the implementation of the exit plan adopted on 2 November which foresees, in particular, the withdrawal of the armed forces from cities that are in the hands of the opposition and the release of detained protesters.
Russian Foreign Minister Sergei Lavrov has yesterday judged the Arab League’s decision “incorrect”, while China urged Syria to implement the exit plan from the crisis, although it did not express its support for the application of possible sanctions against Damascus.
Italy
Monti asks markets for time and sacrifices for Italians
The future head of the Italian government, Mario Monti, has asked some time for the markets to form his team and launch a program that will include "sacrifices" aimed at recovering the credibility lost during the last months of the Berlusconi government.
The former European Commissioner debated yesterday with the political parties and today met with trade unions and employers.
It should form a relatively restricted cabinet, composed of technocrats outside Parliament.
"Monti has spoken of an extensive programme with numerous sacrifices," said Francesco Nucara, a deputy from one of the abundant small parliamentary groups who participated in the meetings, after meeting with the President of the appointed Council.
For his part, the President of the Chamber of Deputies, Gianfranco Fini, has affirmed that he expects Mario Monti to request a vote of confidence from Parliament by Friday to ensure that the new government has sufficient support.
The consultations will end with the presentation by Mr Monti of a limited list, undoubtedly of a dozen Ministers, to the Head of State, Giorgio Napolitano.
Monti has commented on the nervousness of the stock markets and the forcalists yesterday, asking them for time.
In "democracy, there are precise deadlines" to form a government and prepare its program, he stressed, adding that he is "surely that markets will have patience and understand it."
His appointment by the President initially welcomed the markets, but then the concern prevailed, in particular following an unforeseen drop in industrial production in the euro area in September.
The appointed Prime Minister should create a team mainly made up of technocrats, even if he has stressed that he would like to include "politicians".
The new government will have to operate until 2013, the date of the next legislation, he said.
According to the President of the Chamber of Deputies, Gianfranco Fini, the new government will obtain the vote of confidence of Parliament by Friday.
Monti has pledged that, when taking office, he will work taking into account the urgency and with the aim that Italy "regain its prominence" in Europe.
A spokesman for European Commissioner Olli Rehn stressed that "even with a new government, our diagnosis of the Italian economy will not change."
The EU, convinced that Rome will not achieve its objective of achieving a budgetary balance in 2013 despite austerity plans adopted in recent months, has called for the adoption of new rigorous measures.
The "bosses chiefs" Emma Marcegaglia, who will see mates, also insisted on the need to boost the economy, "since a country that does not grow can not respect deficit requirements."
Monti, famous for his competence and independence as European Commissioner (1994-2004), represents a "change of time" for Italy after 17 years of "berlusonism"; the "professor" symbolizes "the challenge of seriousness" and "another Italy", according to the editorialists.
The great question will be the longevity of his team.
President Napolitano wants to avoid an early election, since Italy has to place about 200,000 million euros in state obligations before April 2012.
A Russian scientist has collaborated with the Iranian nuclear program
A Russian scientist, Vycheslav Danilenko, has collaborated with Iran in the development of a detonator that can be used with a nuclear weapon, an U.S. NGO specializing in atomic risk said on Monday.
For the identification of the researcher, the Institute of Science and International Security (ISIS) is based on a recent report by the International Atomic Energy Agency (IAEA) and other documents issued by the UN agency.
The IAEA last week expressed its "grave concern" about a "possible military dimension" of the Iranian nuclear programme.
According to ISIS, relying extensively on IAEA documents, Danilenko, born in 1934, would have worked for three decades, since the 1960s, at a Soviet military nuclear center in Tcheliabinsk (Oural) and would have participated in the manufacture of synthetic diamonds by explosion.
In 1989 or 1991, he would have left the center to form a company focused on the production of "nanodiamants" in Kiev.
The economic problems of his company would have led him to contact, in 1995, the Embassy of Iran in Ukraine.
Later, it is believed to have cooperated with the Iranian program between 1996 and 2002, before returning to Russia.
In its latest report, the IAEA mentions that there are “clear indications that Iran is developing” a nuclear detonation system “with the help of a foreign expert, who not only knew the technique well, but also, as reported by a Member State to the Agency, he worked for most of his career in such a technology in the military nuclear program of his home country.”
Crimes and crimes against Muslims have increased by 50%
Crimes and crimes against Muslims have risen by about 50% between 2009 and 2010, while, in the same period, the remaining types of racial and religious violence have decreased or increased only slightly, statistics published by the FBI on Monday indicate.
According to these figures, the total number of acts of violence committed against Muslims has gone from 107 in 2009 to 160 in 2010, that is, it has increased by 49%, compared to an increase of 13% in the case of violence against Catholics, a 4% reduction in crimes against Jews and an overall increase of 14% in the number of crimes and anti-religious crimes.
The total number of "hate crimes" has grown slightly to 6,628 cases, of which 47.3% are due to racial differences and 20% to religious differences, according to the FBI.
"Following a decline in 2009, it is troubling that these types of crimes and crimes have increased again," human rights advocacy organization Human Rights First said, "the increase in acts of violence against Muslims is particularly significant," the organization said in a statement.
“Human Rights First has long pointed out that violence against Muslims, as well as all types of hate crimes, should be considered a serious violation of human rights.
"The U.S. government can and should strive to end such abuses," he adds, considering that such efforts could include an improvement of police reports on "hate crimes."
“Hate crime” in U.S. federal law was inherited from the era of the civil rights struggle.
This legislation was adopted after the death of Martin Luther King to punish acts of violence linked to race, skin color, religion, origin and, now, sexual inclination.
Twitter changes its platform and looks more like Facebook
The microblogging site Twitter on Monday modified the presentation of its platform to allow its users, in particular, to observe the activity of the people they follow, which makes their service look a little more like Facebook.
A new "activity" tab and another so-called "name" allow you to follow more readably and live the activity of other "Twittes", i.e. of those Internet users whose messages ("tweets") are still on the Internet or who follow you.
This allows to know if they follow messages, if they are credited to new accounts, if they qualify as a favorite a message, etc.
This new Twitter formula was tested "with a small percentage of users in August" before spreading it to the rest of the network on Monday, Twitter spokeswoman Carolyn Penner told AFP.
Twitter, which was known for its uninstinctive functioning, with slightly esoteric terms (RT to forward a message from another person, followfriday pour recommander de suivre un autre utilisateur) faisant le bonheur des initiés, s'ouvre ainsi to a public plus large avec un maniement plus comparable to celui des réseaux grand
In the network, users have debated at the stroke of "tweets" with different opinions the interest of this innovation.
"It's useless, it's invasive and it looks too similar to Facebook," writes @jemb123.
“The Activity feature is going to help me better follow some elements of my Twitter flow,” @dannykronstrom writes.
Austrian
Introduction of a "golden rule" in the Constitution
The government of the social democratic/Christian democratic grand coalition that is in power in Austria has decided on Tuesday to include in the Constitution a budgetary "golden rule" to reduce the public deficit and, thus, avoid a possible degradation of Austria's sovereign note by credit rating agencies (triple A).
It was Social Democratic Chancellor Werner Faymann himself who announced this decision after a Council of Ministers, when the Austrian public debt reaches 74.6%, a figure much higher than the 60% ceiling set by the Maastricht Treaty, but clearly lower than the debts of countries such as Italy, Greece or Spain.
"If Austria's solvency were reduced at a single level, from AAA to AA+, we would have to pay 3,000 million more euros in interest each year," said Vice-Charmacratian Vice-Chanceller for Foreign Affairs Michael Spindelegger.
Austria thus responds to a request for the Brussels Summit to the euro area member countries on 26 October: in it, the Heads of State and Government called for the inclusion, before the end of 2012 of the reduction of the public deficit and the recovery of the balance of public accounts in the Constitution or in a Law with equivalent value.
The device adopted by the Austrian government approaches the "German model", since Germany was the first European country to adopt such a "rule".
Before 2017, the structural public deficit should decrease by 0.75% of gross domestic product (GDP) each year and, as of 2017, the structural public deficit of the federal state should not exceed 0.35% of GDP each year, while regional States, as well as communes, should present balanced budgets.
The objective is that, by 2020/2021, public debt is reduced under the 60% criterion set by the Maastricht Treaty.
A strong yen
Noda threatens new exchange market intervention
Japanese Prime Minister Yoshihiko Noda warned on Tuesday that the Japanese authorities would again intervene the marking of the change if the yen continued to increase.
"We'll intervene, as we did last time, if we see that excessive volatility occurs" in the yen exchange rate, Noda explained to the Senate.
On Tuesday, the yen was ascending to levels close to historical highs that produced the intervention of the Japanese authorities on October 31.
That day, Tokyo massively sold yen for dollars to lower the value of the Japanese currency, which had just reached a new record since 1945 compared to the U.S. currency.
But the yen has gradually traced since then, boosted by the purchases of investors who consider it a "refugement value" in these uncertain times of global economic slowdown and debt crisis in Europe.
The dollar was traded again on Tuesday at less than 77 yen and the euro has fallen below the 105-yen symbolic bar, reaching levels close to those caused by the latest intervention.
Prior to the 2008-2009 financial crisis, the dollar was trading at around 120 yen and the euro at more than 160 yen.
This explosion of the Japanese currency harms exports of the archipelago, since it raises Made in Japan products and reduces the value of income obtained abroad by Japanese companies when they convert them to the national currency.
"Japan is struggling to rebuild" after the earthquake and tsunami of March 11, which devastated the Tohoku (northeast) region, said Noda, who fears that the increase in the yen from the root the weak recovery.
The world’s third economic power has regained growth in the third quarter (1.5% compared to the previous quarter), for the first time in a year, thanks to an increase in exports and the consumption of households affected by the disaster.
But the Prime Minister considers that the high level of the yen did not reflect Japan’s “economic bases.”
Finance Minister Jun Azumi has asked the Bank of Japan (BoJ), which inaugurated a two-day meeting of its Monetary Policy Council on Tuesday to take all possible measures to weaken the yen.
However, the institution's deputy governor, Hirohide Yamaguchi, warned that the BoJ had already taken "adequate measures", implying that greater monetary flexibility should not be expected on Wednesday.
During its last meeting at the end of October, the issuing institute increased by five billion yen (47,000 million euros) its purchases of Treasury Letters, bringing to the 55 trillion yen (519,000 million euros) the threshold of the funds it devotes to the acquisition of various financial securities to flood the liquidity market.
Euro area:
Types grow, bags falter
The risk of contagion from the debt crisis continues to harm European indices.
Despite the appointment of new Heads of Government in Greece and Italy, as well as better growth figures in France and Germany, the rates of the most weakened countries continue to increase.
Still concerned about the stability of the stability of the euro area, European exchanges sink a little deeper into the negative zone on Tuesday, November 15.
In Paris, at 10 a.m., the CAC 40 loses 1.4% and stands at 3,064 points.
Dax loses in Frankfurt by 0.4% and in London by 0.1%.
"Giving into account new tensions in relation to returns on loans from more affected euro area states, stock markets around the world continue to yield part of their recent gains."
“Investors, nervous, are perfectly aware of the risk of contagion,” explains Terry Pratt, an institutional broker at IG Markets.
The Tokyo Stock Exchange has closed with a 0.7% decline, renewing the concerns of market players regarding Europe’s ability to contain the debt crisis.
"Japan, like the rest of the world, is looking at Europe and exchanges will continue to take place in a reduced margin of variations, so that investors will have no evidence that the situation has stabilized," said Mitsushige Akino, fund manager at Ichiyoshi Investment Management.
Recurrence of concerns about the future of the euro area affects the single currency, which continues to decline on Tuesday.
The euro has remained at its lowest level in a month and a half.
Around 10 a.m., the European currency was worth US$1.3570, a drop of 0.4%.
According to the runners, it has lost value after Chancellor Angela Merkel’s party, the CDU, which met Monday in Congress, adopted a motion that foresees, in particular, the option of a struggling country leaving the euro area without leaving the European Union.
Italian and Spaniard rates far exceed 6%
The credit rates of Italy and Spain remain at very worrying levels.
On Tuesday morning, the performance of 10-year Italian obligations reached 6.85%, while Spanish securities at the same time offered 6.2%.
These tensions in relation to the interest rates of the southern countries, but also of France, continue to fuel the fears of the interveners.
The head of the future Italian government, Mario Monti, has asked the markets for time to improve the situation in Italy, which risks suffocating with the weight of its debt.
New Greek Prime Minister Lucas Papademos, for his part, has affirmed that the implementation of the decisions taken at the Euro Area Summit on 27 October would be the "major work" of his Government.
Spain is in the crosshairs of the markets, while legislative elections will be held there on Sunday.
The difference in rates ("spread") between 10-year obligations in Germany and Spain, has reached a new all-time high.
Awaiting euro area growth figures
From a statistical point of view, France has registered economic growth of 0.4% in the third quarter, that is, a figure more favorable than 0.3% expected, driven by domestic consumption.
The Insee, however, has revised down the figure of the second quarter, with a reduction of 0.1% of gross domestic product (GDP) instead of stagnation.
In addition, the fourth quarter is what really worries economists.
Germany, for its part, has registered growth of 0.5% in the same period.
The first forecasts for the euro area as a whole are expected at 11 a.m., after the European Commission has warned about the risk of a new recession.
Energy policy:
The facts must be worth more than dogmas
If there’s one issue that can’t be dealt with taking into account short-term considerations or simply thinking about emotions, it’s energy policy.
The availability and cost of electricity directly influence the purchasing power and competitiveness of companies.
It is appropriate that an electoral meeting is time to reflect on such issues and the place of nuclear energy.
Debate will only be useful if it studies all the economic, social and environmental consequences of the proposed options.
The French must base their opinion on objective facts, on rational data.
Now that we are 7,000 million people on the planet, demographic evolution will mean the doubling of electricity demand before 2050, unless an enormous part of the world’s population is deprived of a vital element.
At the same time, it will be necessary to alleviate the increasing scarcity of fossil resources to continue producing electricity continuously, without forgetting the urgent need to reduce greenhouse gas emissions to combat climate change, an issue that has disappeared from ecological debate.
Fukushima's accident doesn't modify any of these parameters.
For this reason, Germany remains alone in its decision to give up nuclear power.
This is not the case with either Belgium, which undermines the need to find a source capable of replacing it, or Switzerland, which only aims to build state-of-the-art plants.
On the other hand, Britain, Finland, Poland, the Czech Republic, the Netherlands, Sweden, South Africa, China, India and Brazil, to name a few examples, go ahead with their projects.
The United States, for its part, after Fukushima decided to add a unit to its park, resuming the construction of an interrupted power plant after the Three Mile Island accident.
If the German model, based on renewable energies, is sometimes presented as an example, reality goes against such a discourse.
Berlin’s choice is going to represent an increase in the price of electricity, a greater energy dependence on the increased need for imported gas, in particular from Russia, an increase in CO2 emissions due to the construction of gas and coal plants.
In a few months the harmful effects will be noticed.
It must be meditated on the case of Denmark.
Champion of Europe in the wind sector, from which he obtains 30% of his electricity, is also one of the great users of coal and gas due to the intermittency of the wind; for this reason, his CO2 emissions and the price of his electricity are, respectively, 65% and 50% higher than the European average.
On the contrary, thanks to the constancy of our energy policy, our neighbours pay an electricity bill 40% higher than that of French households.
While in a globalised economy the cost of energy is a key factor in competitiveness and a guarantee of the maintenance of industrial utility in the national territory, French companies benefit from the cheapest electricity in Europe.
For this reason, any significant reduction in nuclear energy would mean a sharp rise in the price of electricity, which would detract realism from the wonderful declarations about the reindustrialization of our country and the defense of our purchasing power.
It is a price that the vast majority of the French refuse to pay and, in fact, do not express opposition in principle to this type of energy, according to the polls.
Recall that our country’s energy policy has lost to build an industrial sector with 125,000 director jobs and 410,000 indirect jobs.
Widely export-oriented, it encompasses a galaxy of SMEs specializing in international markets that can create non-relocatable jobs.
If France decided to abandon nuclear energy, it would mean the sacrifice of much of those SMEs and the loss of 6,000 million euros annually in French exports of nuclear equipment and services.
Which electrician would commission an EPR designed to operate sixty years, when the collapse of its manufacturer is planned?
To solve the equation of nuclear energy, it must be admitted that there is no miraculous source of electricity production, just as there is also no diabolical source.
Areva’s development of low-CO2, nuclear and renewable solutions demonstrates the complementary nature of energy sources.
In the case of nuclear energy, it is essential to maintain the highest level of safety.
In France, we have a Safety Authority that exercises strict and transparent control over both existing plants and the construction of Flamanville.
Our teams, of recognized professionalism, are also obsessed with safety.
In half a century, the nuclear sector has suffered three serious accidents.
Just one, that of Three Mile Island, occurred in normal operating conditions; it did not cause consequences for either people or the environment.
In Chernobyl, the accident was due to reactor design and unforgivable human failures.
In Fukushima, some people seem to forget that the accident was due to two unprecedented natural disasters, which killed tens of thousands of people.
The nuclear sector will learn from this accident, just as it did from the previous two.
Thanks to this progress and cooperation between the security authorities, and French and German operators and builders, the EPR presents a design capable of resisting such phenomena.
As they study it by all the electrical actors who want to build a power plant, the disruption of their construction in Flamanville would be a great gift for Areva and EDF’s competitors, as it would leave them free to take the lead in the inevitable development of the nuclear sector.
It is essential and legitimate to seek the best energy options for our country, but it would be irresponsible to let the excitement, dogmas and partisan maneuvers dominate such an important debate for the economic, social and financial situation of our country.
Immigration, a burden or an opportunity for the economy?
Nicolas Sarkozy’s immigration policy has been criticised from various points of view: the saturation of police, judicial and administrative services subjected to the policy of figures, the compatibility of this policy with the self-proclaimed status of “human rights country” of France.
More recently, it is the administrative harassment of foreign students that has made headlines, with the Interior Minister (see Libération newspaper on May 23) proclaiming from the past that France "does not need foreign talents, workers or waiters".
However, it is rarely economically analysed.
The question is that, on this issue, there is considerable consensus between the left and the right.
In the case of the right, Jacques Chirac fixed the orientation declaring in 1976 that "900,000 unemployees should not be a problem for a country that has about two million immigrant workers", while on the left, the tone was fixed by Michel Rocard explaining that France "can not take charge of all the misery of the world" in 1990.
The only difference, the degree of generosity
In 2005, both the left and right shared fears of the invasion of national territory by a crowd of Polish plumbers.
On both sides, immigrants are considered to be a burden to the French economy and society.
The only difference is the degree of generosity with which they act regarding immigrants.
In his program, François Hollande is limited to including banalities, indicating that co-development will solve the "migration problem".
This idea of immigration as a burden, a problem, may be electorally profitable, but economically it comes very expensive.
Economists Ian Goldin and Geoffrey Cameron point out in a recent work that sums up the most recent knowledge about immigration (How Migration Shaped Our World and Will Define Our Future, May 2011, Princeton University Press, 352 pp.), there is a broad consensus among specialists about the positive impact of migration flows on economic growth, wages and employment in countries receiving immigrants.
The limitation of immigration weakens growth and harms employment.
Fears related to the impact of immigrants are based on the idea that they can replace national workers, particularly low-skilled workers, by pushing to reduce their wages.
But experience shows that, in reality, immigrants are rather complementary than substitutes for national workers and exercise mainly professions in sectors with lack of employment.
Low-skilled immigrants work in sectors that don’t attract domestic workers and the most skilled in dynamic sectors where training doesn’t meet the demand for staff.
A Positive Effect on Wages
In the same way that a surgeon would have difficulty working in a country that lacks anesthetists, the complementarity between nationals and immigrants causes the arrival of the latter to positively influence the wages and employment of nationals.
Taking into account the above, Giovanni Peri calculated that an increase of 1% of migratory flows represents a long-term increase in real wages of between 0.6 and 0.9%.
And, regardless of the fact that the diversity that immigrants contribute contributes to the creation of ideas and economic growth (there is a large proportion of naturalized U.S. Nobel laureates and companies such as Google, Intel, Paypal, eBay and Yahoo were founded by immigrants).
Immigrants are also net contributors to social systems as they contribute on average 1% of the total budget in European countries.
The International Labour Organization calculates, for example, that in Germany an immigrant who arrives with an age of 30 will make a net contribution (income minus expenses) of 150,000 euros to the public budgets on average throughout his life.
At the beginning of his mandate, Nicolas Sarkozy wanted to go look for the points of growth that he lacked "with his teeth".
These teeth, which deterred immigrants, had exactly the reverse effect.
Europe invented democracy, it only needs to think of a European democracy
Democracy is a precious but also fragile good.
The European continent should be able to remember this since, although it was invented in Athens more than two thousand years ago, throughout the 20th century it has suffered the tragedies of the World War, totalitarianisms, the Holocaust, the gulag, but also Franco in Spain, Salazar in Portugal, the colonels in Greece.
Some perhaps believed that, in order for democracy to triumph definitively over its enemies, it would be enough to exercise the vote and as directly as possible.
We know, from Napoleon "the Little One", that it was something thought.
Universal suffrage, although it is a necessary condition for democracy, is not enough on its own and can even become an "instrument of oppression", as the republican philosopher Etienne Vacherot formerly said.
No, democracy requires much more: a public space, a public spirit, values, an organization, a separation and a control of the powers, an education, Lights, economic and social solidarity, justice.
The crisis we are experiencing in Europe is an economic crisis, but it is about to become a political and democratic crisis.
The palinody of the true-false Greek referendum had, in this respect, a double revealing effect: it showed the need for a powerful return from the political sphere, but also the worrying sense of its impossibility of its inconsistency.
This ambivalence must be reflected to overcome it.
The truth is that the front lines of the democratic battle have now shifted and, in many cases, Republican soldiers no longer move forward in a good sense.
We will not win if we demand a succession of national referendums, organized according to needs and in a disorderly manner.
A blank signature to the absolute European monarch or a vacuum stop of the nationalist hole: what options for the citizen!
No, we do not want to take blows of effect, but a common exercise, in full, quiet and continuous, of options and democracy, this time at European level.
There is no crisis in Greece, Italy, Spain, Portugal (in fact, we would not know where to end the list), because there is only one crisis, the crisis in Europe.
The totality of Europe is the one that folds to the orders of the markets and the rating agencies, which suffers their powerlessness and their lack of solidarity.
It is the whole of Europe that suffers a humiliation in the international sphere.
We worry about the transfer of sovereignty to Europe, but it is all Europe that slowly loses sovereignty and, with it, each of its nations.
The most urgent thing is for popular sovereignty to regain the helm of the European project.
Because only together will Europeans be able to reduce the weight of their debts, free themselves from the markets and prepare their future through investments.
Exiting the crisis will require decisions at European level that cannot be taken without total democratic legitimacy, unless it may lead us to disaster.
Having struggled for a long time against this proposal by the Socialists and Jacques Delors, everyone is currently calling for European economic governance.
Let it be like that.
But to think about it without greater political and democratic integration, and without it being lived as such by the citizens, it would be new madness and lead us to a new impasse.
We must assume a new stage of European construction.
In order to build the Republic in France, it was necessary to "make" republicans.
Interested revolution would not be enough.
This lesson still applies.
We must build a more economically integrated Europe.
It can only be achieved by building a more democratic Europe at the political level, a true European popular sovereignty.
To build Europe, let’s make Europeans.
"Contactless" chip cards are widespread
The World Chip Card Show kicks off Tuesday in Villepinte, near Paris, with an optimistic atmosphere.
In fact, 2011 should end with an 11% increase in global sales of microprocessor cards (bank cards, SIM card phones, etc.), with a total of 6,000 million units, according to Eurosmart cabinet calculations.
The most powerful segment is contactless technology, which allows you to pay for common transportation or purchases by simply bringing the card closer to a reader.
This year 460 million contactless chip cards will be sold worldwide, 320 million more than the previous year!
Its share should continue to increase in 2012 (580 million units are expected) and account for about 10% of the global chip card market (6.925 million cards).
Europe, which has long lagged behind the Asia-Pacific region, is gaining traction in the contactless payment sector.
Around 26 million Visa bank cards already have this function, which is accepted at 75,000 payment terminals.
In France, its beginnings are more modest: 400,000 contactless Visa cards are being used.
But large surfaces work for four.
Carrefour has issued 2.5 million contactless Pass MasterCard cards this year.
In order to put the upper gear, however, it is necessary that the banks make them available to their customers.
"There are two large French establishments that have pledged to disseminate systematically in the future, among their customers, bank cards equipped with the contactless system," said Gérard Nébouy, CEO of Visa Europe France.
Banks test payment by mobile phone
But it only constitutes a first stage towards the means of payment of the future: the mobile phone.
Currently, there are few devices equipped with NFC (Near Field Communication) technology, indispensable for transacting.
“They’re lagging.”
"But I should finally experience a real boom next year."
"We expect between 80 and 120 million units to be sold in 2012 worldwide," explains Marc Bertin, president of Eurosmart.
But the banks take the lead.
Crédit agricole bank yesterday announced the launch of a test between December and next June in Caen.
The iPhones of around 200 customers and collaborators will be equipped with a case that allows contactless payment.
The BPCE has a similar project in Strasbourg and Marseille.
And Société générale has also said yesterday that it is preparing the commercialization of an offer that allows its customers to pay without contact through their mobile.
From the Sahara to Nigeria, the terrorist front is on the rise
Paris is concerned about links between al Qaeda in the Islamic Maghreb and the Boko Haram sect.
Ties between the al Qaeda terrorist group in the Islamic Maghreb (Aqmi) and the Nigerian sect of Boko Haram, which had long been suspected, have been confirmed.
On Sunday, Algerian Deputy Foreign Minister Abdelkader Messahel even claimed that Algiers already "has certainty."
“The way both organizations operate and intelligence reports show that they are cooperating,” he explained, without providing further details.
Paris seems to reach a similar conclusion.
The investigation into the abduction of two Frenches in Niger last January would have resumed the walkways.
Kidnapped in a Niamey restaurant by people related to Aqmi, Antoine de Léocour and Vincent Delory died the next day along with several of their kidnappers during an operation launched by French special forces.
In the rubble of the intervention, French and Nigerian investigators uncovered several telephone chips belonging to the terrorists.
Terrorist Axis
The study of the calls made indicates to several interlocutors based in Mali, Niger and Nigeria.
According to Radio France Internationale (RFI), two numbers especially captured the interest of the researchers: one leads to a Nigerian who remained for a long time in Maiduguri, a city in northern Nigeria, the birthplace of Boko Haram, and the other to a man who, supposedly, is close to Aqmi and the sect.
"It's about progress, but we certainly need to wait to have more formal evidence before we talk about operational cooperation between Aqmi and Boko Haram."
"But it's possible."
“We’ve known for years that the sect enjoys external financial and intellectual support and that it’s becoming increasingly active,” says Kunle Amuwo, a Nigerian researcher.
In recent months, these "supports" had become increasingly evident in the evolution and discourses of Boko Haram or in its operational modes, which now involve the use of kamikazes or increasingly complex explosive devices and of the same style as those used by Aqmi.
The international community, starting with Washington, has since been pressuring Abuja to take into account the problems generated by Boko Haram.
The Nigerian authorities, in fact, have long considered the group as a community of enlightened ill dignified of interest.
A quick success
Founded in 2002 in Maiduguri around a mosque, school and fundamentalist imam Mohammed Yusuf, Boko Haram (meaning, in hausa, “Western education is a sin”), it incites the establishment of an Islamic emirate in the Muslim north of Nigeria.
In this poor region, the association is experiencing rapid success.
It politicizes and demands strict respect for Sharia, while its militants throw themselves into the fight against the impious central government.
The latter are oriented in the first place to churches, bars, administrations.
In 2009, Boko Haram militants were especially active and attacks against the police stations multiplied.
Abuja's reaction was, as it usually happens, widespread.
The army attacked the city and 800 were killed.
Mohammed Yusuf, arrested in life, is killed in prison.
Since then, the sect radicalizes and abandons its northern fiefdoms.
Last Christmas, it claims an attack that causes 80 dead in Jos and, later, another in Abuya.
In June, Boko Haram again attacked Abuja, particularly against the police-protected headquarters, with a suicide attack, the first of its kind.
In August, the United Nations headquarters became its first international target.
The creation of a terrorist axe in Africa, which would range from Mauritania to Somalia through Nigeria, disturbs all security specialists.
While Aqmi has attacked Mali and Niger and that the Somalis of Al-Chebab operate in Kenya, Boko Haram emerges as a new threat in the center of the continent.
Last week, during a meeting in Nigeria with President Goodluck Jonathan, Alain Juppé warned him against Boko Haram and said he was willing to “share all the information.”
The World According to Republican Presidential Candidates
In relation to China, with Iran and with the help to Israel, Mitt Romney, Herman Cain, and Rick Perry compete in simplicity and lack of knowledge.
Crisis-weakened America can manage a complex world with simplistic or even simplistic ideas?
Listening to Republican presidential candidates, voters might think it is.
For a few days, Mitt Romney, Herman Cain, Rick Perry and the other candidates have been making more thunderous statements and shortcuts about foreign policy, traditionally a strong point for Republicans.
Mottoms, often baseless, over China, Iran, aid to Israel are spread out.
Mitt Romney, the candidate best placed in the race to designate the "Grand Old Party", has threatened to bring the Chinese before the WTO and denounce them "for manipulation of their currency", a key issue in an America exasperated by the migration of entire fragments of its sector towards Asia.
It doesn't matter if that entails the explosion of a trade war, he said.
Jon Huntsman, Obama’s former ambassador to China and the only candidate who possesses a sophisticated view of foreign policy, almost chokes at an attitude that “bulds” emotions.
He has called for a firm, but constructive, dialogue with Beijing (Obama’s current position).
But Huntsman, which stagnates in the depths of the polls, remains inaudible.
In the style of Reagan
Romney has also pledged military intervention in Iran if the sanctions fail to disrupt his nuclear program.
"If I'm elected, I'll stop Iran," he boasted.
"If Obama is re-elected, they'll have the bomb."
He also vowed to increase military aid to Israel, accusing Obama of having failed his obligations in relation to that partner.
Entrepreneur Herman Cain, the second highest-ranked candidate, shows an embarrassing ignorance of such issues.
Recently, he called for countering the Chinese military threat because Beijing “tries to develop nuclear capability,” apparently ignoring the fact that China already has the military atom since 1964.
Texan Governor Rick Perry, who supports Israel and wants to intervene in Iran, says he is willing to deploy the U.S. military in Mexico to fight drug cartels.
The use of torture against the alleged terrorists, banned by Obama in 2009, is also advocated by Cain, Perry and candidate Michele Bachmann.
The differences are considerable with 2008 Republican candidate John McCain, a heavyweight in foreign policy.
Opponents of the incumbent president answer that Reagan also had simple ideas and won the Cold War.
They remember that Obama himself was an amateur and that he had to loosen the piston in the fight against terrorism.
They rightly insist on the failure of his naïve dialogue with Iran, the hasty departure of Iraq's "boys" and their misrepresentations in Libya, and labell him as weak.
Attacks aren't easy for them.
The population rather positively judges Obama’s national security balance, which wiped out bin Laden.
From Iraq, she is wary of the military interventions that generate a sinking.
And, arguing that we need to economically rebuild America to resurrect its leadership, Obama seems to be fairer.
Nord Stream, the new gateway for Russian gas in Europe
The Nord Stream pipeline, which directly connects Western Europe with Russia passing through the bottom of the Baltic Sea, has already been put into service.
It must supply gas to meet the needs of thirty million households.
Gazprom, the Russian gas giant, has wanted to celebrate the event with great pomp.
Last Tuesday, more than 500 guests, including four Heads of State and Government, participated on the shores of the Baltic, between the music of the orchestra and a delicate buffet, at the inauguration of the Nord Stream, the new gas pipeline that communicates directly, under the sea, Russia with Germany.
Symbolically, German Chancellor Angela Merkel, Russian President Dmitri Medvedev, French Prime Minister François Fillon and his counterpart from the Netherlands, Mark Rutte, have turned together the valve steering wheel that controls the entry of Russian gas into the Western European network.
With this new pipeline, 27.5 million cubic meters of additional Russian gas will arrive each year to Western Europe through a first had 1,224 km connecting the St. Petersburg region with the Mecklenburg-Pomerania Land.
A second tube should be completed at the end of 2012 which will allow a total of 55,000 million cubic meters of gas, that is, sufficient to cover all French consumption.
In addition, there is already talk of the possibility of building a third tube along the same route.
For Gazprom, the timely completion of this huge work presents airs of victory.
Poland and the Baltic countries had strongly opposed the project.
In fact, the Nord Stream will allow to supply gas directly to Germany, avoiding landow through third countries (Poland and Ukraine).
Suddenly, these two countries lose something of their power: for them, it will be more difficult to influence Russia by threatening it with closing access to markets in Western Europe.
At the height of the debate, Warsaw did not hesitate to compare the Nord Stream as a "new German-Soviet pact", referring to the alliance between Hitler and Stalin to occupy Poland in 1939.
In other words, if the issue is politically sensitive.
Western Europe, supporting the Russian project, has gained greater security of supply.
But the price is a weakening of the Europe of energy, given that the project has generated a gap between Eastern and Western Europeans.
Russia has known, in just five years, to triumph its vision of a direct alliance between Russia and the rich Western countries, large consumers of gas and with increasingly limited reserves.
"This pipeline is a component of our partnership with Russia, a new artery that unites us organically," François Fillon confirmed during the inauguration of the pipeline, demonstrating the extent to which the large contracts in the gas sector are as political as economic.
All this explains the broad smiles on the faces of Gazprom managers, numerous in Lubmin, at the point of arrival of gas.
The construction of this gas pipeline is also a technical achievement.
The underwater tube is made of cement-coated steel to protect against ship anchors.
It rests on the bottom of the sea, at an average of 200 m deep.
To build it, it was necessary to avoid minefields dating from World War II.
"It was necessary to remove or destroy a hundred of them, as a precaution," said Jens Müller, a spokesman for Nord Stream.
It was also necessary to exercise extreme protection of fauna and flora.
The tube is a long metal snake that weighs like 242 Eiffel towers and is not interrupted in its entire length: neither by valves, nor by maintenance stations.
A platform of control was planned in the waters of Sweden, but those responsible for the project renounced it when they saw that the Swedes were not very willing to allow a Russian state society to settle permanently at sea off its coasts.
"In the event of a breakdown, we'll disrupt the passage of the gas in less than a minute," Jens Müller says.
In case of leakage, the gas from the pipeline will therefore be released into the atmosphere.
But the builders consider it unlikely that there will be a leak, since they have foreseen joints that, according to forecasts, will have a leak every 100,000 years.
The control of the state of the pipeline is ensured by means of an underwater robot, dragged by a vessel, which performs external inspections, as well as by means of a device circulating inside the pipeline.
In the shape of a cigar, it is introduced inside the tube, which has a diameter of one meter, and moves with the gas.
On arrival, after three full days of travel at a speed of three meters per second, it supplies accurate data on possible deformations of the metal.
The pipeline has been designed to operate for fifty years without the need for any repair.
The circulation of the gas is ensured simply by pressure.
In the beginning it is 200 bars and, at the exit of 100 bars.
Five companies have joined forces to carry out this project.
The leading company is Gazprom, which owns 51% of the stakes.
She was joined by Germany’s E.ON and BASF (15.5% each), as well as the Dutch Gasunie and the French group GDF Suez (9% each).
These five companies have created a joint venture, called Nord Stream, responsible for building and exploiting the pipeline.
It is headquartered in Zoug, Switzerland, where imposition is especially favourable.
Gazprom then rented all gas transport capacities for a period of twenty-five years.
“Gazprom will be paid for the total capacity, regardless of the amount of gas that is actually transported,” Jens Müller says.
The financing of the construction of the pipeline has been carried out in 30% with the contribution of shareholders and 70% through bank loans.
The total cost of the work should reach 7.400 million euros for the two tubes.
For GDF Suez, for example, the investment has reached 240 million.
But minority shareholders are confident that they won’t exceed their investment, since Gazprom alone assumes the risk of spending.
The pipeline emerges from the Baltic in Lubmin, on a sandy coastline planted with pines.
Valves, 4.5 meters high, form the gas outlet gate.
There are sensors that measure the pressure, temperature and specific characteristics of the gas on arrival, before it is transferred to German gas pipelines.
Since last Tuesday, the terminal receives a million cubic meters of Russian gas every hour.
At full capacity, there will be three million per hour and in 2012, six million.
Moleskine, a brand notebook.
Created half a century ago by an Italian company, Moleskine wants to give people the taste for writing.
It has an air of adventure, hours of writing by candlelight.
The Moleskine notebook is easily recognized, with its black rectangular pastes, its gum, its broken white leaves, as aged by time.
However, Moleskine notebooks with the current appearance were born... in 1997.
The Italian company Modo Modo, based in Milan, decided at the time to launch to the market a notebook of design notes, but distinguished.
Maria Sebregondi, at that time consultant of the company, took care of the launch of the new product.
The notebook is inspired by an ancient production technique, mole skin, which refers to varnished cotton that was used, among other things, to cover benches.
This kind of notebook was highly valued by writers and travelers.
"Moleskine has adapted to today's taste an old and forgotten object, an icon of avant-garde artists until the 20th century," says Maria Sebregondi, who since then became responsible for the brand in Mode Mode.
He achieved success.
Between 2006 and 2010, sales increased by 26%, reaching a volume of 12.7 million units sold.
Far the confidential production of the first years has remained, in which only 3,000 notebooks were designed.
Moleskine goes beyond the Italian borders and exports its products to 70 countries, from France to the United States.
It is difficult to determine the profile of the buyer, since the notebook attracts both businessmen and students and professionals.
Like Moleskine, its users are cosmopolitan, open-spirited and cultured.
The small Italian company, which had twelve workers in its beginnings, now owns about 100 and, since 2008, it has two offices: one in Milan and the other in New York.
He distributes his notebooks in bookstores, from the Fnac to the small shops in the center of the cities, and has been able to impose his identity.
“We’ve adapted to the times and the need to write, despite the rise of emails,” explains Maria Sebregondi.
This cultural firm goes hand in hand with the development of Moleskine: recently, the brand has launched the exhibition "Detour" where architects or artists exhibit their own notebook.
In August, he also launched a notebook with leopard tapas, on the occasion of the Locarno Film Festival.
Taking advantage of its success and cultural aura, Moleskine has been able to diversify its products: bags, agendas, pens, computer cases... The brand covers a wide range of supports.
An envious success: in the autumn of 2006, Mode Mode was acquired by the investment fund SG Capital Europe, currently Syntegra Capital, writing a new page in the history of Moleskine.
Why the unemployment rate for disabled workers remains so high?
About 19% of disabled people are looking for a job.
The 15th Week for the Employment of Disabled Persons Begins Today.
Despite a resolved public policy, applied since 2005, the unemployment rate among people with disabilities doubles the average rate: 19%, compared to 9% for the population as a whole.
Daunting?
"No," says Éric Blanchet, managing director of Adapt, the association that organizes the week for the employment of the disabled.
"The progress made for fifteen years is considerable.
“Back then, the unemployment rate was not twice but three times higher than the average.
"However, there's still a lot to do."
It should be mentioned that unemployed people continue to face numerous obstacles to access to employment.
They are usually older: 37% are over 50 (17% on average).
They have an unhigh level of qualification: 80% have not finished high school and, of the 2.3 million students in higher education, only 10,000 are disabled.
Finally, long-term unemployment is widespread among them: 53% for the disabled, compared to 38% on average.
That is, there are numerous issues that especially hinder the return to the world of work and that “constitute the crux of the problem,” says Christian Grapin, CEO of Tremplin, an association created in 1992 to help companies hire disabled workers.
These are objective obstacles.
In addition, there are those who are not mentioned; people’s mentality advances, but too slowly.
A study carried out in April by the IMS-Entreprendre association for the Cité pointed out that the directors surveyed have "a level of knowledge about low disabilities", with an "excessive representation of people in wheelchairs, poor physical or intellectual."
The result: private companies with more than 20 workers are very far from the goal of hiring 6% disabled workers, a goal that was fixed in a law of... 1987.
According to the latest available figures, which date back to 2008, they reached a ratio of 2.6%.
"We now reach around 3%," says Pierre Blanc, CEO of Agefiph, the body responsible for collecting the contributions of companies that do not respect this law.
The proof: the number of establishment "subjected to this contribution", as well as the amounts disbursed, have decreased between 2007 and 2011, from 59,000 to 46,000 structures (and from 606 to 480 million euros).
"The law of 2005, which increased contributions, has generated great pressure for companies," explains Pierre Blanc.
"But this improvement also reflects true awareness, particularly among large groups."
According to the Ministry of Labour, only 59% of companies that have between 20 and 49 workers have disabled employees in their workforce, compared to 82% of companies with between 100 and 199 workers.
Hazard: "A joy"
About to face the Bleus on Tuesday night (20h45), Eden Hazard and Georges Leekens do not hide the pleasure generated by this friendly match.
We won’t dare to say that there was collusion between them, but the image is symbolic.
Sitting next to each other, on the stage of the Stade de France auditorium, Georges Leekens and Eden Hazard have really made amends.
Five months after his striker's gesture of grudges against Turkey, the Belgian coach has nothing but praise for him.
"I was very happy for Eden, for his progress and because he's Belgian."
"I want to move forward," Lille's striker replies.
Regarding the contrast between his presentations in his team and in the selection, "it's a set", it has been justified.
"In Lille, I'm all year, which doesn't apply to Belgium.
"I've got to place myself."
Summoned to play against Romania on Friday night, taking into account this match against France, Hazard comes to Saint-Denis to finally "brighten".
Without forgetting "pleasure".
He has been animating the Bleus since he was seven, as well as during the victorious campaigns of 1998 and 2000.
"I've always been his fan," he says.
Undoubtedly, that will especially reinforce the "particular" character of the meeting.
Especially because he will face "Mathieu (Debuchy), Yohan (Cabaye) and Adil (Rami)", he recalls.
“We’ve just faced each other recently.
"Playing against them is always a joy."
Easily, Leekens has referred to the "pride".
"Pride" for playing "a gala match".
"Pride" also for checking, as "neighboring", that France "again is a very high-level team", about a year after Knysna's trauma.
"Laurent Blanc has done a formidable job," he said enthusiastically.
While Belgium will not attend the next European competition, the coach would be happy to be able to share a plane with the Bleus towards... Brazil, in 2014.
"Our little Belgians are growing," he said.
“We’re building something; the players are ambitious, they’re hungry.
"They will be able to see him in the next matches". Including the match against France.
Portugal shows a lot of confidence
Paulo Bento's Portugal is faced with great optimism for the return match against Bosnia (22h00), which will take place on Tuesday in Lisbon.
In the first leg, both teams lacked realism.
"When these 90 minutes go by, we'll be a happy team and, above all, a happy country."
Paulo Bento is already seen in Poland and Ukraine.
It has no doubt; the Selecçao, which has been directing since September 2010, will qualify for Euro 2012.
Indeed, only 90 minutes separate Portugal from happiness.
But there's one match to play and a rival, Bosnia, to defeat.
Considering the one-way match (0-0), on Friday in Zenica's hell, it seems clear that the thing is not decided.
Portugal dominated the game for more than an hour, yes, but could not score the valuable goal that would have given it peace of mind.
"It's a good result, but at the same time it carries a risk, since we haven't scored away from home."
But at home, in the luminous Estadio de la Luz in Lisbon, the Portuguese are convinced that they will pass, before an audience that should be equally enthusiastic, even if it is evident that the party "is very matched", dixits an optimistic but lucid Bento.
"We hope that the game will be very matched, but we are completely convinced that we can take the lead," the 42-year-old coach said.
"We continue to have a 50% chance of participating in Euro 2012," Carlos Queiroz's successor said.
Friday's draw in Bosnia forces the Portuguese to score and win.
The Bosnians could get a draw, although they also need to score.
"It's a good result, but at the same time it carries a risk, since we haven't scored away from home," Bento said.
"We are going to play against a team that has a high technical quality and that has players with a high individual quality," he added.
As in Zenica, the Portuguese will go with the intention of exerting, from the beginning, a maximum pressure on the Bosnians to "not leave them time to think".
A good idea, whenever, this time, is realistic.
